[33mcommit debf9a13d21a23d4ef9dd4e08da9fdb72595d4df[m[33m ([m[1;36mHEAD[m[33m -> [m[1;32mmaster[m[33m)[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 12:43:08 2026 -0600

    docs: scan-before-blank fails for a reason, not just by a number
    
    Retried the idea CLAUDE.md records as costing 66%, on the theory that the
    original implementation was at fault: it used a backwards `rposition`, which
    walks 1600 bytes against the prefetcher. Rewrote it forwards and vectorised.
    
    Still **-24%** -- streaming 1324/1361/1361/1361 against 1024/1057/1031/1032 --
    and `cells_blanked` came out **identical**, 108,350,200 either way.
    
    Identical is the whole finding. The scan compares against the *blank*, and the
    blank carries the current style; it must, or a row already empty goes unwritten
    under a new background colour. `payload::streaming` emits an SGR every 232
    bytes, so cells left by an earlier blank never match the blank in force now.
    The scan runs to the end of every row, saves nothing, and costs a full extra
    read of the grid.
    
    So the 66% was never anomalous. The write reduction the idea was supposed to
    buy does not exist on any payload that colours its output, which is most of
    them. A high-water mark does not share the flaw -- it records where text
    reached rather than what the cells contain -- and is now the only version of
    this worth measuring.
    
    Reverted. `blanking_writes_the_whole_row_and_that_is_the_cheaper_choice` went
    red the moment the behaviour changed, which is what it is for.

