Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 12:38:19 2026 -0600

    feat(core): put the attribution counters behind `instrument`, on by default
    
    Chasing per-run overhead, the profile pointed at `put_run` 7.4% and `blit` 5.9%
    against `put_ascii_run` at 11.4% -- more time in bookkeeping than in writing
    cells, at ~32 bytes a run. Some of that bookkeeping is **mine**: nine counters
    went into hot paths this session, `cells_bulk` per run, `bytes_utf8` per
    multi-byte character, `dispatches` per sequence.
    
    Measured, ten interleaved pairs on `payload::streaming` in cells mode: medians
    1374.1 with them against 1389.7 without, faster without in **8 of 10**. About
    **1.1%**.
    
    **My first estimate of that was 3%**, from three pairs, and it was too big --
    the same three-pairs-look-consistent error the width tables produced on
    battery. Ten pairs is what separated 1% from 3%, and the direction was right
    both times.
    
    So they are gated. `instrument` is on by default, so tests and benches get it
    without asking and the published numbers include its cost; an embedder turns it
    off with `default-features = false` and gets the 1% back. Verified both ways:
    with the feature off every attribution counter reads zero and
    `cargo build -p baud-core --no-default-features` is clean.
    
    **`cells_written`, `bytes_in` and the style-table pair are deliberately not
    gated.** They carry the write-amplification claim the architecture rests on,
    and a number that vanishes in release is a number nobody checks -- which is how
    `tmpl` came to write 223 million cells that no counter saw.
    
    1.1% is a good trade while optimising: these counters found the scalar
    `put_ascii_run`, the dead 1 MB template, the unguarded grapheme searches and
    the 13.6x dispatch density, worth about +40% between them.

