[33mcommit 80c9ea7224928ccb472617eb738ac5f31a47157f[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 12:30:21 2026 -0600

    docs: 16 lanes is right for the scan too, and aarch64 was the platform paying
    
    Asked whether more can be done for aarch64 around the scalar loop. Two answers,
    one of them already banked.
    
    **The scalar loop was aarch64's alone.** x86-64 with AVX2 has always had
    `scan_avx2` at 32 bytes a step; the scalar `run` lived in `Portable`, which is
    what aarch64 uses. So the machine this project is developed, profiled and
    measured on was the one running the slow path, and the "12% regression" comment
    that kept it there was measured on that same machine.
    
    **32 lanes is not better, re-tested for `run` specifically.** The existing
    `LANES` comment covers `final_byte` and `st`; `run` was scalar when it was
    written, so it was never in scope. Measured now: streaming 1313/1374/1338 with
    32 lanes against 1342/1410/1396 with 16, and flat on btop. Two NEON registers a
    step buys nothing when the run averages ~32 bytes and the loop exits in its
    first or second iteration either way.
    
    Both recorded next to the constant, since that is where the next person asking
    this will look.
    
    What is left in the scan is not per-byte work. With `blit` and `put_run`
    outlined the profile splits 38.7% scan, 14.0% blit, 8.0% put_run -- and blit
    and put_run are *per run*, at ~32 bytes a run. The remaining costs are the
    data-dependent exit branch, which mispredicts about once per run, and per-run
    bookkeeping. Neither is a lane-width problem.

