    carried a comment that a `core::simd` version had measured a 12% regression.
    That number was taken on a harness which reports the same ~230 MB/s for builds
    differing by 2.5x, and it kept the hottest loop in the project scalar for the
    project's life.
    
    The old claim was half right and over-generalised, which is worth keeping: the
    vector form really is flat on btop and vim, whose runs are short because they
    dispatch a CSI every 17 bytes. It is text -- `cat`, logs, build output -- that
    has long runs, and that is what `payload::streaming` models.
    
