Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 12:21:10 2026 -0600

    perf(scan): vectorise the bulk scan -- +41% spans, +25% cells on streaming
    
    `Scan::run` was scalar, with a comment saying a `core::simd` version "measured
    a 12% regression on the streaming payload" because "printable runs there
    average ~32 bytes" and "vectorising a scan is only free when the runs are
    long".
    
    **That measurement predates `ingest` and was taken on a harness that reports
    the same ~230 MB/s for builds differing by 2.5x.** It is worth nothing, and it
    locked a scalar byte loop into the hottest path in the project. The profile is
    what reopened the question: with `blit` and `put_run` outlined, this loop is
    **38.7%** of the streaming cells path, the largest single item.
    
    Re-measured with `ingest`, medians of 60 reps, three interleaved runs:
    
      | payload | scalar | simd | |
      |---|---|---|---|
      | streaming, spans | 1244 / 1230 / 1243 | **1766 / 1744 / 1730** | **+41%** |
      | streaming, cells | 973 / 1109 / 1104 | **1377 / 1379 / 1379** | **+25%** |
      | btop | 458 / 462 / 453 | 462 / 461 / 456 | flat |
      | vim | 250 / 253 / 254 | 251 / 252 / 252 | flat |
    
    No range overlaps on streaming, in either mode.
    
    **Flat on btop and vim, and that is the original claim being half right.** Their
    runs *are* short -- one CSI dispatch per 17 bytes leaves little to scan -- so
    vectorising buys nothing there. It buys a great deal on text, which is what
    `cat`, logs and build output are, and the old comment generalised a real effect
    on escape-dense capture into a rule about the scan.
    
    One unsigned compare rather than two: subtract `0x20` and test the result
    against `0x7e - 0x20`, so the range check is a single `simd_gt`. The scalar tail
    stays for the final partial block.
    
    576 tests, the property suites at PROPTEST_CASES=20000, bare-metal builds, and
    the audit goes *down* to 7 of 8 because a hand-rolled loop went away.

