[33mcommit 1361b0c7181f11826db0f3e046f59485e1ceb16c[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 12:11:27 2026 -0600

    perf(grapheme): skip the lookback searches for 98.9% of codepoints
    
    `ClusterBreak::advance` runs for every codepoint and, above its `cp < 0xa9`
    guard, did **two unconditional binary searches** -- `EXTENDED_PICTOGRAPHIC` and
    `INCB_CONSONANT` -- to establish that an ordinary character is neither an emoji
    nor an Indic consonant. The guard covers Latin-1 and nothing else, so btop paid
    both on every `U+2500..U+257F` box-drawing character and vim on every CJK one.
    `is_break` profiled at 12-13% of btop's cells path.
    
    `LOOKBACK_BLOCKS` is a generated membership bitmap over all four lookback
    tables: one bit per 32 codepoints, **391 of 34,816 set**, so 98.9% of the
    space skips every search. The fast path reproduces exactly what those searches
    would have concluded, and the `in_run` arms survive because they never depended
    on a lookup.
    
    **32 codepoints a bit, not 256, and the granularity is the point.** `U+25xx`
    holds box drawing *and* emoji -- `U+25AA`, `U+25B6`, `U+25FB..FE` -- so a
    256-wide block cannot separate them and btop would have kept searching.
    
    Measured, `ingest`, medians of 60 reps, three interleaved runs, none
    overlapping:
    
      | payload, cells | before | after | |
      |---|---|---|---|
      | cjk | 124.3 / 126.3 / 127.0 | **152.0 / 146.8 / 144.2** | **+17%** |
      | vim | 227.8 / 224.2 / 225.7 | **251.4 / 248.9 / 253.0** | **+11%** |
      | btop | 407.1 / 416.2 / 413.2 | **453.7 / 433.7 / 432.7** | **+6%** |
    
    The gain tracks multi-byte density, which is the shape a fix to this path
    should have.
    
    **This is the second attempt at `is_break` and the first one failed**, which is
    the useful part. A bitmap over `BREAK_PROPERTY` measured flat and was reverted:
    that search is reached through `GraphemeClass::of`, which already had an ASCII
    fast path, and it was never the cost. The cost was two searches one level down
    that nothing guarded. Same technique, right target.
    
    766 GraphemeBreakTest cases pass, and the generator's `--check` agrees the
    committed table matches the pinned data.

