[33mcommit a8834374367c888c756a85f794cbe7bea9ae0858[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 12:05:17 2026 -0600

    test: gate write-only Storage fields, and delete the 1 MB one
    
    Asked for a test that fails the build on this class rather than a one-off fix,
    which is right: the win was worth less than the gate.
    
    `every_storage_field_is_read_somewhere` parses `pub struct Storage` and
    requires each field to appear in a reading position somewhere in `baud-core`.
    It fails on the field this pass found, by name:
    
        Storage fields written but never read: {"tmpl"}
    
    **Nothing else could have caught it.** `Storage` is one block and its fields
    are `pub`, so the dead-code lint does not reach them; a write counts as a use;
    and the tests passed because nothing depended on the value. It compiled, ran,
    and cost 2048 cell writes per style-table miss -- 223 million of them on btop,
    about 1.8 GB -- for a buffer no code path consulted.
    
    So `tmpl` is gone: `NSTYLE * MAX_COLS` cells, **1 MB** of a 24 MB `Storage`,
    now neither written nor read. btop measures 376 MB/s against ~306 before this
    pass. 576 tests.
    
    **`OpMode::Templated` is the loose end and is the owner's call.** `tmpl` was
    its storage, and `grep` finds the variant matched nowhere in `baud-core` -- it
    is behaviourally identical to `Computed`, which is why the bench's "templated"
    row tracks its "computed" row to within noise (1153.3 against 1157.6). By this
    repo's own rule about modes -- never record one you do not act on -- it should
    either do something or go, but removing a public enum variant is a scope
    decision rather than a cleanup, so it is flagged rather than taken.

