Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 12:03:06 2026 -0600

    perf(style): stop filling a 1 MB template nothing reads -- +27% on btop
    
    Instrumenting dispatch found it. btop is 63% SGR and interned 109,274 styles
    against 690,086 hits, where vim interns 2 and streaming 8 -- truecolour churns
    a 64-entry direct-mapped table. Then `intern_style` does this on every miss:
    
        RowBlock::nth(k, Cols::from_stride(MAX_COLS))
            .of_mut(&mut self.s.tmpl)
            .fill(c);
    
    **2048 cells per miss, for a buffer nothing reads.** On btop that is 109,274 x
    2048 = ~223 million cell writes and ~1.8 GB of traffic, and `grep` finds
    exactly one mention of `self.s.tmpl` outside its declaration: that write.
    
    Measured, `ingest`, medians of 60 reps:
    
      | payload, cells | with fill | without | |
      |---|---|---|---|
      | btop | 306.4 / 325.5 / 322.8 | **408.3 / 411.7 / 428.3** | **+27%** |
      | vim | 224.7 / 234.7 / 235.1 | 226.5 / 224.2 / 231.4 | flat |
      | streaming | 1152.9 / 1136.7 | 1156.0 / 1151.6 | flat |
    
    Ranges do not overlap on btop. Flat elsewhere because neither payload misses:
    vim interns twice in a whole recording.
    
    It is also a rule #1 violation that was invisible, and for a reason worth
    recording: `intern_style` does not touch `cells_written`, so those 223 million
    writes were counted nowhere. btop's cell amplification reads **0.2x per byte**,
    which should have looked impossible for a terminal drawing a full screen.
    That is the third uncounted write path this instrumentation pass has found and
    by far the largest.
    
    No behaviour change: `style_of` reads `tmpl_key` and `tmpl_valid`, which are
    still maintained. `tmpl` itself is read by nothing, and 575 tests agree.

