[33mcommit e6db38801248ced974c0e2bb584b77a6cee42684[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 11:53:51 2026 -0600

    feat(cells): count all four line-block paths, and instrument CSI dispatch
    
    Continuing to instrument the cells path. Three more uncounted write paths, and
    one measurement that redirects where the work should go.
    
    **Three more uncounted paths.** The previous commit counted
    `scroll_region_up`'s region branch; `scroll_region_down`, `IL` and `DL` take
    the same route into `insert_lines_in`/`delete_lines_in` in `baud-domain` and
    were equally invisible. All four now share `count_line_block`, which counts the
    rows moved as well as the rows blanked -- a `copy_within` per row writes every
    cell it touches, so a move is a write.
    
    **No payload reaches any of them, which is why they lasted.** vim reports
    **zero** scrolls on its own recording and 74% of its cells as `fill`: it
    redraws by erasing and rewriting rather than by scrolling, so all of that is
    `EL`/`ED`. btop never scrolls at all. The unit test now drives a region scroll,
    `IL` and `DL` directly, and checks the counts against arithmetic rather than
    against the other counters -- the internal-consistency invariant cannot see a
    path that increments neither side.
    
    **Dispatch is the finding.** `Term::dispatches` says streaming dispatches one
    CSI per 232 bytes while btop and vim both do one per **17** -- 13.6x denser.
    The profile already puts `dispatch` at 17% of streaming's cells path, where
    `path_split` says sequences are 2% of its bytes, so a dispatched sequence
    costs roughly eight text bytes. On real capture that ratio is the whole game,
    and it fits the throughputs: streaming 1150 MB/s against btop 312 and vim 211.
    
    So the next target is the dispatch path, not blanking. Blanking is 73% of
    streaming's *cells* and near none of a TUI's.
    
    The audit caught the helper on the way in -- two `usize` parameters and an
    `as usize` -- and it takes `Row` and uses `at_least_one().min(..)` instead.
    Neither limit moved. 575 tests.

