    feat(cells): count the region-scroll path, which wrote 88,000 uncounted cells
    
    Continuing to instrument the cells path, and the instrumentation found a hole
    in itself.
    
    `scroll_region_up` has two paths. The full-screen one advances a ring and
    blanks through `fill_row_counted`, which counts. The **region** one calls
    `View::delete_lines_in` in `baud-domain`, where the counters do not reach, and
    nothing counted the rows it copies up or the rows it blanks. So every
    amplification figure for an application using DECSTBM was understated, and that
    is most TUIs.
    
    It went unnoticed because the payloads do not reach it: vim sets a region equal
    to the whole screen and takes the O(1) ring instead, and btop never scrolls at
    all -- `fill 0%`, which is exactly what `CLAUDE.md` records for it.
    
    **`cell_attribution_accounts_for_every_write` could not catch this, and the
    reason is worth keeping.** It asserts the three buckets sum to `cells_written`,
    so a path incrementing *neither* stays perfectly consistent while being
    invisible. Internal consistency is not completeness, and I only found that out
    by deleting the fix and watching the invariant still pass.
    
    So `a_region_scroll_counts_the_cells_it_moves_and_blanks` counts in the other
    direction -- a region of known height, a known number of scrolls, and the cells
    that must follow. Verified by removing the counting: it reports 0 against
    88,000.
    
    Also splits `cells_scroll` and `scrolls` out of `cells_fill` as a subset rather
    than a fourth bucket, so the attribution still sums. `cells_scroll / scrolls`
    is the width actually blanked per scroll, which is the quantity every blanking
    experiment in CLAUDE.md turned on.
    
    574 tests.

