[33mcommit 548fe84893ab03cec1099a0e4e75031e67734102[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 10:50:05 2026 -0600

    perf(cells): vectorise put_ascii_run -- +30% on the text-heavy payload
    
    Instrumenting the cells path first, as asked, and the instrumentation is what
    pointed here. `Term::cell_attribution` splits `cells_written` three ways, and
    on `payload::streaming` it is bulk 27%, single 0%, fill 73%. Against a dtrace
    profile of the same workload -- `put_ascii_run` 33%, `scroll_region_up` 17% --
    that gives a cost per cell:
    
      | path | cells | time | per cell |
      |---|---|---|---|
      | fill (scroll, blank) | 73% | 17% | 0.23 |
      | put_ascii_run (text) | 27% | 33% | 1.22 |
    
    **5.3x per cell for the same eight bytes.** `fill` is `slice::fill` and lowers
    to a vector store; `put_ascii_run` writes a different content per cell and was
    a scalar loop. `otool -tv` confirmed it: `ldr`/`str`/`orr` and **no vector
    register at all**.
    
    Only the content varies within a run -- style and flags are fixed -- so under
    `repr(C)` on little-endian a cell is `(style, flags) << 32 | content`, and
    eight are one `u64x8` OR and one 64-byte store. Nine vector instructions in the
    function now, against zero.
    
    No `unsafe`: `baud-domain` forbids it, so the reinterpretation goes through
    `zerocopy`, which `PackedCell` already derives for the shm and disk paths. Big
    endian keeps the scalar loop.
    
    Measured with `ingest`, medians of 60 internally-timed reps:
    
      | payload, cells | scalar | vector | |
      |---|---|---|---|
      | streaming | 840.9 | **1107.0** | **+30%** |
      | btop | 304.7 | 305.1 | flat |
      | vim | 222.1 | 224.0 | flat |
      | streaming, spans (control) | 1244.0 | 1226.3 | unaffected |
    
    **The scoping is the honest part.** btop and vim are escape-dominated -- 66%
    and 44% of their bytes -- with only 18% of cells from bulk text, so this is not
    their bottleneck. The win is where text dominates: `cat`, logs, build output.
    
    Absolute numbers are on battery and should be re-taken on mains. The *ratio* is
    not in doubt: three pairs, scalar max 841.1 below vector min 1089.0, and a
    codegen change from zero vector instructions to nine.
    
    Also `cell_attribution_accounts_for_every_write`, asserting the three counters
    sum to `cells_written` -- the same invariant `path_split` carries, and what
    catches a fourth write site added without a counter. 574 tests.

