[33mcommit d1dc4edca5ad5b6736d1db807d261cad95fdf20a[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 10:33:21 2026 -0600

    fix: a test lost its #[test] in an edit of mine and stopped running
    
    `blanking_writes_the_whole_row_and_that_is_the_cheaper_choice` has not run
    since `b6429f4`. Renaming it, I replaced a slice that ran from the doc comment
    to the `fn` line, and `#[test]` was inside it -- the "span-delete that took its
    neighbours with it" CLAUDE.md already records, except this one was silent
    because the compiler had no reason to complain about a function that still
    parsed.
    
    **Restored, and it failed immediately.** The assertion said `(20_000 + 50) *
    200` and the answer is `20_001 * 200`. The arithmetic is now written out
    instead of guessed: 50 rows for the startup clear, plus 19,951 scrolls, because
    the first 49 newlines fill a 50-row screen without scrolling. So the assertion
    was wrong for two commits and nothing could tell me.
    
    `every_test_named_in_the_docs_exists` did not catch it and cannot: it collects
    every `fn <name>` in the tree, so a function whose attribute was stripped still
    satisfies "the test named in the docs exists". Clippy caught it, as dead code.
    
    So the compiler's own analysis becomes the gate: `#![deny(dead_code)]` on every
    test crate root in `baud-core/tests/`. A test that stops being a test is
    unreferenced by definition, which is exactly what that lint is for, and it
    costs nothing.
    
    567 tests, up one from 566 -- the difference being the test that was not
    running. Full suite at PROPTEST_CASES=20000, audit, bare-metal and sources all
    green.

