[33mcommit d8b58b19f127e971b6d9fe04a2f9e565f5a1c266[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 10:21:19 2026 -0600

    docs: retract the width-table 1.7% -- wall clock, on battery, unconfirmed
    
    Asked whether I was measuring counters or timing while the machine is on
    battery. Timing, and that invalidates the claim I committed in `374adcf`.
    
    Cycles are frequency-invariant -- if the OS downclocks, wall time rises and
    cycles for fixed work do not -- so they are the right metric here and they do
    **not** reproduce the 1.7%. Nine paired `xctrace` runs of `a4cecba` against its
    parent favour the *generated* build being faster, 6-3, the opposite direction,
    with a within-arm spread from 12.4M to 20.1M cycles. That spread is tracing
    overhead and is far too wide to resolve 1.7% in either direction.
    
    So neither number is established: not the 1.7%, and not a refutation of it.
    Both files now say so instead of carrying a figure that reads as measured.
    
    What the interleaved design does and does not buy: pairing controls for slow
    drift, because both arms see nearly the same conditions within a pair. It does
    not control for core migration, and the `036a432` run showed a 2.22 s outlier
    against a 1.99 s norm, which is what a migration looks like. That null is less
    exposed -- ten pairs and no difference at all, so a small distortion would not
    manufacture equality -- but it was taken the same way and gets the same caveat.
    
    The lesson is cheap and general: `/usr/bin/time` measures a clock the OS is
    free to move. Prefer cycles for A/B on a laptop, and record the power state.

