    testing both:**
    
      | anomaly | in multibench | with baud alone |
      |---|---|---|
      | `036a432` overlong UTF-8 | -5% | **0%** |
      | `a4cecba` width tables | -5% | **-1.7%** |
    
    `036a432` was entirely the harness. The width tables are not: two thirds of the
    recorded number was `multibench` -- six terminal implementations in one binary,
    where 5.4 KB of new tables moves everything after them -- and a third is a real
    cost that an embedder linking baud would also pay.
    
    So the class is **inflated by the comparison binary, not invented by it**, and
    generalising from the first result would have been wrong. Both numbers now sit
    in CLAUDE.md and MEASUREMENTS.md in place of the 5%.
    
    The residual is still unexplained: the payload is pure ASCII, `Width::of` is
    never called, and 5.4 KB of extra rodata is the remaining suspect. It is a
    smaller thing to explain than it was.
    
    Unchanged: the tables stay generated. The hand-written set gave every Arabic,
    Hebrew and Devanagari combining mark a column of its own, and 1.7% of a
    synthetic ASCII benchmark is a better trade than 5% was.

