[33mcommit 343dcc09e63d69d9bd4f74bb9e48abb71f441cdf[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 08:38:28 2026 -0600

    feat(bench): a harness that ingests one payload and does nothing else
    
    `baud-bench` cannot answer "what did *this* payload cost". It forks a child,
    opens a pty, and runs every payload and every recording in one process, so a
    counter trace over it is dominated by whatever is largest.
    
    Three questions have now needed a workload with one variable in it, and each
    time the answer was that the harness could not produce one:
    
      * sizing the UTF-8 decode path, where a `dtrace` sample came back dominated
        by `put_ascii_run` because the synthetic payloads are ASCII;
      * explaining `036a432`, where an `xctrace` CPU-counter run over the whole
        bench gave 30,474 samples against 21,852 for the two arms -- a 40%
        difference in duration, so the totals are not comparable and the mix that
        did shift cannot be attributed;
      * and the same for the width-table and blanking anomalies before that.
    
    `ingest <payload> <reps>` has no fork, no pty, no `read(2)`, no timing and no
    printing inside the loop. The process *is* the measurement, so counters over
    the whole run are counters over the ingest of one payload. It reports
    `utf8_share` at the end so the payload's character is visible in the same line
    as the result: streaming 0.0%, vim 28.2%.
    
    Also adds `payload::cjk` -- pure CJK, no escapes, every byte through the
    decoder. The synthetic payloads are ASCII and report 0.0%, so neither could
    price the decode path at all, which is why estimating it from `streaming`
    produced "~5% and correspondingly small" and was wrong by a factor of five.
    
    Enabled by Xcode being installed: `xctrace list templates` now offers CPU
    Counters, so `docs/research/notes/d-memory-hierarchy.md`'s narrowing is
    confirmed -- the counters were an install away, not blocked by SIP. CLAUDE.md's
    "not available on this machine even as root" was true of dtrace's `cpc`
    provider and false in general.

