[33mcommit a5a7c6169522c1998a5fb3a857cb115b7902bf5d[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 07:57:05 2026 -0600

    perf(parse): outline the cold Ground arms, and rule out the size explanation
    
    Investigating `036a432`, the one step of the erosion still unexplained. Two
    findings, and the second undoes the first as a *cause*.
    
    **Its own recorded explanation is wrong.** The commit guessed "State gained a
    field", and `Utf8Min` did join `State::Utf8` -- but `size_of::<State>()` is
    **8 bytes before and after**. The field landed in existing padding. Probed by
    const assertion against `036a432~1`, not inferred.
    
    **Code size did change, and matches.** `feed_with` went 11,260 -> 11,788 bytes
    across that commit, +4.7%, against a ~5% loss on the spans path. `control` is
    inlined into it and the commit added a Ground arm ordinary text never reaches:
    printable ASCII is taken by the run scanner first, so that arm is live only
    when a byte is re-fed after a failed decode.
    
    So both cold arms now sit behind `#[cold] #[inline(never)]`. `feed_with` goes
    **14,968 -> 13,488** at HEAD, -9.9%, nearly three times what `036a432` added.
    
    **And it buys nothing measurable.** Three interleaved pairs on multibench:
    1307.6/1327.7/1321.8 against 1293.8/1321.6/1315.0 -- higher in all three, by
    about 0.5%, with the ranges overlapping in every pair. By this repo's rule that
    is not a result, and I am not going to report it as one.
    
    That is the useful half. If +528 bytes cost 5%, then -1,480 bytes should have
    paid back more than 5%; it paid ~0.5%. **Code size explains the correlation and
    fails as the cause**, so the mechanism is something size does not capture --
    alignment, or branch-predictor aliasing. CLAUDE.md now says so, and says not to
    try shrinking a function again, because it has been tried and measured.
    
    The change is kept on its own merits rather than on a number: those arms *are*
    cold, saying so is true, and a 9.9% smaller hot loop is not a regression. 566
    tests.

