[33mcommit 9039d298606b90c734fd6b071af3ad933ad30287[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 06:43:22 2026 -0600

    research: model the whole UTF-8 validator, and check it against CPython
    
    § 6.2 joins the nibble tables, so `scripts/gen-utf8-tables.py` now models the
    complete algorithm rather than the half a byte pair can see: "we expect two
    continuations if the previous byte matches 111|, or if the byte before that
    matches 1111|", compared against where two continuations actually occur, which
    is bit 7. Disagreement anywhere means invalid.
    
    Two things there are easy to get wrong and I got both wrong first:
    
      * **"Previous" is previous to the *pair*, not to the index.** The pair judged
        at `i` is `(data[i-1], data[i])`, so the byte before it is `data[i-2]`.
        Reading it as `data[i-1]` rejects `E0 A0 80`, which is U+0800 and valid.
      * **Bit 7 is not an error.** It records that a pair is two continuations,
        which is exactly what § 6.2 counts; masking it into the error test rejects
        every three- and four-byte character. Table 8 says so in the row's title
        and it is easy to read past.
    
    Both were caught by verification rather than by review, and the oracle is
    CPython's own decoder -- independent, unlike a reference written beside the
    thing it checks. **All 10,321,920 complete three-byte strings agree**, along
    with every complete one- and two-byte string and 122,496 four-byte boundary
    combinations. `--exhaustive` runs the three-byte sweep in 13s; the default uses
    boundary values so the script stays quick to re-run.
    
    Strings ending mid-sequence are excluded and counted, not silently passed. The
    block validator cannot judge them -- a trailing `C0` has no successor to be
    overlong against -- and in baud that is a division of labour rather than a gap:
    `State::Utf8` carries an open sequence across a read boundary and `fast_utf8`
    declines exactly that case. CPython has no such notion and calls a lone `C0` an
    invalid start byte, which is true of the string and not of the pair.
    
    Still no hot-path change. What remains is where a block validator sits relative
    to `fast_utf8`: validation is not decoding, and baud needs the codepoint, the
    width and a cell out the other side.

