[33mcommit 22e1589bc29bf303b113765bbae3ea91670214d2[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 06:39:27 2026 -0600

    research: derive the UTF-8 lookup tables, and verify all 65,536 pairs
    
    `[[source: utf8-lookup]]` § 5 builds three 16-entry tables indexed by the high
    and low nibbles of the first byte and the high nibble of the second. Bits 0-6
    encode Table 8's seven error patterns; a bit set in all three ANDed lookups
    means the pair is invalid. Bit 7 marks a continuation pair, which § 6.2 uses
    for the long forms and which is not itself an error.
    
    `scripts/gen-utf8-tables.py` derives them rather than transcribing them, for
    the reason `docs/vt-coverage.md` refuses a hand-written DEC line-drawing table:
    a guessed table is unrecoverable and looks like it worked. Here it is worse
    than cosmetic -- a wrong entry makes the validator **accept** invalid UTF-8,
    and `E0 80 AF` decoding to `/` is a bug this project has already had once.
    
    So the patterns are predicates, the tables fall out by construction, and all
    65,536 byte pairs are checked against a reference decoder written from the
    definition of UTF-8 rather than from the patterns -- agreement is then evidence
    rather than tautology.
    
    **It caught an error on its first run, of exactly the kind transcription would
    have shipped silently.** `F4 80`..`F4 8F` is U+100000..U+10FFFF and perfectly
    valid; a too-large pattern of "lo(b1) >= 4 with any continuation" rejects it.
    Table 8 row 6 reads `10|01...11`, so the continuation's high nibble is 9, A or
    B -- 8 belongs to row 5, paired with the overlong four-byte form, which is why
    the paper puts two errors on one bit.
    
    No hot-path change: this commit is a generator and a proof, and the tables are
    not wired into anything yet. What remains before code is § 6.2's rule for the
    3- and 4-byte forms, which two-byte tables cannot express, and deciding where a
    block validator sits relative to `fast_utf8` -- validation is not decoding, and
    baud needs the codepoint out the other side.

