[33mcommit 172f5905bfdce2bc8ce82b019621e4b3f77010f2[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 06:36:48 2026 -0600

    research: measure the run lengths a block decoder would work on
    
    Note A set this as the gate before any SIMD UTF-8 work, because
    `scan/portable.rs` records that vectorising the ASCII scan lost 12% when
    printable runs averaged ~32 bytes: "vectorising a scan is only free when the
    runs are long". `scripts/utf8-runs.py` measures it against the recordings.
    
    | recording | mean run | p50 | p90 | multi-byte bytes | in runs >=16 | in runs >=32 |
    |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
    | btop | 15.5 | 10 | 29 | 76,032 | 96.9% | 71.1% |
    | vim | 15.1 | 10 | 22 | 39,086 | 69.2% | 48.3% |
    | asciiquarium | 4.4 | 4 | 7 | 0 | - | - |
    
    **The mean would have given the wrong answer.** Runs average ~15 bytes, half
    the length that made the ASCII scan lose -- so a run-weighted reading says
    "too short to vectorise". But multi-byte bytes are not spread evenly across
    runs; they cluster in the long ones, and 96.9% of btop's sit in a run of at
    least sixteen. What a block decoder processes is bytes, not runs.
    
    The bar is lower here too: the ASCII scan was replacing about one
    branch-predicted compare per byte, where this replaces 7.0 instructions.
    
    Also checked rather than assumed: `core::simd`'s `swizzle_dyn` is the `pshufb`
    the algorithm needs, and lowers to NEON `tbl` on aarch64 and SSSE3 on x86-64.
    So this can be one portable implementation behind the `Scan` trait rather than
    a third entry point, and unlike `scan_avx2` it would be testable on the machine
    this project is developed on.
    
    The remaining gate is the one note A already names: the paper's derivation of
    the lookup tables is unread, and getting them wrong means accepting invalid
    UTF-8 -- the overlong form `E0 80 AF` decoding to `/` is a bug this repo has
    already had once.

