[33mcommit e1f3be06d644da5dfd099f0ac0089e109c7de5b7[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 23:06:12 2026 -0600

    research: dpfsm assumes out-of-order actions, which a terminal cannot give
    
    The gate set one commit ago is answered, and the answer closes the proposal
    this repo has been carrying as its main throughput lead.
    
    `[[source: dpfsm]]` § 2.1, in its own words: "This paper uses the Mealy
    formalism and assumes the existence of a function that is invoked with the
    current input symbol s_i and the state q_i reached after reading that symbol.
    In the context of parallelizing FSMs, we will make a simplistic assumption that
    the function can be invoked out of order."
    
    **A terminal's actions cannot be invoked out of order.** Print-then-scroll is
    not scroll-then-print, writing cell A then B is not B then A, and an SGR
    applies to what follows it and nothing before. Ordering is the semantics, not
    an implementation detail. Satisfying the assumption means buffering every
    action and replaying it in order -- which is exactly the sequential dependence
    the method exists to remove -- or proving the actions commute, which they do
    not.
    
    So the 3x single-core and 21x eight-core are real results about FSMs whose
    clients tolerate out-of-order invocation, and note B has cited them through
    every revision as though they applied here. Both its measured preconditions --
    57-90% of bytes in the state machine, convergence within tens of bytes -- were
    true and neither was the one that mattered.
    
    What survives is narrower and unmeasured: scanning carries no actions, so
    finding where sequences begin and end and which bytes are parameters could be
    enumerated over a block, leaving only the dispatches ordered. Those are the
    minority of bytes, since parameter digits are collected rather than acted on.
    That is a better-structured scanner, not a parallel state machine, and it is
    recorded as a different proposal rather than folded into the old one.
    
    Added to CLAUDE.md's "things already tried that lost", which is where a closed
    lead belongs so the next reader does not re-propose it.
    
    This reorders the work. The UTF-8 lookup algorithm is now the lever that is
    both sized -- 14.5% of btop's bytes, 28.2% of vim's -- and not blocked.

