[33mcommit ed7352793ed7ac0fca74bc079270a684a2e7aa06[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 23:04:44 2026 -0600

    research: note B assumed a pure DFA for three revisions; williams' has actions
    
    `[[source: dpfsm]]` composes transition *functions* and yields the state you
    end in. baud's machine does not merely move between states: 31 call sites in
    `machine.rs` execute, print, collect, accumulate parameters and dispatch **on
    the transition**, and those actions are the point -- they are what writes cells.
    
    Knowing the end state of a sixteen-byte block is therefore not the same as
    having processed it, and note B has proposed this method for three revisions
    without once saying so. The paper does handle output, for Huffman decoding and
    HTML tokenization, so the question is answerable rather than fatal -- but *how*
    is what has to be read before anything is designed, and it has not been.
    
    Recorded as the gate rather than guessed at. The shape of a possible answer is
    in the note and marked unverified: most bytes inside a control sequence produce
    no action, so a vectorised pass might find where sequences end and what
    parameters they carry, leaving only the dispatches ordered. That is a
    better-structured scanner rather than a parallel state machine, and it is not
    the proposal this note has been carrying.
    
    Third precondition I have skipped this session, after the cache story in note D
    and the bit-based NFA one commit ago. The pattern is reading a technique's
    benefit and not its requirements, and the fix is cheap every time: ask what the
    method assumes, then check the code.

