[33mcommit e0a502dcd3ffd1f68f354d576e5989b7c9b4d7cf[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 23:04:02 2026 -0600

    research: retract the bit-based NFA claim; baud's parser is a DFA
    
    `51daba6` said Hyperscan's bit-based NFA was a cheaper first increment than the
    enumerative method, because baud's thirteen states fit a u16 mask. True and
    useless: `Term::st` is a single `State`. `[[source: williams]]`'s parser is a
    DFA, so the live set always has exactly one member, the mask always holds one
    bit, and there is nothing for a vectorised bit operation to do.
    
    Hyperscan's technique earns its keep where alternation makes many states live
    at once -- that is the serialized per-state table lookup it avoids, and a DFA
    never has more than one state to look up. I read the benefit without checking
    the precondition, which is the same error as note D's cache story one note
    over, committed one commit later.
    
    The applicable technique is the one note B already carried: composing
    **transition functions** rather than tracking state sets. A function over
    thirteen states is a 13->13 map, sixteen bytes, one lane per state; composing
    two is `compose(f, g)[i] = g[f[i]]`, one `pshufb` or NEON `tbl`. That is what
    § 4.2's "one or two shuffle operations per input symbol" means, and thirteen
    states in a sixteen-lane register is why it fits.
    
    Hyperscan still contributes corroboration -- its decomposition into string
    matching plus finite automata is the split baud already has -- but not a
    shortcut. The ordering stands on the measurements and not on this: escape
    sequences are 44-80% of real bytes.

