[33mcommit 51daba6d18d29c338d994633ad2b002a9b03a47f[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 22:52:03 2026 -0600

    research: read Hyperscan, and find a cheaper first increment than note B proposed
    
    Note B has had `[[source: hyperscan]]` pinned and marked **unread** since it
    was written, wanted because the note's case otherwise rested on one academic
    result and an argument. Read now, because the measurements say the state
    machine is the largest lever on every real payload.
    
    Its automaton engine uses a **bit-based NFA, one bit per state**, chosen
    deliberately over transition tables: "it outperforms traditional NFA
    representations that use byte arrays to store transitions, and look up a
    transition table for each current state in a serialized manner", and it
    "leverages SIMD instructions to perform vectorized bit operations".
    
    **baud's thirteen states make that almost free.** The whole state set is a
    `u16` mask and a transition is a shift and an or rather than a gather.
    Hyperscan sets its own DFA/NFA threshold at 16,384 states; baud is three orders
    of magnitude below where that engine starts to strain.
    
    And it is a *different, cheaper* proposal than the enumerative method note B
    has been carrying. Enumeration runs one lane per possible start state to buy
    chunk-parallelism. A bit-based NFA tracks the live set in one register, needs
    no lanes, and makes each step cheaper instead. It composes with enumeration but
    stands alone -- implementable against baud's existing sequential structure with
    no change to `ingest_is_chunk_invariant`, which the enumerative version would
    have to be careful with. Note B previously proposed only the harder half and
    offered no increment; now it has one.
    
    Ranked by measured share, which is what decides the order:
    
    | work | btop | vim | asciiquarium |
    |---|---|---|---|
    | escape sequences | 66% | 44% | 80% |
    | UTF-8 decode | 14.5% | 28.2% | 0% |
    | bulk scan (already vectorised) | 19% | 27% | 10% |
    
    Also fetched and **discarded**: Lemire's SIMD transcoding paper. It covers
    UTF-8 <-> UTF-16 and baud does neither, and the manifest is for sources a claim
    rests on rather than for everything adjacent. The probes said `latin1` misses,
    which is the encoding baud actually has besides UTF-8.

