[33mcommit 6262eff872e1fdb24cba2065dadb6fa315d3783c[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 22:50:15 2026 -0600

    feat(bench): count the bytes that reach the UTF-8 decoder, and be wrong in public
    
    `Term::utf8_share` counts bytes entering a multi-byte lead or continuation.
    Distinct from `bytes_slow`, which is mostly control sequences: this is only the
    work a vectorised validator could ever speed up.
    
    | payload | reaches the decoder |
    |---|---|
    | payload::streaming | 0.0% |
    | payload::tui | 0.0% |
    | asciiquarium | 0.0% |
    | btop | **14.5%** |
    | vim (CJK/emoji) | **28.2%** |
    
    **Note A estimated this at "probably ~5% and correspondingly small" and that
    was wrong.** The estimate came from `payload::streaming`, which is pure ASCII
    and therefore 0%, and the conclusion was generalised from it. Real terminal
    output is not ASCII: btop draws with `U+250x` box characters and vim with CJK,
    three bytes each. This is "payloads that don't exercise the case" from
    CLAUDE.md's own measurement-failure list, applied to a research estimate rather
    than to a benchmark, and it nearly closed a real opportunity.
    
    So the lookup algorithm is worth building: 14-28% of real bytes go through a
    decoder `[[source: utf8-lookup]]` puts at 7 instructions per byte against
    lookup's 0.21 on ASCII and under 1.0 on anything.
    
    The counter is on the byte-at-a-time path only -- `blit` never reaches it --
    so the bulk path is untouched.

