[33mcommit 680200535f0bd42620b9ff9611aed7e19750c30f[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 22:41:11 2026 -0600

    research: profile the erosion, and price the decoder against the literature
    
    Asked to research the literature for performance, and to keep investigating.
    Both, and the investigation moved the research target twice.
    
    **The erosion is profiled and is mostly not a defect.** Nine points across
    `2d23dc8..9f6ddc3` on multibench -- the first time that range was measured on a
    harness that can resolve it. The largest step is the last, −13%, and this repo
    had already priced it: `0a12283` and `29bd2fb` fixed full-screen scrolling,
    which did not move the grid at all, and CLAUDE.md says "against the version
    that did not scroll at all this costs about 12%, and that is the honest price
    of scrolling". Add ~3-5% for width tables kept deliberately so that Arabic,
    Hebrew and Devanagari marks stop taking a column each, and the gap is paid-for
    correctness rather than rot. `2d23dc8`'s 1690 was a terminal that did not
    scroll and mis-rendered combining marks.
    
    One step is unexplained: `036a432` at ~5%. It is *not* decoder work, and the
    first draft of note A said it was. `Term::blit` hands raw arena bytes to
    `put_ascii_run` -- no `Codepoint`, no `Utf8Acc` -- so 95% of streaming never
    reaches the decoder. Same layout class as the width tables.
    
    **Note A exists now, group A having been declared and empty.**
    `[[source: utf8-lookup]]` is Keiser and Lemire, pinned and hashed. Its Table 13
    puts a finite-state UTF-8 validator -- which is what baud has -- at 7.0
    instructions per byte on every input, against the lookup algorithm's 0.21 on
    ASCII and under 1.0 on anything. That is a real algorithmic gap.
    
    It is also a **small** lever here, and the note says so rather than quoting the
    10x. `Term::path_split` already bounds it without new code: the decoder sees
    ≤5% of streaming bytes and at most ~29% of vim's, much of which is escape
    bytes rather than text. The large lever remains the one note B identified and
    nobody has built -- the state machine, at 57-90% of real capture bytes, both
    preconditions measured favourable.
    
    Two doc-gate catches on the way in, both mine: a path line-wrapped across a
    newline, and an AVX2 intrinsic name that reads as a test identifier.

