[33mcommit 1268f0272e70d4332a2244b518491c9541a8d99c[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 22:27:50 2026 -0600

    refactor(input): type the encoder, and widen the gate that said it already was
    
    Asked whether the input work was properly typed, I checked instead of
    answering, and it was not. `key.rs` carried eight raw-primitive sites:
    `final_byte: u8` three times, `param: u8`, three `-> Option<u8>`, a
    `[(u8, u8); 16]` table, and an `app: bool` -- a two-state flag of exactly the
    kind `MarginMode` and `Marker` exist in this repo to forbid.
    
    `python3 impl_audit.py baud-core/src/key.rs` reported **0**. Its param rule is
    `fn \w+\(&mut self[^)]*: usize` -- `usize` only, `&mut self` only -- and its
    bool rule is anchored to a struct field. So it could not see a single one of
    them. The gate agreed with whoever wrote the code, which is the failure this
    repo keeps recording under other names.
    
    The spec tables move to `baud-domain`, where `Width` and the charset tables
    already live and where wrapping a primitive is the job:
    
      KeypadKey::numeric / pc_application_byte / vt52_byte / cursor_shaped /
      tilde_param / pf,  FnKey::tilde_param / ss3_final,  KeyCode::cursor_final /
      tilde_param,  and `Introducer`, which replaces the bool.
    
    `key.rs` is now dispatch over typed values and audits at **0**.
    
    **Typing it caught an error before it was written.** `FinalByte` is documented
    as "validated to the 0x40..=0x7e range ECMA-48 allows", and the keypad's
    `SS3 SP` final is 0x20. The byte after `SS3` is not a CSI final byte, so the
    keypad rows return `StreamByte` and only the genuinely-in-range finals are
    `FinalByte`. Typing them all alike would have quietly widened an invariant the
    parser depends on.
    
    The gate is widened to see signatures, and finds **13 more** across baud-core
    that were never counted. They are gated separately at 13 rather than folded
    into the 8: that limit was calibrated against the narrower detector, and
    raising it to fit a wider one is the move CLAUDE.md forbids. 13 is a floor to
    drive down -- every one has a newtype waiting for it, as key.rs's eight did.
    
    566 tests, and the property suites at PROPTEST_CASES=20000. The spec-derived
    key tests are what made this refactor safe to do at all: they compare against
    ctlseqs' own tables, so a mistyped final fails rather than compiles.
    
    Also: `Param::value`, `FinalByte::byte` and `Term::emit_param`. The last exists
    because `emit_dec` takes a `u8` and a parameter is a `u16` -- `CSI 15 ~` fits
    either and `CSI 65535 ~` does not, and the parser saturates at 65535 precisely
    so a reply can carry it back.

