[33mcommit b6429f47616deeb1646aa081d7e6e5fb5673d88a[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 22:12:52 2026 -0600

    perf: remove the scan before blanking; it cost 66% of streaming
    
    `6f2499a` read each row before blanking it and wrote only up to the last cell
    differing from the blank. The write reduction was real -- streaming cell
    amplification 20.7x -> 16.6x, vim 12.3x -> 6.8x -- and it cost two thirds of
    streaming throughput. Bisected, confirmed against its parent over three
    interleaved pairs, then priced with four arms. ghostty is built from an
    unchanged checkout and stayed at 1105-1136 across all of them:
    
    | arm | row read? | write | spans, streaming |
    |---|---|---|---|
    | backwards scan, shortened write | yes | short | 520.2 / 568.8 / 567.1 |
    | forwards scan, shortened write | yes | short | 582.7 / 572.6 / 561.1 |
    | backwards scan, full-row write | yes | full | 510.8 / 516.0 / 496.5 |
    | **no scan**, full-row write | no | full | **1334.0 / 1387.9 / 1402.4** |
    
    The variable that tracks the result is whether the row is **read**. Not the
    direction, not what is written, not the borrow count -- which kills the
    explanation I reached for first, that this was the `blit` failure this file
    records, a `View` helper rebuilt in a loop. Hoisting the borrow moved nothing.
    
    `0e7cb84` applied the same pattern to `fill_counted`, the EL/ED paths. Both go.
    Its own cost was never isolated: the fix reverts both and the arms measured the
    pair, so that half is inference from a shared shape.
    
    The test that asserted the win now asserts the decision, with the arms in its
    doc comment and an instruction not to re-implement the scan without a
    multibench A/B.
    
    Because the harness is the real finding. This shipped with a commit message
    that correctly said "the ranges overlap, by this repo's rule that is not a
    result" -- right reasoning, wrong instrument. `baud-bench` reports ~230 MB/s
    for both variants and cannot resolve a 2.5x effect. It is what every increment
    was checked against for about twenty commits, while README advertised 1.47x
    ghostty and the code was at 0.49x.
    
    That also puts the older blanking anomaly back in question, and CLAUDE.md now
    says so: the "removing a read made it half as fast" result is 142-150 against
    213-229, which is baud-bench's range. Three candidate mechanisms were ruled out
    honestly and the anomaly itself was never confirmed on an instrument that could
    see it. It has been refusing a high-water mark and lazy blanking ever since.
    
    Also: VT52 input, closing the base layer.
    
    `ESC A`-`ESC D` for the cursor keys, which DECCKM is asserted *not* to move --
    it is an ANSI mode and a VT52 cannot be in one. The keypad is `ESC ? <final>`,
    whose finals are the VT220 table's rather than 2498's: keypad `2` is `ESC ? r`
    where the PC keypad sends `CSI B`, a VT52 keypad having no cursor duties to
    borrow. Keys a VT52 does not have -- function, editing, Home, End -- send
    nothing, because neither table gives a form and falling through to the ANSI
    path would emit a CSI at a parser that cannot read one.
    
    Two reader bugs the spec-derived tests caught, both of the same kind:
    `spec_bytes` had no `ESC` arm, so the expectation for `ESC ? SP` was the three
    letters E, S, C; and the 2703 table has a single "Normal/Application" column,
    so the two-column reader saw one cell and stopped at zero rows. A row count is
    what turns a reader's silence into a failure.
    
    566 tests. Every base-layer row in docs/input-coverage.md is now done or out
    with a reason.

