[33mcommit 3afaaeadcfaf4592722cbf02031d56bc8de2ed22[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 22:08:48 2026 -0600

    research: group D was empty, and its first source refutes my own explanation
    
    `MANIFEST.toml` has declared group D -- "memory hierarchy and write
    amplification" -- since the corpus was set up, with nothing in it. Every claim
    this project makes about caches and working sets, rule #2 included, has rested
    on its own benchmark and on argument.
    
    That was tolerable until a measurement produced a 66% regression nobody could
    explain and I offered three mechanisms from intuition. The first thing the
    source did was kill the one I had just written down.
    
    I had inferred: a full-line write needs no prior contents, so it avoids a fill
    where a read forces one; the scan therefore drags cold grid into cache and
    violates rule #2. `[[source: apple-cpu-guide]]` § 4.20 says the L1D "usually
    operates as a write-back write-allocate cache", so the store allocates too and
    the read adds no fill the write avoided. Wrong on this hardware, and wrong in
    the confident-sounding direction -- it cited this repo's own rule, which made a
    guess read as a derivation.
    
    § 4.6.10 supplies one that fits: a store closely followed by an overlapping
    load can cause a memory ordering violation, and "the offending load must be
    re-executed". That is the ingest loop -- `put_ascii_run` stores cells, the next
    scroll's scan loads those same cells -- and it predicts all four arms where the
    cache story predicts one. It needs the load to *exist*, not to run in any
    direction (C3 forward is equally slow), does not care what is then written (C4
    full-row is equally slow), is unaffected by borrow count (C2), and vanishes
    when the load goes (C1).
    
    Recorded as a candidate, not a cause. No counter has been read.
    
    Which is the other finding. CLAUDE.md concludes from SIP blocking dtrace's
    `cpc` provider that "hardware counters are not available on this machine even
    as root" and that two anomalies therefore stay open. The premise is true and
    the conclusion is scoped too widely -- the same shape as the three refusals
    already narrowed here. § 6.1-6.2 documents Instruments and a named event list
    as the supported route to the PMCs, and dtrace is not that route. Untested,
    because `xctrace` is absent: only the Command Line Tools are installed, not
    Xcode. So the blocker is an install, not an architecture, and the width-table
    and blanking anomalies should be re-read against the narrower claim.
    
    Pinned `external` rather than `retrieved`: every page is stamped "Registered
    Developer Use Only" and must not be committed to a public repo. Read, hashed,
    re-fetchable. Extraction ran a seven-term positive control first, after the six
    ECMA-48 probes that missed on letter-spaced text and were read as absence.

