[33mcommit 8d70a8d6d938375395e9645e48320248d41b3481[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 21:45:29 2026 -0600

    feat(input): the keypad, and a reader that was dropping a row
    
    All 24 rows of `[[source: ctlseqs]]` line 2498, both columns, chosen by
    DECKPAM/DECKPNM. The expectations are read out of the table rather than
    transcribed, which is what found both bugs below.
    
    **The application column is not uniformly `SS3`.** Keypad `2` is `CSI B`, `0`
    is `CSI 2 ~`, `1` is `SS3 F`, `7` is `SS3 H`. Four rows one way, two the
    other, and four more on the editing keypad -- a table written from the pattern
    would be wrong in eight rows, and wrong in a way each application reads as a
    different keystroke rather than as an error.
    
    **And its introducer is fixed per row, not read from DECCKM.** Routing those
    rows through `cursor_key` sent `CSI F` for a key the specification spells
    `SS3 F` flatly: DECCKM governs the cursor keys, DECKPAM governs the keypad,
    and they are different modes. Identical mistake to the F1-F4 one three commits
    ago, caught the same way -- by an expectation that came from the table instead
    of from the code. `cursor_key` now takes its introducer as an argument.
    
    **`table_at` was silently dropping `- (minus)`.** It skipped any line starting
    with `-`, which is a table rule and also a key. Every table read so far
    happened not to contain one, so it had never mattered; the keypad has one, and
    only the row-count assertion caught it. A row dropped by the reader looks
    exactly like a row the specification does not have -- the same shape as the
    six ECMA-48 probes that missed because the text was letter-spaced.
    
    `spec_bytes` also now expands `SP`, `TAB` and `CR` as tokens: the keypad's
    Space is `SS3 SP`, which is three bytes and not four.
    
    Line 2628's VT220 keypad is recorded `out`, not `todo`: it is the
    `sunKeyboard` alternative and disagrees with 2498 on 11 rows, so sending both
    would be sending two different keys. Same choice as Home/End. The test asserts
    the disagreement rather than assuming it.
    
    563 tests.

