[33mcommit cb9078e7f3b04d94b3d3506e4197b1c41f3fe5d0[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 21:18:09 2026 -0600

    feat(input): text keys, and three meta modes that were one bit under one number
    
    The text keys: printable characters, Control folding, Backspace, Tab,
    Shift-Tab, Escape and Return, with LNM deciding whether Return sends CR or
    CRLF. Encoded per the input encoding, so a terminal in ISO 8859-1 sends
    ISO 8859-1 -- `ESC % @` switches how baud decodes, and an encoder that kept
    emitting UTF-8 would break the round trip that makes the mode useful.
    
    Writing the encoder found the bug. `?1034` was mapped to `MetaSendsEscape`
    and its doc comment said "sets the high bit / sends ESC" -- the "/" being
    where the conflation was visible without opening the spec.
    `[[source: ctlseqs]]` line 995 gives `?1034` as `eightBitInput`, *add 128*,
    and line 1000 gives `?1036` as `metaSendsEscape`, *prefix ESC*. They are
    opposite branches of the same truth table, baud implemented the second under
    the first's number, and `?1039` did not exist at all. So an application
    asking DECRQM which branch was in force got the same answer for both.
    
    Nothing could detect it while no encoder read the bit: it was the `parsed`
    failure, one step short of a wrong answer. Writing a reader is what turned it
    into one.
    
    All three now exist. `?1036` and `?1039` are separate bits because four of
    the sixteen rows at line 2337 disagree between them, so one bit cannot answer
    both. `?1034` is implemented rather than refused because terminfo reaches it
    -- `smm`/`rmm` are `CSI ? 1034 h`/`l` in every `xterm*` entry, and
    tests/conformance had been counting them as recognised-and-dropped. It
    applies under ISO 8859-1 only, where a byte above 127 is a character; under
    UTF-8 it is a continuation byte, which is why the ESC prefix is the default.
    
    The defaults are baud's choice and are recorded as such. ctlseqs documents
    all three and states no default for any, so following it alone leaves the
    question open; `[[source: foot-meta-defaults]]` answers it, and baud follows.
    Same shape as DECAWM.
    
    Seventeen mode bits do not fit a u16, so `Modes` is a u32. The assertion that
    was supposed to catch that named `Column132` while `SynchronizedOutput` had
    been declared after it -- an instrumentation that had stopped measuring the
    thing it guarded, benign at fourteen bits and not at sixteen.
    
    561 tests. Coverage table at 149 rows.

