[33mcommit 36efa94995593c4fcd07cd9836154e6600628eaf[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 20:56:02 2026 -0600

    The key encoder, base layer: cursor keys and Home/End
    
    `Term::key(KeyEvent) -> bool` writes into the reply buffer, because that
    is where terminal-to-application bytes already go: `mouse_report` -- also
    an input event -- uses it, and a key and a mouse report travel the same
    pty in the same order. That channel was settled by precedent rather than
    invented.
    
    **Base layer only, deliberately.** An application that has not
    negotiated an enhancement must receive what any other terminal would
    send, which is the reasoning that makes `DA` under-claim as a VT102.
    `fixterms`' `CSI u` and kitty's progressive enhancement are negotiated
    and neither is implemented.
    
    Three rules, all read from `[[source: ctlseqs]]`:
    
    - DECCKM picks the introducer: `CSI A` normal, `SS3 A` application
      (line 2473). `C1Transmit::ss3` is new -- the type had `csi`, `dcs` and
      `st` but no SS3, because nothing had needed to *send* one.
    - A modifier appends `1 ; mod` before the final byte (line 2565), `1`
      being the first parameter's default.
    - **A modified key uses `CSI` even in application mode.** `SS3` takes no
      parameters, so there is nowhere to put the modifier. The specification
      states it for F1-F4 at line 2564 and the same holds for the cursor
      keys; an encoder that kept `SS3` would emit what nothing parses.
    
    The tests read the `Key | Normal | Application` tables out of the pinned
    source and compare, rather than restating them -- a table transcribed
    into a test agrees with a table transcribed into the code from the same
    memory, and neither has been read. The row count is asserted first,
    because a table that failed to parse would pass every comparison by
    having nothing to compare.
    
    Releases and repeats return false and write nothing: only kitty's layer
    reports them, and only once asked. Unimplemented keys do the same rather
    than sending something plausible.
    
    549 tests. `docs/input-coverage.md`: two rows to done, ten to go.

