    Resolve the encoding open decision: the survey says none
    
    `docs/vt-coverage.md` carried "the literature and terminal survey for it
    will land in docs/research/notes/". It landed -- `g-text-encoding.md`,
    which the same file cites 74 lines further down. Second stale
    cross-reference found today by reading the document against itself.
    
    Its § 3 table surveys seven terminals and the `ESC % G` / `ESC % @`
    column answers the question directly:
    
      xterm             supported, gated on wide_chars
      kitty             --
      ghostty           --
      alacritty         --
      wezterm           --
      foot              --
      Windows Terminal  --
    
    baud honours both selectors, so it matches xterm and exceeds every other
    terminal surveyed. **No further encoding is indicated.** kitty states
    the reason in the same table: "there is no actual advantage … now that
    UTF-8 encoding is universal". A program emitting Shift-JIS converts to
    UTF-8 itself, because every terminal it might run in requires that.
    
    `Encoding` stays the extension point and `is_byte_per_column` stays
    written around "fixed-width single-byte encoding" rather than ASCII, so
    the shape is ready if a user ever asks: an embedder-supplied 256-entry
    table, covering every ISO 8859 part, the Windows code pages and KOI8
    with one mechanism. That is an embedder API, not a protocol feature --
    there is no escape sequence to select it.
    
    545 tests.

