[33mcommit 7dfec452dcf96c66c0c3545d66717cece6c899ba[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 20:33:05 2026 -0600

    Correct the PTY profile: sample counts waiting, dtrace counts working
    
    `sample` put `read` at 82% of a PTY run and baud at 18%, and I drew
    "the syscall costs four times what the parser does" from it. That was
    wrong. `sample` counts a thread blocked *in* a syscall as time spent
    there.
    
    `sudo dtrace -n 'profile-4999 /pid == N && arg1/ ...'` samples only
    on-CPU userland:
    
      symbol              sample (wall)   dtrace (on-CPU)
      read                        82%              3.7%
      feed_with                                     38%
      scroll_region_up                              31%
      put_ascii_run                                 26%
      baud, total                 18%               93%
    
    Both are true about different questions. Wall time is dominated by
    waiting for the child to produce bytes; CPU time is 93% baud's own code.
    
    The conclusion survives and its reason changes: a PTY row still cannot
    show a parser change, but because the harness is **producer-bound** --
    the child cannot write fast enough, so a faster parser waits longer --
    not because the syscall is expensive. That is the null-consumer ceiling
    of 198.6 MB/s seen from the inside.
    
    **And `scroll_region_up` is 31% of on-CPU time** for an operation
    documented as O(1). The ring advance is one addition; what costs is the
    row scan `fill_row_counted` does, which is `#[inline(always)]` and lands
    inside it. The blanking work moved rather than vanished, and the profile
    is where that shows rather than the amplification counters.
    
    Also records what `sudo` did *not* buy: SIP blocks the `cpc`, `fbt` and
    `pid` providers even as root, so hardware counters need a recovery-mode
    reboot. The width-table 5% and the blanking 38% stay open.
    
    545 tests.

