[33mcommit 7e1bc02cbb71a3ebbc8b645d0582803287ddcd73[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 20:20:10 2026 -0600

    Research the state-machine literature, and state plainly that nothing is built
    
    Answering "did we vectorise it at all" first, because the note read like
    a design: **no**. baud vectorises *scanning*. The `Scan` trait is `run`,
    `final_byte` and `st`, and every one answers "where is the next
    interesting byte"; `feed_with` uses them to skip ahead and falls back to
    `control(byte)` one byte at a time for the rest. That is what ghostty
    and simdjson do, and it is the 10-27% of real bytes. The enumerative
    method is a proposal with two measured preconditions and no line of
    code.
    
    Adds `[[source: hyperscan]]` -- NSDI '19, a vectorised automaton engine
    that shipped, by one of simdjson's authors. Wanted because note B
    otherwise rests on one academic result and an argument, and because its
    decomposition is the split baud already has: string matching in SIMD,
    automata for the remainder. Marked **unread**; no claim rests on it yet.
    
    `[[source: parabix]]` is recorded as **gated** rather than omitted. It
    is the third leg -- transposing input to bit-planes so the automaton
    runs as boolean ops over bitstreams -- and its absence should be visible
    instead of looking unconsidered. IEEE Xplore is subscription and both
    the SFU and archive.org copies return HTML.
    
    Two arXiv identifiers were tried for it and **both were the wrong
    paper**: 1407.2062 is Nick Salter on Atiyah and Kodaira, 1412.3232 is
    Pauli blockade in carbon nanotubes. Both would have been pinned as a
    SIMD source if the extracted text had not been probed first -- the
    control fired on `bitwise` and `parabix` and found neither. Guessing an
    identifier is the same failure as a guessed table; the manifest note
    records it so the next person searches instead.
    
    545 tests, 46 sources, 28 citations.

