Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 20:14:57 2026 -0600

    Slice operations in the cell loops, with no measured effect
    
    `put_ascii_run` indexed `self.cells[base + start + z]` and `src[z]` per
    cell -- two bounds checks the compiler has to prove away -- and `fill`
    walked `for x in a..b` writing one cell at a time. Both are now slice
    operations: `iter_mut().zip()` for the run, `slice::fill` for the span.
    
    **No measured effect.** btop in memory 277-285 before against 289-290
    after, vim 190-195 against 201, ranges overlapping. By this repo's rule
    that is not a result, and it is not claimed as one.
    
    Kept anyway, and the reason is not a speedup: one bounds check instead
    of one per cell, and a `fill` that can lower to a vector store rather
    than a scalar loop. If it ever does matter, it matters in the right
    direction.
    
    Why it could not have been large: `put_ascii_run` only handles the bulk
    path, which is 19% of btop's bytes and 10% of asciiquarium's. Removing
    its entire cost caps out near there, and the profile puts it at 194
    samples against `feed_with`'s 267.
    
    The three loops in `put_ascii_run` stay three loops. The duplication is
    deliberate and measured -- threading `flags` as a runtime value cost 5%
    -- and this changes how each loop indexes, not how many there are.

