[33mcommit 0e7cb84a1ebecba4bc58aa77911f6eb262a1543a[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 20:01:17 2026 -0600

    Scan the erase paths too, and profile where PTY time goes
    
    Extends the bounded blank from `fill_row` to `fill`, so `EL`, `ED` and
    the selective-erase paths stop writing to the margin whatever the row
    holds -- an erase-to-end-of-line on a short line is the same waste
    scrolling was. vim's cell amplification 6.8x -> 6.5x. Compared against
    the blank rather than `PackedCell::EMPTY` for the same reason as before:
    the blank carries the current style.
    
    **And profiled rather than reasoned about.** macOS `sample` needs no
    dependency and no code change, and answers "where is the time" where a
    hand-placed timer only answers "how long was the thing I already
    suspected". Self time over 20 s:
    
      __wait4                12422   harness reaping forked children
      read                    3296   the PTY syscall
      feed_with                267   baud
      scroll_region_up         248   baud
      put_ascii_run            194   baud
      dispatch, anywhere_c0     20   baud
    
    `__wait4` runs after `t0.elapsed()`, so it is outside the timed region
    and does not enter the reported MB/s -- but it is three quarters of the
    process's life, which is worth knowing before reading any profile of
    this benchmark.
    
    Inside the timed loop: **`read` 82%, all of baud 18%**. The syscall
    costs about four and a half times everything the parser and grid do
    together, which is the null-consumer ceiling seen from the other side --
    198.6 MB/s with no parser at all against 158 with one.
    
    So **a PTY row cannot show a parser change**: a 20% improvement in
    baud's own work moves a PTY number by under 4%, inside the noise those
    rows already carry. Parser changes belong in the memory column; the PTY
    column is a claim about what a terminal delivers.
    
    That is also why the blanking variants were so hard to separate. They
    were being measured through a transport costing four times what they
    touch, which is a better explanation of that mess than any property of
    the variants.
    
    545 tests.

