[33mcommit ac0ed9860c27fec8646db000701babac9d8a9a03[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 18:09:42 2026 -0600

    Measure the headroom in scroll blanking before optimising it
    
    Blanking is 73% of cell traffic on the streaming payload, because
    scrolling blanks the incoming row across the full width regardless of
    how much was used. Whether that is waste depends entirely on the
    workload, and the three recordings disagree completely:
    
      recording      mean used width   blanking   narrowing would buy
      asciiquarium      200 of 200        31%     nothing, rows are full
      btop              144 of 200         0%     nothing, it never scrolls
      vim                70 of 200        74%     about half its cell traffic
    
    So this is a win for text-oriented output -- `cat`, logs, an editor --
    and zero for a dense full-screen TUI. A single figure for "the cost of
    blanking" would have hidden that, and `payload::streaming` at 60 columns
    of 200 resembles only the vim case, which is how it looked like a
    general result.
    
    Not implemented. Three ways to take it and they differ in what they
    risk: a per-row high-water mark is correct only if all sixteen write
    sites maintain it; scanning before blanking keeps no state but reads a
    row to avoid writing it; blanking lazily is the `RowRot` move applied
    again, paid once a frame rather than once a scroll. Choosing between
    them is a measurement against this headroom, and the headroom is what
    this commit adds.
    
    `how_much_of_a_row_is_actually_used` pins the three figures so whichever
    is chosen can be measured against them, and so a recording that changes
    is caught rather than quietly moving the target.
    
    545 tests.

