[33mcommit b0f1795717289401159810b39b801af871b0e31b[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 17:58:04 2026 -0600

    Measure state-machine convergence: note B's second precondition holds
    
    `notes/b-data-parallel-fsm.md` proposes vectorising the state machine by
    enumerating every possible start state at once. It pays only if the
    enumeration collapses -- once all starts agree, one lane is enough
    again. `[[source: dpfsm]]` § 5.2 reports convergence is common on their
    inputs; whether it holds on a VT stream was the note's second open
    question.
    
    Eight parsers are driven into deliberately distinct states -- ground,
    escape, CSI entry, CSI params, CSI intermediate, OSC, DCS passthrough,
    APC -- then fed the same bytes from 200 sample points across each
    recording.
    
      recording        converged        mean    worst
      btop             100% of 200    23.6 B      153
      asciiquarium     100%            4.4 B       12
      vim              100%           21.6 B      141
    
    Every sample converged, in tens of bytes. The enumeration has to be
    carried only until the lanes collapse, so this is cheap.
    
    The ordering is the opposite of the intuition the note started with.
    asciiquarium converges *fastest* and is the most escape-dense, one
    escape every 5.4 bytes. Dense control sequences force every start
    through the same few states; it is long printable stretches that keep
    them apart, because Ground and the string states disagree for as long as
    the text runs. The note had assumed VT streams are mostly text and that
    this would make every start behave alike -- both halves were wrong, and
    in opposite directions.
    
    **Both preconditions are now measured and both are favourable**: the
    state machine takes 57-90% of real bytes, and enumerations collapse
    within tens of bytes. What is left is not a question about VT streams
    but about the instruction sequence -- whether `shuffle` is still the
    right primitive on targets that now have `tbl` and AVX-512, which the
    note still marks unverified.
    
    `Term::same_parser_state` is the only new public surface, and it is
    equality rather than an identifier on purpose: convergence is a question
    about sameness, so it answers exactly that and leaks neither the state
    space nor its numbering. `State` stays `pub(crate)`.
    
    Its control is `the_start_states_differ_to_begin_with`. If the eight
    prefixes all landed in one state, every distance below would read "1
    byte" and mean nothing -- a probe that cannot fire looks exactly like a
    fact.
    
    544 tests.

