[33mcommit 886ce9e1977dd0980f4d37a0531a08bf8c464d8c[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 17:45:53 2026 -0600

    Attribute cost to the three parse paths, and answer note B's open question
    
    `feed_with` has three exits -- a bulk printable run, a whole escape
    sequence recognised in one go, and everything else one byte at a time.
    Nothing measured which got the input, so "the parser is slow" and "one
    path is slow and gets most of the bytes" were indistinguishable.
    
      payload              bulk scan   sequences   byte-at-a-time
      payload::streaming        95%          2%          3%
      btop                      19%         66%         15%
      asciiquarium              10%         80%         10%
      vim                       27%         44%         29%
    
    **The synthetic payload sends 95% of its bytes through the SIMD
    scanner; real programs send 10-27%.** The scanner with the AVX2 path and
    the `_mm256` intrinsics CI now guards is optimising the path real
    capture barely uses, and the state machine handles 57-90%.
    
    The fast path is not *missing* either: btop takes it 31,606 times
    against 31,683 escapes, 99.8%. The cost is inside it.
    
    **This is the number `notes/b-data-parallel-fsm.md` said had to come
    first.** That note argued for vectorising the state machine -- Mytkowicz
    et al.'s enumerative method, `[[source: dpfsm]]` -- and listed as its
    first open question that the state machine's share of the cost "has not
    been measured ... it is the number that decides whether this is worth
    doing at all". It is 57-90% on real input and 5% on the synthetic
    payload, so the measurement had been impossible to take usefully until
    there were recordings to take it against.
    
    The same note asserted "VT streams are mostly printable text". They are
    not: btop is one escape every 16.5 bytes, asciiquarium one every 5.4.
    That assumption came from the payload and it is the premise the
    convergence argument rested on, so it is corrected rather than deleted.
    
    Also replaces the growing positional tuple with a named `Cost`: six
    `f64`s in a row, two of which are a ratio and its own subset, is the
    shape the hit-rate counters were in when they could be silently
    transposed.
    
    Cost of the counters: interleaved A/B, 3x9. spans and btop-in-memory
    both overlap on every range. No result -- they are free.
    
    Also types the bench's `total` as `Bytes`, which took the audit from 16
    to 12: `total` and `reps` were adjacent `usize` parameters in every
    signature, and swapping them would run 48 MB nine bytes at a time.
    
    542 tests.

