[33mcommit c4f25fb72a98adf9a0c6178107db182c74070d92[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 17:14:33 2026 -0600

    Restore write-amplification measurement, and find what it was hiding
    
    Rule #1 -- bytes written per byte ingested <= 1 -- is the first thing
    `README.md` says every result here is predicted by, and it had no
    measurement: the old counters printed `inf x amplification` from
    nothing and were removed rather than repaired.
    
    Restoring it needed the quantity split, because one number was
    conflating two:
    
      payload              mode       span     cell    blanking
      payload::streaming   Runs       0.283x   20.7x    20.7x
      payload::streaming   Computed   0.000x   28.3x    20.7x
      btop                 Runs       0.000x    1.9x     0.0x
      asciiquarium         Runs       0.000x    4.5x     1.4x
      vim                  Runs       0.000x   12.3x     9.1x
    
    **Only span is rule #1.** A `Run` costs `size_of::<Run>()` however many
    bytes it covers, so it amortises to 0.283x. A cell mode cannot compete
    and is not trying to: one ASCII byte becomes an eight-byte `PackedCell`,
    so 8x is a floor and reading the rule as covering it makes the rule
    unsatisfiable by construction. The first version of this commit reported
    the sum and printed 20.95x against a rule of 1, which is the "arithmetic
    that can't be right" this repo already warns about.
    
    **Two findings.**
    
    Blanking is 73% of all cell traffic on the streaming payload. Scrolling
    blanks the incoming row across the full width regardless of how much was
    used -- 200 cells to store 60 characters. That is the cost worth
    attacking, not the storing.
    
    And the span path is **never used by a full-screen application**. All
    three recordings enter the alternate screen and never leave, and
    `put_run` materialises every write there. Spans measure `cat`, logs and
    shell output; btop, vim and asciiquarium never touch them. The 1.47x is
    a real number about a real workload and it is not a TUI number. It also
    answers the `Run`-width question that prompted this: `Run` costs nothing
    on real capture because no runs are stored.
    
    Counted in bulk through five `*_counted` helpers rather than at each of
    the sixteen write sites, so a wrong span is wrong everywhere and
    therefore visible. Writing them by regex first replaced the helper
    bodies with calls to themselves -- infinite recursion that compiled --
    which is why the helpers are checked by name now.
    
    Cost: interleaved A/B, medians of 3x9. spans 205.3 -> 225.6, cells
    250.5 -> 245.5, every range overlapping. No result: the counters are
    free.
    
    541 tests.

