[33mcommit 96a45c2d290f347c43f5828f8cff2f97e22662e0[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 16:49:01 2026 -0600

    Return the bench counters as Stat, not as bare u64
    
    The throughput itself is right as `f64` -- it is a rate, `dt` is
    already `f64` from `as_secs_f64`, and an integer would round 158.2 to
    158 and take the ranges with it. A 4% delta is a finding here, so the
    decimals are the resolution the A/B discipline runs on.
    
    The problem was next to it. `run_once` returned `(f64, u64, u64, u64)`
    and the caller destructured `(mbs, p, h, b)`, where `h` and `b` are
    `tmpl_hits` and `tmpl_builds`: adjacent, the same width, and feeding a
    *ratio*. Transposed, they report the inverse hit rate, which looks
    entirely plausible -- and that rate is the diagnostic that caught
    `StyleId::bucket` masking the wrong bits, 112,144 interns at 51.6%
    against 0 at 100%. A plausible wrong value there is expensive.
    
    Now it returns `Stat`, and the rate goes through `Stat::ratio_to`, which
    this repo already wrote for hit rates and nothing was calling: it
    saturates at zero rather than dividing by it, and naming both operands
    is what makes a swap visible instead of merely possible.
    
    Output is byte-identical and the audit is unchanged at 14/14 -- `Stat`
    is a newtype, so typing these costs no budget.

