[33mcommit f407eccd04370b9dfaf2faf313565f9f4b8b1f47[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 16:38:51 2026 -0600

    Separate the transport from the parser on real capture
    
    Adds `Tr::Memory`: the payload is copied into the slot instead of being
    read into it, so there is no fork, no `read(2)` and no line discipline,
    and the slot is still `READ_CHUNK` -- the parser sees the chunking a PTY
    would give it and nothing else is left. Until now every row in this
    benchmark went through a PTY and the two could not be told apart.
    
      recording      memory   pipe    PTY    PTY costs
      btop            304.0   247.7   157.2    48%
      vim             220.5   174.1   158.0    28%
      asciiquarium    173.2   138.9   130.6    25%
    
    **The tty tax is a fraction of what is left, not a constant.** btop
    loses nearly half its throughput to the transport; asciiquarium loses a
    quarter. That is the ordering escape density predicts: asciiquarium is
    parse-bound at 5.4 bytes per escape, so the syscall is a smaller share
    of its total, while btop at 16.5 spends more of its time waiting on the
    tty than working.
    
    The consequence is worth stating because it is easy to get backwards:
    **the memory column is what a parser change should be measured against,
    and the PTY column is what a terminal should quote.** Measuring a change
    in memory and comparing it to a PTY figure would flatter it by up to a
    factor of two.
    
    The pipe column is a 64 KiB pipe, not the 1 MiB one `README.md` quotes:
    `F_SETPIPE_SZ` is Linux-only and this is Darwin. It is a third
    transport, not a faster pipe.
    
    The memory branch is inline in `run_once_t` rather than its own
    function, because a second `total: usize` parameter and a second
    `as f64` are two more untyped sites and the audit budget is a floor, not
    slack. It went 14 -> 16 and back to 14 by removing them rather than by
    raising the limit.
    
    537 tests.

