[33mcommit 9775b79f451c4aee5b6b7ff15a0bdf1df87a0fb0[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 16:26:06 2026 -0600

    Replay the recordings through the PTY, and answer an open question
    
    `CLAUDE.md` asked whether the margin survives real escape streams
    instead of `payload::tui`. It does not survive intact: the synthetic
    payload is optimistic by about a third.
    
      payload         1 ESC per   median of 9, three runs
      asciiquarium      5.4 B     129.0   131.7   131.2
      btop             16.5 B     158.8   159.4   158.0
      vim              17.2 B     158.5   161.5   155.3
      payload::tui        --      235.4   239.8   228.9
    
    Same conditions as the existing spans rows -- 48 MB a repetition,
    OpMode::Runs, PTY transport -- so they are comparable to them, which is
    the only reason the comparison is worth stating. Ranges do not overlap:
    btop tops out at 160.0 against the synthetic floor of 211.1.
    
    **The mechanism is the part worth keeping: throughput tracks escape
    density, not content.** btop and vim differ completely in what they draw
    -- truecolour box art against scroll regions over CJK and emoji -- and
    have almost the same density and almost the same throughput.
    asciiquarium is three times denser and 17% slower. An escape leaves the
    bulk ASCII path for the state machine, so the ratio of the two is what
    any of these numbers measures. Matching *escape density* is therefore
    what designing a payload means, and `payload::tui`'s matches nothing
    real.
    
    Both caveats point the same way, so these are an upper bound: a
    recording is fixed, so a 48 MB repetition replays a 138-524 KB capture
    90-350 times and the caches never see it cold; and each is ten seconds
    of one program on one machine.
    
    The child writes a recording straight from the embedded slice rather
    than regenerating, because "fresh per write" has nothing to freshen in a
    fixed capture. The banner says the rows are replayed rather than leaving
    the reader to assume otherwise.
    
    537 tests.

