[33mcommit 790272ae5a9c53388d3a50cb7acd2990a6aca81b[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 16:07:48 2026 -0600

    Check the reply channel on real capture, and record DA2
    
    The replay tests read the grid and threw away everything baud *said*.
    A recording drives the reply channel too, and the tests written to
    validate real capture were dropping its replies on the floor.
    
    vim's capture carries three queries. Two are `CSI 6 n`, answered with
    CPR. The third is `CSI > c` -- **secondary** DA, not primary -- and the
    right answer is silence, which is the half worth testing.
    
    That silence had no row in the coverage table. `CSI c` was there as
    done; DA2 was not mentioned at all, in either direction, so a reader
    could not tell whether it had been considered. `spec_inventory` did not
    catch it because `CSI > c` *is* counted -- it reaches
    `Csi::Unsupported { marker: Gt }` and increments `unsupported` -- and
    counting is what that check requires.
    
    It is out, and the reason is the one primary DA already makes one step
    further: `[[source: ctlseqs]]` line 817 shapes the reply as
    `CSI > Pp ; Pv ; Pc c`, where `Pp` is a terminal type and `Pv` a
    firmware version. baud has neither honestly. An application parses
    whatever arrives as the report it asked for, so a wrong identity is
    worse than none -- which is why primary DA under-claims as a VT102.
    
    `real_capture_drives_the_reply_channel` pins the silence *and* the
    count, because refusing is not the same as not knowing. Its control is
    `a_recording_that_asks_nothing_is_answered_with_nothing`: btop and
    asciiquarium query nothing, so an empty reply from them proves the
    harness is collecting rather than always returning empty.
    
    This is what the recordings were for. The table said DA2 nothing, and a
    real editor asking for it is what surfaced the omission -- not a
    re-reading of the specification, which had been read.
    
    537 tests.

