[33mcommit 8b06d2d688b8950ebb19dcc50d03342ffff7f2cc[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 15:47:18 2026 -0600

    Replay real capture: btop, asciiquarium and vim
    
    `baud-bench/src/payload.rs` has said since it was written that the
    synthetic payloads want validating against real capture "before it is
    designed around", and until now that had not happened: every stream baud
    was tested and measured against was written by the same people who wrote
    baud. This is that capture, recorded once and committed as fixed input.
    
      btop.raw          524288 B  truecolour 38;2, box drawing, dense
                                  repaint, SGR mouse modes
      asciiquarium.raw  335479 B  curses animation: heavy cursor addressing
                                  with 16-colour SGR
      vim-unicode.raw   138571 B  scroll regions (72 DECSTBM), and CJK,
                                  emoji, ZWJ, RTL and combining marks
    
    Each was chosen for a stream the others do not produce. Together they
    are 998 KB and about 217,000 escape sequences from three programs that
    have never heard of baud.
    
    `scripts/capture-tui.py` is how they were made and is kept for
    provenance, not because anything runs it. `script(1)` alone is not
    enough: backgrounded it has no stdin, so the child sees EOF and exits
    after 152 bytes of setup, and it has no window size to give -- btop
    answered the first attempt with "Terminal size too small" *inside the
    capture*, which is how the size got fixed. The harness opens the pty
    itself and sets `TIOCSWINSZ`, and can drive the program with keys, which
    is what makes vim scroll rather than sit on one screen.
    
    They are fixtures under `tests/recordings/`, deliberately not entries in
    `docs/research/sources/MANIFEST.toml`: that manifest is for
    specifications a claim can cite, with a url and a retrieval status. A
    recording is neither retrieved nor citable -- it is input. **And a
    capture is not reproducible**: btop draws live CPU figures, so a second
    run produces different bytes. That is why they are committed rather than
    regenerated, and why the test pins each file's length and FNV-1a.
    
    Four tests, and they run in CI with everything else:
    
    - the recordings are unchanged
    - ingesting them never panics and leaves the cursor on the grid, in all
      three OpModes
    - the result does not depend on where the reads were cut -- chunk 1, 7,
      64, 1023 against a real stream that splits multi-byte UTF-8 and
      control strings across boundaries constantly
    - each recording still carries what it was chosen for, because a
      replacement that no longer has truecolour or CJK would keep the others
      passing while testing less
    
    The ingest test carries a positive control: every assertion in it holds
    trivially for a blank grid, so it also requires the recording to have
    painted cells. `Runs` is exempt -- it reports representation, and an
    unpromoted line is legitimately blank there.
    
    535 tests.

