[33mcommit 3825d56d7a24b7fe3cd6b34f4d45b790a9fdce3f[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 14:44:47 2026 -0600

    Add the input type system, checked against the specification's table
    
    The types the three input layers agree on. `Modifiers`, `FnKey`,
    `KeyCode`, `KeyEventKind`, `KeyEvent` -- no encoder yet, because the
    layers disagree about encoding and that disagreement is the next
    decision, not this one.
    
    `Modifiers` is a bitset rather than four `bool` fields, and not only
    because `baud-domain` is gated at zero of those: the wire form *is* a
    mask. `[[source: ctlseqs]]` line 2565 gives fifteen rows of
    `Code | Modifiers` and every one is `1 + mask` with Shift 1, Alt 2,
    Control 4, Meta 8. Four booleans would have to be folded back into that
    number at every call site, which is where one call site folds it
    differently.
    
    The tests read the table out of the pinned source and check all fifteen
    rows, rather than restating it: a modifier table written into a test
    from memory agrees with one written into the source from the same
    memory, and neither has been read. This is `spec_inventory.rs`'s
    direction -- the document checks the code -- applied to the send side.
    The row count is asserted first, because an empty table would otherwise
    pass every assertion by having nothing to check.
    
    Reading the source rather than remembering it caught the special case:
    "the SS3 sent before F1 through F4 is altered to CSI when sending a
    function key modifier as a parameter". Unmodified F1 is `SS3 P` and
    shift-F1 is `CSI 1 ; 2 P`, so an encoder that appends the modifier
    without changing the introducer sends something nothing parses.
    `FnKey::changes_introducer_when_modified` is that rule, tested across
    all twenty.
    
    `from_wire` and `FnKey::new` refuse out-of-range rather than clamping.
    Clamping invents a keypress the application never made, which is the
    same refusal `DA` makes by under-claiming.
    
    Not extending `every_public_function_is_called_by_a_test` to
    `baud-domain`: 135 of its 233 public functions are not named in any test
    because they are newtype accessors exercised *through* `baud-core`.
    The gate is meaningful for `Term`, whose public methods are the surface
    an embedder calls and where one with no caller means nothing exercises
    that path -- which is how `resize` hid a panic. Applying it to the
    vocabulary types would buy 135 direct-call tests and no coverage.
    
    528 tests.

