[33mcommit 4280065558e8d5d41651bdec0ed5b3eca399b6d5[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 13:42:50 2026 -0600

    Clear both buffers on resize, and clamp the alt-screen restore
    
    Two more from the axis the byte-driven generator cannot reach, both in
    the same family as the resize crash.
    
    **The primary was left rotated.** `resize` discards the row rotation,
    which is only sound if the buffer that rotation described is cleared
    with it. `clear_screen` clears whichever screen is *current*, so a
    resize taken on the alternate screen cleared alt and left the primary
    holding rotated content with `rot` back at zero -- every row displaced.
    The path is ordinary rather than exotic: a full-screen application is on
    the alternate screen, which is exactly when a user resizes the window.
    Both buffers are cleared now.
    
    **`?1049 l` did not clamp.** Its own comment quotes
    `[[source: ctlseqs]]` saying it restores "cursor as in DECRC" -- so it
    is DECRC, and it must not be a second implementation. It was one, and
    the second lacked the clamp the first had just gained, so a save taken
    before a resize put `cy` outside the grid again. Both now go through
    `place_saved`.
    
    That is the shape worth naming: an operation defined by reference to
    another one, implemented twice. The quotation was right there in the
    comment.
    
    Verified by removing the clamp again: `the_alt_screen_restore_clamps_
    like_decrc` fails, and passes with it.
    
    515 tests, 53 properties at PROPTEST_CASES=20000.

