[33mcommit 4904710c499952f82ef279050c7e7e99650ca60f[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 13:29:36 2026 -0600

    Fix a resize crash and an unclamped DECRC
    
    `resize` is a public entry point no generated program could reach --
    the generator emits bytes and this is an API call -- so it had no
    property coverage at all. Adding it found two bugs, one of them a panic.
    
    **The panic.** `RowRot` is bounded by the row count at the time it was
    advanced, so shrinking the window can leave it at or past the new one.
    `View::phys` is a single conditional subtract, deliberately, because
    both operands are normally below `rows`; past that it names a row
    outside the grid, so `clear_screen` blanked rows nobody can see and
    `viewport`'s `rotate_left` panicked outright -- 41 rows of rotation
    against a ten-row slice. The reproduction is a full-screen application
    and a user dragging a corner. `resize` now resets the rotation before
    clearing, and RIS does too: the grid is cleared in both, so there is
    nothing left for a rotation to describe.
    
    **The unclamped restore.** DECSC may predate a resize, and `resize`
    homes the cursor but cannot touch a position already stored. `ESC 7` on
    fifty rows then `ESC 8` on one left `cy` at 49. That is not only a wrong
    CPR answer -- which `Term::cursor` already rules out for the column, "a
    200-column page has no column 201" -- because writes and erases index
    with `cy` and `phys` assumes it is in range. Restore now clamps, in one
    place, since DECRC arrives two ways and two copies of a clamp is how one
    of them comes to lack it. `cx` clamps to `cols` rather than `cols - 1`
    so a saved deferred-wrap sentinel survives an unchanged grid.
    
    Found by `resize_between_reads_is_sound`, which feeds the stream in two
    halves with the resize between them: SIGWINCH does not wait for a CSI to
    finish. It shrank to `[CupHuge, Decslrm]` / `[Restore]` at one by one.
    
    513 tests, 53 properties at PROPTEST_CASES=20000.

