Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 13:10:57 2026 -0600

    Enter VT52 mode from the generator
    
    DECANM was not in the private-mode list, so no generated program ever
    left ANSI. The entire VT52 command set was unreachable from every
    property here -- fourteen operations, including `ESC Y` direct
    addressing, whose two bias-`0x20` coordinate bytes are a parser
    sub-state nothing else reaches.
    
    `Op::Vt52Block` enters with `CSI ? 2 l`, runs VT52 commands, and leaves
    with `ESC <` in a single op. Self-contained on purpose: VT52 changes how
    every subsequent byte parses -- `ESC [` is not a CSI introducer there --
    so a program left in VT52 would make the composing properties diverge
    for a reason that is not a bug, the same way RIS and a pending UTF-8
    sequence did.
    
    It broke `replies_are_well_formed` on the first run, and again the
    property was what was wrong. `ESC / Z` is the VT52 identity: the one
    reply that is neither a CSI nor a control string, because in VT52 mode
    there is no CSI to answer with. Checked against xterm's `CASE_DECID`,
    which emits `ESC`, `/` and the id byte. The property had no arm for it
    because nothing could reach VT52 to produce one, so it was a property
    about ANSI replies wearing the name of a property about replies -- the
    third time that exact shape has turned up here, after the `ESC`-only
    introducer assumption and the alt-screen filter.
    
    508 tests, 52 properties at PROPTEST_CASES=20000.

