[33mcommit 9e0b6d072f729541776f1b51c659ab1d9307d21d[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 12:38:18 2026 -0600

    Generate multi-byte UTF-8, and fix three properties it exposed
    
    `Op::Text` emitted `0x21..0x7f`, so no generated program ever contained
    a multi-byte sequence, and `properties.rs` uses `any::<u8>()`, which
    spells a well-formed grapheme cluster about as often as it spells a
    well-formed CSI. The half of the job this repo calls "what the bytes
    between the sequences mean" -- UTF-8 decoding, width, clustering -- had
    no property coverage at all, and chunk invariance is exactly where it
    matters: a multi-byte sequence split across a read boundary is the
    classic terminal bug and this suite could not produce one.
    
    `Op::Utf8` emits every width class -- 2/3/4-byte, combining marks, CJK,
    ZWJ emoji, regional indicators, an Indic conjunct -- and the malformed
    forms whose recovery has already been wrong here: the overlong
    `E0 80 AF` that used to render `/`, the truncation `C2 41 42` whose
    recovery ate the valid successor, a lone continuation, and a surrogate.
    
    Three properties failed and none of them was a bug in baud. A program
    ending mid-sequence is an **open state**, verified rather than assumed:
    
      E2 86              -> nothing        (pending; the third byte usually
                                            arrives in the next read)
      E2 86 92           -> U+2192         (completes)
      E2 86 ESC [ 0 " q  -> U+FFFD, DECSCA (flushed by a byte that cannot
                                            continue it, per Unicode 3.9.5,
                                            and the escape is applied)
    
    So appending ops to a program is not invisible, and worse, two suffixes
    flush at different moments under whatever rendition is current --
    `[Decsca(1), <E2 86>]` makes one side's flush a *protected* U+FFFD,
    which selective erase spares and plain erase does not. That is why the
    shrink for `decsed_erases_a_subset_of_ed` had a DECSCA in it.
    
    The composing properties now diverge after a `FLUSH` op rather than
    before it, with the baselines taking the same path. Behaviour pinned by
    `an_incomplete_sequence_at_the_end_renders_nothing_until_flushed`.
    
    507 tests, 51 properties at PROPTEST_CASES=20000.

