Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 11:58:07 2026 -0600

    Prove the AVX2 scanner is not a portable fallback
    
    The previous step proved `generated_programs_match_on_avx2` runs on CI.
    It does not prove any AVX2 instruction executed: the property compares
    `feed_avx2`'s output against `feed`'s, and identical output is exactly
    what it asserts, so it would pass unchanged if `feed_avx2` delegated to
    the portable scanner.
    
    I recorded that as needing a counter inside `scan_avx2` -- a hot-path
    change that cannot be measured on arm64 -- and that refusal was wider
    than its reason. A counter is one way to observe the scanner, not the
    only one. `feed_avx2` calls `feed_with::<Avx2>`, which monomorphises, so
    the question is answerable from the binary: the specialisation must
    exist, and its body must contain `vpmovmskb`, the AVX2 movemask, which
    is the single point where an intrinsic's result enters the type system
    through `LaneMask::from_movemask`. `Portable` compiles to SSE2 on that
    target and emits `pmovmskb` without the `v`, so the two markers do not
    collide.
    
    Validated locally as far as arm64 allows: the binary here has one
    `feed_with` symbol and zero matching `Avx2`, so the check discriminates
    rather than passing vacuously.
    
    `docs.rs` gains the intrinsic to `NOT_A_TEST` with its reason: the
    scanner reads backticked snake_case in the docs as test names, and the
    first version of this commit shipped that failure because the
    pre-commit hook runs `treefmt` and not the suite.
    
    This is `CLAUDE.md`'s "grep for `_mm256` after any port" moved out of a
    habit and into the gate.

