[33mcommit 86d5b8ecc198bfd75a841f20da44fd625f0678eb[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 11:39:22 2026 -0600

    Send every parameterised CSI bare and saturating, not just with a value
    
    Reachability was the first rung: the enumeration checks prove each
    operation is emitted. They say nothing about what it is emitted *with*,
    and the answer was that every numeric op always wrote a number. Of the
    thirty-one finals the generator produced, only `c` was ever sent bare.
    
    A defaulted parameter is a distinct code path per operation, and the
    defaults are not uniform -- CUP is 1;1, SU is 1, SGR is 0, DECSTBM is the
    whole screen. That is the arithmetic the CPR off-by-one lived in, so
    "the operation was dispatched" is not the same as "the operation was
    dispatched with the parameter it would get in the wild". The saturating
    end is the same axis: `Param::push` stops at 65535, and that ceiling was
    only ever reached through CUP.
    
    `every_parameterised_final_is_also_sent_bare_and_saturating` requires
    every `(intermediate, final)` the corpus sends with a parameter to also
    appear bare and with a five-digit value. It derives both sets from the
    corpus rather than from the table, which is what made it useful: the
    hand-written table missed five operations, and the check named them in
    two rounds -- DECSCUSR `SP q`, DECIC `' }`, DECDC `' ~`, DECSCA `" q`
    and DECRQM `$ p`. All five carry an intermediate, and a bare spelling
    has to keep it; dropping it names a different operation, which the first
    version of the table did.
    
    Nothing broke at PROPTEST_CASES=20000 once all thirty-six were covered.
    That is a weaker result than the DECSTR bug the reach check found, and
    it is still worth having: the defaults are now exercised rather than
    assumed.
    
    506 tests, 51 properties.

