[33mcommit 0f086ca6fb7d67985c1b8528f1afcd5222ab380b[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 11:13:22 2026 -0600

    DECSTR must not leave the alternate screen or reset the mouse
    
    Widening the generator to emit every `done` CSI form immediately found a
    real bug in a row that had been `done` for a long time. What was missing
    was an input shape, not an implementation.
    
    `soft_reset` cleared `screen`, `alt_saved`, `mouse` and `mouse_encoding`.
    So a full-screen application sending `CSI ! p` to recover a confused
    terminal was dropped onto the primary screen mid-draw, and `alt_saved =
    None` discarded what `?1049 h` had saved, so leaving the alt screen
    afterwards could not restore the primary.
    
    The alternate screen and mouse tracking are xterm extensions, so a DEC
    manual's list of what DECSTR resets cannot mention them and xterm is the
    authority. In `ReallyReset` all four are inside the `if (full)` RIS
    branch (14432-14562): `send_mouse_pos`/`extend_coords` at 14439/14443,
    and `FromAlternate` at 14503 next to `ClearScreen` -- which DECSTR must
    not do either. Its soft path does nothing mouse-related at all.
    
    `saved` stays: DEC STD 070 has DECSTR set the saved cursor to home and
    xterm resets it only under RIS. Where the two disagree about a *DEC*
    function the DEC manual wins; where the item is an xterm extension,
    xterm does.
    
    Found by `the_alt_screen_never_leaks_into_the_primary`, which shrank to
    `[Decstr, Sgr(1), El(0), ...]`. Pinned deterministically in
    `tests/xterm/soft_reset_scope.rs`; three of its four cases fail against
    the old behaviour, and the fourth is RIS, which is unchanged.
    
    Also makes the two documents that read `docs/vt-coverage.md` agree with
    it. HPA's row was written `` `CSI \` ``, and CommonMark does not process
    backslash escapes inside a code span -- so it rendered as `CSI \` and
    named no final byte. It now uses a double-backtick span, and
    `spec_inventory` reads both forms; its comment called the old one "a
    markdown escape", which is the misconception that kept it.
    
    504 tests.

