[33mcommit fdaa82bf1a13deb005afaddb9ec99cc1246e0f5d[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 08:08:04 2026 -0600

    Fix a chunk-invariance bug past the sixteenth CSI parameter
    
    The two feeds discarded excess parameters differently, so the result
    depended on where a read boundary fell.
    
    `fast.rs` accumulates a parameter into a local and stores it only under
    the capacity bound, so a seventeenth parameter was parsed and dropped.
    `machine.rs` wrote each digit straight into `params[nparam - 1]` and
    `nparam` pinned *at* capacity -- so once the sixteenth parameter was in,
    the separator was swallowed and the seventeenth's digits appended to the
    sixteenth. `CSI ...;61;62;63 m` stored 6263 where the fast path stored
    62.
    
    Measured, not argued:
    
      payload                                    one read   byte at a time
      CSI 0;0;...;0;1;4 m  (bold, then dropped)  0x1        0x0
      CSI 1;2;...;63;64 m  (eighteen attrs)      0xffff     0x7fff
    
    `ingest_is_chunk_invariant` did not catch it because its generator does
    not emit sequences with more than sixteen parameters.
    
    The fix gives `ParamIdx` a named `CAPACITY` and a `next_capped` that
    saturates one *past* it, so "sixteen arrived" and "more than sixteen
    arrived" stay distinguishable and the digit branch knows to stop. Reads
    go through `Term::nparams`, which clamps -- slicing `params` with the raw
    count would now panic, so the clamp is not optional. `fast.rs` and
    `term.rs` take their `16` from the same constant instead of four
    literals.
    
    Found while testing whether the DECRQSS reply buffer survived `WIRE`
    growing 13 -> 18. It does; this was underneath.
    
    A/B on Host B, medians of 9 interleaved, against `2ed265b`: spans
    197.3 -> 201.0, promote hybrid 217.8 -> 216.0, TUI through a PTY
    218.8 -> 217.3. Every range overlaps, so there is no result and the
    bound check is free.
    
    495 tests.

