    Translate the national replacement sets
    
    The row said "no machine-readable source for the per-language
    substitutions". I applied the test I had used for the line-drawing table --
    does an implementation have it -- and xterm does. `charsets.h` carries them
    as `MAP`/`UNI` macros with the Unicode codepoint beside each replaced
    position: 14 sets, 162 substitutions, generated rather than transcribed.
    
    Cited as xterm's judgement, not as DEC's, exactly as the line-drawing table
    is cited as foot's. foot itself was worth checking and does *not* have them
    -- `enum charset { CHARSET_ASCII, CHARSET_GRAPHIC }` is the whole of it --
    so the refusal was mainstream, and mainstream is not a source.
    
    `PackedCell::with_charset` is now the single point where a wire byte becomes
    a character under a named set, so no path can honour German and forget
    Finnish.
    
    **The run had to carry the set.** `promote` replays stored bytes into cells
    long after the designation, so a line written under German and materialised
    later would be translated with whatever set was current. `Run` took the byte
    from padding it already had -- the same reason it carries the protection bit.
    
    Two bugs found while testing, and both are the same shape as things already
    in this file:
    
      * `put_ascii_run` tested `flags == NONE` first, and a national set sets no
        flag -- only the line-drawing set has one. So UK and German text took the
        ASCII fast path untranslated, and graphics worked only because
        `refresh_flags` gives it a flag. The charset is tested first now.
      * My promotion test wrote no text, so the line never promoted, and in
        `OpMode::Runs` `row()` reports representation -- an unpromoted line reads
        blank. It failed for the wrong reason. CLAUDE.md warns about exactly
        this trap.
    
    Measured, paired: 910.2 [899.7-917.0] before, 912.6 [892.7-925.4] after --
    overlapping, no result. The charset test is hoisted out of the loop like the
    two beside it.

