[33mcommit 9f6ddc36cdc19186eb4fb1f8d6ce11676b3be1e5[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 06:17:43 2026 -0600

    SGR 58/59: the rendition can grow because the cell does not hold it
    
    I wrote that 58/59 "need a third ColorWord, which the eight-byte rendition
    has no room for", and called it the one refusal whose reason held up when
    checked against the structure. It did not hold up; I had not checked it.
    
    `StyleWord` is **twelve** bytes. `PackedCell` is the eight-byte one, and it
    holds a `StyleId` *index* into a 64-entry table rather than a rendition. So
    the rendition can grow without the cell moving: sixteen bytes now, costing
    `NSTYLE * 4` -- 256 bytes in a 24 MB Storage. The cell was never being asked
    for room.
    
    `SGR 58` takes the same `5;n`, `2;r;g;b` and ISO 8613-6 colon spellings as
    38 and 48, so it joins their parser rather than getting a parallel copy. The
    colon/semicolon distinction is the part that is easy to get wrong -- reading
    `58:2:Pi:r:g:b` as the five-part form shifts every channel by one -- and one
    copy of it cannot drift from another. `SGR 59` and `SGR 0` restore the
    default, which means "follow the foreground".
    
    DECRQSS reports it and omits it when default, so the answer stays something
    an application can send back verbatim.
    
    Measured, paired: 916.5 [906.9-927.7] before, 922.4 [877.5-928.2] after --
    overlapping, no result. The 24 MB Storage assertion in perf_floor still
    passes; 256 bytes does not move it.
    
    What remains of the row is a scope decision and says so: `Attrs` has eight
    free bits, so Fraktur, proportional spacing, framing and the ideogram
    attributes are out because nothing consumes them and no fixture sends them,
    not because there is nowhere to put them.

