[33mcommit aa29c839c749b7170ba6226c5b7764c23ed992e7[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 06:06:20 2026 -0600

    SGR fonts go in the rendition word, which had sixteen spare bits
    
    The row said fonts were out because "no renderer consumes them, and a bit
    set and never read is the failure this table exists to catch", and I
    repeated that they needed per-cell bits the eight-byte cell does not have.
    `StyleWord` carried an explicit `_pad: u16` -- sixteen free bits, put there
    because zerocopy rejects implicit padding.
    
    A font is a *rendition*, not a property of a cell's content, so it belongs
    in the rendition word and never needed `PackedCell` to grow. I checked the
    wrong word before calling it blocked.
    
    `SGR 10` selects the primary font and `11-19` the nine alternates, `SGR 0`
    resets it with everything else per ECMA-48 § 8.3.117, and it is part of the
    interning key -- two cells differing only in font are two styles, which is
    what makes the key correct rather than merely small.
    
    "Never read" resolves as it did for charset designations and `?1001`: baud
    records the selection, and `Term::style_of` is the accessor a renderer needs
    anyway. The cell holds an index into a 64-entry direct-mapped table, so
    without it a renderer cannot get the colours either -- and it re-reads the
    slot rather than trusting the id, because that table evicts on collision.
    That is the check DECSCA protection needed and did not have.
    
    DECRQSS reports the font and omits it when primary, so the answer stays
    something an application can send back verbatim.
    
    The audit caught a cast: `sgr()` returns `u8` now, since the range is
    `10..=19` by construction and baud-core's budget is a floor rather than
    slack. Measured, paired: 929.6 [924.0-936.0] before, 925.6 [917.2-934.0]
    after -- overlapping, no result.
    
    Of the row, 58/59 stay out for a *measured* reason rather than an assumed
    one: an underline colour needs a third `ColorWord` and the rendition word
    has no room for one.

