[33mcommit 77411384bfb4d6f40f9879cab313c2601b376168[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 05:52:39 2026 -0600

    8-bit C1 works under Latin-1, where it is unambiguous
    
    Fifth refusal, and this one I created myself. It reads "8-bit C1 introducers
    are out -- they are UTF-8 continuation bytes", which is true *in UTF-8*, and
    baud has not been always-UTF-8 since `ESC % @` landed. In ISO 8859-1 there
    is no multi-byte sequence for `9B` to be part of, so it is unambiguously
    CSI. The reason was scoped to an encoding without saying so, and then the
    encoding became a variable.
    
    `c1_as_escape` maps a C1 byte to the 7-bit form it stands for, and dispatch
    goes through the `Escape` state rather than a second table.
    `[[source: ctlseqs]]` calls `ESC [` "a 7-bit equivalent to 8-bit" `9B` -- the
    same operation, so two tables could drift and one cannot.
    
    8-bit ST needed the string states too, which the first attempt missed:
    `9C` became payload and the control string never ended. OSC, DCS passthrough,
    DCS ignore and the SOS/PM/APC state all take it now.
    
    The UTF-8 half is unchanged and still out, for the reason that has not moved:
    a stray `9B` there is a decode error and renders U+FFFD.
    
    Two doc rows were also wrong rather than merely narrow. "No escape sequence
    in scope selects" the national replacement sets -- every one of them is
    selected and named; what is missing is the *glyph mapping*. And the C1 row
    now says which encoding it is talking about.

