[33mcommit 7849d6d8f693f764da767893653cd2e94849a543[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 05:32:53 2026 -0600

    Check that a shared final byte means two operations, not one
    
    I named this gap and said it needed scoping before building. It needed
    counting: ctlseqs gives exactly two finals more than one arity, so it is a
    small check rather than the framework I was imagining.
    
    Every other check asks whether a sequence reached *a* handler. None asked
    whether it reached the *right* one, which is how the five-parameter `CSI T`
    came to scroll the screen -- SD and XTHIMOUSE share a final byte and differ
    only by parameter count, so "was it dispatched" answered yes while the
    answer was the wrong operation.
    
    `T` (1 and 5) and `s` (0 and 2) are the whole population. Feeding both
    arities must leave the terminal in different states; two operations that
    share a byte and produce the same result are one operation wearing two
    names.
    
    The `s` pair is fed with `?69h` first. Without DECLRMM both arities really
    are SCOSC, correctly, and comparing them would measure the mode rather than
    the arity -- a check that passes for the wrong reason is the shape this
    session has hit repeatedly.
    
    Verified by re-planting the original bug: removing the XTHIMOUSE arm makes
    the check fail rather than pass.

