[33mcommit b6194b4181a11f839f03057a11030715120fe77a[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 05:24:43 2026 -0600

    Five-parameter CSI T is XTHIMOUSE, and it was scrolling the screen
    
    Applied the reading rule to the last refusal and it found a live bug next
    door.
    
    `?1001` is refused because it "requires a cooperating program, else xterm
    will hang". Read against its scope, the hang is an *event-loop* property --
    "all X events are ignored until the proper escape sequence is received" --
    and baud has no event loop to block. The real reason is that baud has no
    selection model to highlight with, which is narrower and does not go away.
    
    But the escape sequence that warning is about is
    `CSI Ps ; Ps ; Ps ; Ps ; Ps T`, and `[[source: ctlseqs]]` gives the final
    byte `T` to two operations separated only by **arity**: one parameter is SD,
    five are XTHIMOUSE. baud read the five-parameter form as SD.
    
    So an application asking to highlight a region **scrolled the screen**. And
    silently: nothing was counted, because SD is implemented and the sequence
    reached a real handler. Every check that asks "was this dispatched" said
    yes. It was dispatched to the wrong operation, which is a failure mode none
    of them look for -- `Params::len` is new because arity was not something the
    parser had ever needed to ask about.
    
    Recognised now, counted, and not acted on. Both arities pinned: five must
    not scroll, one still must.

