[33mcommit aa031365372c10a573a73626f96ea70396201eeb[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 05:15:10 2026 -0600

    Pin what the three standing refusals actually refuse
    
    Two of them are being read as unimplemented operations and they are not.
    DECRQM and DECRQSS are implemented; what each refuses is a particular
    *answer*, and refusing it is the correct behaviour. DECRQM answers 0 rather
    than 2 for a mode baud does not have, because 2 says the mode exists and is
    merely off, whereupon the application sets it and believes it took effect.
    DECRQSS answers 1 for valid and 0 for invalid, which is the reverse of the
    DEC manuals and what ctlseqs records as the errata.
    
    `?1001` is the one that is genuinely unimplemented, and the invariant for
    that is not silence but *countability*: a terminal a sequence can hang is
    worse than one that ignores it, and ignoring it invisibly is the `parsed`
    failure. It is counted.
    
    All three asserted rather than described now, so the next reader does not
    have to re-derive which kind each is.
    
    And the reading rule that closed the other three is written down, because it
    caught me three times in a row. A refusal's stated reason and its scope
    drift apart: "a guessed table would be unrecoverable" became "no table",
    "X10 would name a different cell" became "no X10", "answering a clipboard
    read is unsafe" became "record nothing". In each case the reason was sound
    and applied to something narrower than what was refused -- and in each case
    the reason survives the fix. baud still does not guess a glyph, still
    refuses a position X10 cannot name, still answers a clipboard read with
    silence.

