[33mcommit d403d9559ea72f45ccc6c57bc211a0ea2d50b0d7[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 05:08:09 2026 -0600

    Implement X10 mouse reports, refusing only what X10 cannot name
    
    The refusal said mouse reports are SGR-only because X10 caps each ordinate
    at 223 against a 2048-column grid, so a report from column 400 does not
    truncate -- it names a different cell. That is true, and it is a reason to
    refuse *that position*, not the encoding. Same shape as the glyph table: a
    reason not to answer wrongly, read as a reason not to answer.
    
    The cost of reading it the second way: an application that enables `?1000`
    and nothing else -- the original protocol, which is what `[[source:
    ctlseqs]]` documents first -- got silence on an 80x24 screen where every
    single byte was encodable.
    
    X10 is the default now, because that is what the protocol says it is: no
    mode turns it on, it is what you get without `?1005`, `?1006` or `?1015`.
    `?1006` selects SGR and resetting it returns to X10 rather than to "off",
    since there is no off. Positions within 223 are sent as `ESC [ M Cb Cx Cy`;
    the rest are dropped and counted in `mouse_unencodable`, exactly as before.
    
    The boundary is pinned at 223 inclusive, one-based, and both sides of it are
    tested -- along with the same position under `?1006`, which has no such
    limit because it writes decimal.
    
    That is the last of the six refusals that had a closable half. The three
    remaining are not gaps: DECRQM answering 0 and the DECRQSS errata are
    correct behaviours filed as refusals, and `?1001` is a refusal to implement
    something that hangs the terminal.

