[33mcommit e4e6f0865a446c68f92e85db81a61fcb39c6e932[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 04:58:24 2026 -0600

    Count the OSC 52 refusal instead of dropping it silently
    
    Of the six refusals, three are not gaps at all -- DECRQM answering 0 and the
    DECRQSS errata are *correct behaviours* filed under "refusals", and `?1001`
    is a refusal to implement something that hangs the terminal. This one had a
    real hole in it.
    
    `Osc::Clipboard(_) => {}`. A bare no-op, for both halves.
    
    The silence is load-bearing and stays: answering a clipboard read with
    anything, empty string included, tells a remote program the feature works and
    it will keep asking, and what it would read back is whatever the user last
    copied. That is the right answer to the *application*.
    
    Being unable to tell afterwards that one was asked is a different thing, and
    it is the failure vt-coverage.md refuses everywhere else -- the same
    distinction DSR forced, where no reply is correct and no *count* was not. An
    embedder that wants to implement the clipboard needs to know the requests are
    arriving; with a bare `=> {}` it could not.
    
    `clipboard_reads_refused` and `clipboard_writes_dropped` count them, kept
    apart because they are different: the read is refused on security grounds,
    the write is dropped because baud has no clipboard to write to. Neither is
    `unsupported` -- OSC 52 is recognised, and refusing is not the same as not
    knowing.

