    Catch a document being wrong about the code
    
    I found the `OSC 8` staleness by reading, said the class "still needs a human
    sweep", and that is a gap rather than a conclusion. This is the check.
    
    `CLAUDE.md` listed `OSC 8` under "Unmeasured -- do not assert", beside reflow
    and selection, as something still needing random access into variable-length
    spans. Hyperlinks are implemented. Every existing check passed while it said
    so: no link was broken, no path had moved, no test name was stale. The
    document was wrong *about the code*, which is the one thing those checks
    cannot see -- and it is the failure `vt-coverage.md` exists to prevent, one
    file over.
    
    The rule is narrow on purpose. A mnemonic named in a "not done" section that
    also carries a **done** row in the coverage table is two documents in the
    same repository contradicting each other, and one of them is wrong.
    Struck-through entries are exempt: those are records of things that were once
    true and say so.
    
    It caught my own prose first. The sentence explaining that `OSC 8` had been
    removed from the list still named `OSC 8` inside the list, which is exactly
    the shape the check looks for. The list says what is unmeasured; git history
    says what used to be on it.
    
    Verified against a planted reintroduction: adding DECSTBM to the unmeasured
    list names DECSTBM rather than passing.

