Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 04:27:39 2026 -0600

    CLAUDE.md described a project two months behind the code
    
    Swept the agent-facing notes against what the code actually does, because a
    stale instruction file is worse than none: it is read first and believed.
    
    `OSC 8` was listed under "unmeasured -- do not assert", alongside reflow and
    selection, as something needing random access into variable-length spans. It
    is implemented: the link index rides in spare `CellFlags` bits and `NLINK`
    slots evict on collision. An agent reading that list would have thought
    hyperlinks were unbuilt and might have built them again.
    
    Nothing at all was said about the text half of the job, which is now most of
    this session's work: conformant UTF-8 decoding, generated width tables,
    UAX #29 clustering against Unicode's own 766-case suite, and a switchable
    input encoding with detection that reports rather than guesses. An agent
    told only about the control protocol would have no reason to look for any of
    it, and would re-derive the same decisions -- or worse, the same bugs.
    
    And the inventory direction is recorded, because it is the part that is easy
    to get backwards: `spec_inventory.rs` reads ctlseqs' own lists and requires
    baud to account for each entry. Every other check reads baud's table, and a
    table cannot show a row nobody wrote. That is not an abstract distinction --
    the first run found that full-screen scrolling had never moved the grid.

