    Inventory the ESC forms that carry an intermediate
    
    The `ESC Fe` check took only two-byte forms, so the half of the escape
    surface with an intermediate -- `ESC # 8`, `ESC SP F`, `ESC % G`, and every
    charset designation -- still had nothing but baud's own table checking it.
    131 forms now, because `ESC ( C` is expanded: `C` is a placeholder for the
    charset list, so feeding the literal letter would have tested one arbitrary
    byte instead of all of them, across all four G-slots.
    
    92 of 131 came back silent on the first run and the harness was wrong, not
    baud. `Term::charset` reports GL only, so designating into G1, G2 or G3 was
    invisible from outside -- `ESC ) 0` recorded the line-drawing set and nothing
    could observe that it had. Likewise `ESC % G` and `ESC SP G` change state no
    accessor exposed.
    
    Three accessors rather than three exemptions: `charset_in` for any G-slot,
    `input_encoding`, `c1_transmit`. All three are things an embedder needs
    anyway -- SO/SI can invoke G1 without another designation, so a renderer that
    can only see GL cannot draw the screen.
    
    That left six, and they are one category rather than six exceptions: each
    asks for what baud already does. S7C1T wants 7-bit replies, `ESC % G` wants
    UTF-8, and `ESC ( B` designates ASCII into a slot that already holds it.
    Satisfied, not silent -- the distinction dtterm's `is2` forced when it opened
    with `ESC SP F`.
    
    Verified against planted silence: dropping German makes the check name
    `ESC ( K` and its three sibling slots.

