    Inventory OSC and DCS against the specification
    
    The last two protocols with nothing but baud's own table checking them. I
    said the check "would need a different shape", which is a description of the
    work rather than a reason not to do it.
    
    The different shape is the payload. A string protocol declines for the wrong
    reason if the payload is implausible, so `OSC Ps` is fed `;1` -- a valid
    colour index where one is wanted, harmless text elsewhere -- and each `DCS`
    form is fed empty with `ST`, the shortest well-formed string of its shape.
    39 OSC codes and 8 DCS forms.
    
    `OSC 104` was the one finding, and it was the harness rather than baud: it
    resets a palette entry, the snapshot had no palette, and the prepared state
    never set one, so a correct reset changed nothing observable. Both are fixed
    rather than exempted -- the snapshot carries all 256 entries now and the
    preamble sets one -- which also means `OSC 4` is genuinely checked instead of
    passing because a malformed payload got counted.
    
    `OSC 52` is exempted with its reason: the clipboard read answers with silence
    on purpose, because any reply, empty included, tells a remote program the
    feature works and it will keep asking.
    
    Both verified against planted silence, and the DCS one took two attempts
    worth recording. Planting a return before the *valid* DECRQSS reply did not
    trip it, because an empty payload takes the invalid path and still answers;
    planting on that path names `DCS $q`. A check verified against the wrong
    branch is a check that has not been verified.

