[33mcommit 29bd2fbca837e7f8411f4d36a644ae07b63fbd39[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 03:54:06 2026 -0600

    Rotate the grid on scroll instead of copying it
    
    The previous commit made full-screen scrolling correct and cost 45% of cell
    ingest, and I wrote up the ring-mapped viewport as the fix rather than
    attempting it. It turned out to be four compile errors wide, so here it is.
    
    A scroll rotates an index: screen row `y` lives at physical row
    `(y + rot) % rows`. Every addressing path goes through `View::phys`, which is
    what makes it one line rather than twenty-six chances to forget it --
    `at`, `row_slice`, `get`, and the three direct row loops in the region copy
    paths all route through it.
    
    `Term::viewport` undoes the rotation once, in place with `rotate_left`, when
    a renderer asks for a contiguous slice. That is why it takes `&mut self`:
    not a wart, but where the laziness is paid for. A renderer reads once a
    *frame*; the copy version paid once a *scroll*, which on a streaming payload
    is every screenful.
    
    Measured, memory transport, ghostty stable across both: the copying version
    41.3 MB/s, the rotation **907.2**. `phys` uses a conditional subtract rather
    than `%` -- both operands are below `rows`, so one subtraction is enough,
    and the modulo was worth ~4% by itself (871 -> 907).
    
    Against the version that did not scroll at all, ~1035, this costs about 12%.
    That is the price of scrolling and it is stated rather than buried. The perf
    floor goes back to 70 with the whole episode written into the test, so the
    next person to see 38 MB/s knows what it means.
    
    Four callers needed changing for the `&mut self`, one of which was the
    README example -- and `the_readme_example_is_the_compiled_example` caught the
    drift immediately, which is the check earning its place.
    
    448 tests, and the property suites at PROPTEST_CASES=20000.

