[33mcommit 0a12283a1d8f64aeae8ebaa23f1f627243b30971[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 03:35:24 2026 -0600

    Full-screen scroll did not move the grid
    
    Found by inventorying ctlseqs' own CSI list rather than baud's table:
    `CSI 1 S` changed nothing observable. It is not SU that was broken.
    
    `scroll_region_up` takes an O(1) ring advance for the full-screen case --
    move `head`, blank the incoming row, return -- and `head` indexes the **run**
    table. `vp` is indexed by screen row, and in `Computed` and `Templated`
    nothing re-derives it. So the screen did not scroll: old text stayed exactly
    where it was and only the bottom row went blank. Every line of output past
    the last row, which is the most common scroll there is.
    
    It survived because every test in scrolling.rs sets a region with DECSTBM
    first, and a region takes the copying path. The full-screen case had no
    content test at all -- only cursor and region-boundary assertions.
    
    The ring path is right for `Runs`, where content *is* the runs, and it also
    needed the promoted flags cleared: only the last row was reset, so a
    promoted row kept materialised text from before the scroll.
    
    **The correction costs 45% and that is recorded, not hidden.** Cell ingest
    70+ MB/s to ~38, because a streaming payload scrolls every screenful --
    write amplification around 20:1 against rule #1's <= 1. copy_within per row
    instead of a scalar loop changed nothing; the cost is the frequency. The
    floor moved 70 -> 30 with the reason written into the test, not relaxed.
    
    Both states are unacceptable and only one is *wrong*, so the correct one
    ships. The fix that gets both is a ring-mapped viewport -- rotate `vp` by
    `head` as the run table already is -- which makes `viewport()` a projection
    rather than a contiguous slice. A design change, and the one CLAUDE.md
    already describes.
    
    Two smaller things the same inventory turned up:
    
      * `CSI > Ps <anything but q>` fell through to the **standard** table, so
        `CSI > 1 n` was answered as a DSR and `CSI > 1 s` acted as SCOSC. Same
        wrong-namespace hole the Lt/Equals guard fixed; `>` kept it because one
        final in the namespace is implemented, so the guard looked present.
      * TBC, SM/RM and DSR dropped unhandled *parameters* in silence. Counted
        now -- in a separate `unsupported_param`, because "I do not know this
        sequence" and "I know it but not this parameter" are different
        questions, and folding them together made rxvt's `CSI 1;3;4;5;6 l`
        reset string look like a conformance failure.

