[33mcommit ff4092415aaa6aba4bf6e4fbb5c5d0d8b6ddbcfe[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 03:16:03 2026 -0600

    Check the coverage table against ctlseqs, not only against the code
    
    vt-coverage.md is baud's own enumeration, and that is its weakness as
    evidence. coverage.rs and enumerated.rs both check the table against the
    code, and neither can see a row nobody wrote: an operation the
    specification defines and baud never listed is invisible -- not `todo`, not
    `out`, absent. Everything so far verified that baud does what its own table
    claims, which says nothing about what the table left out.
    
    spec_inventory.rs checks the other direction. For every plain CSI final
    ctlseqs defines, baud must either handle it or count it in
    Term::unsupported -- never consume it in silence.
    
    The claim is deliberately weaker than parity. ctlseqs documents 117 CSI
    sequences, many of them xterm extensions baud does not commit to, so
    requiring all of them would be demanding a different project. Requiring that
    none be *silently swallowed* is the actual invariant, and it is the `parsed`
    failure this file is built around, checked against the specification's list
    rather than baud's.
    
    Six failures on the first run were all the extractor, and each is a way this
    kind of check lies:
    
      * `CSI H` / `CSI f` is one cell naming two forms; taking the last token of
        the joined string missed `H` and claimed CUP was undocumented.
      * HPA's final byte *is* a backtick, written as a markdown escape, so
        splitting the cell on backticks cut the escape in half and yielded the
        backslash as the final.
      * `CSI p`, `CSI q` and `CSI ~` were reported as specification operations
        baud dropped. There is no bare `CSI p`: ctlseqs defines `! p`, `" p`,
        `$ p`, `# p` and `> Ps p`, all ending in `p`, none reached by `ESC [ p`.
        Taking the last token regardless invented an operation.
    
    `plain_final` now parses both sides identically -- parameters only, no
    marker, no intermediate -- so the two lists mean the same thing before they
    are compared. Verified against a planted gap: deleting the CUU row makes the
    check name `CSI A` rather than pass.

