[33mcommit 7837ea143e23e65d3c5caaee0972ab53e1aa4c48[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 03:05:18 2026 -0600

    Encoding detection that reports rather than guesses
    
    I kept explaining why auto-detection was a policy question instead of
    building it. It is implementable without guessing, if it answers rather than
    acts.
    
    `InputEncoding::detect` takes a sample and returns one of three things:
    
      * a UTF-8 BOM at offset zero is a signature, and decisive;
      * anything ill-formed as UTF-8 rules UTF-8 out -- ISO 8859-1 has no
        ill-formed bytes, all 256 are characters, so it is what *remains* rather
        than a guess;
      * a sample of nothing but ASCII returns **None**, because the two
        encodings agree on every byte below 0x80.
    
    The third answer is the one that makes this honest. Most of what a terminal
    sees is ASCII and decides nothing, and a rule that answered anyway would be
    inventing evidence -- which is also why detection cannot be a default.
    
    **Nothing in baud calls it.** An embedder does, and decides. Guessing an
    encoding wrong corrupts text silently and gives the application no way to
    find out, which is the failure vt-coverage.md refuses everywhere else, so
    the guess is not baud's to make; xterm makes it a resource for the same
    reason. detection_does_not_switch_the_terminal pins that feeding Latin-1-ish
    bytes changes nothing.
    
    A sequence truncated by the end of the sample is not evidence either. The
    next read may complete it, and treating it as ill-formed would make the
    answer depend on where the read boundary fell -- the chunk-invariance
    failure this project has had before, in a function that never sees a chunk
    boundary but would have inherited the shape of the bug.
    
    Done count 136 -> 137.

