[33mcommit aff69f5cfac42cc27b1b615f5879c5bbc3558e9a[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 02:57:03 2026 -0600

    Keep two escape intermediates, which makes the two-byte finals reachable
    
    I listed the eleven multi-byte charset designations as out and said they
    needed "a parser feature". They needed a one-line bug fixed.
    
    `[[source: williams]]`'s `escape_intermediate` *collects* intermediates,
    plural. baud wrote `self.esc_inter = Some(b)` each time, so `ESC ( % 6`
    overwrote the `(` with the `%` and the G-slot was gone before the final byte
    arrived. Every two-byte designation was unreachable for that reason, not by
    decision, and the Tier 0 row said "final byte consumed; DECALN dispatched"
    without mentioning that it kept only one.
    
    Two are kept now, and a third makes the sequence inert -- williams: "if more
    than two intermediate characters arrive, the parser can just flag this so
    that the dispatch can be turned into a null operation."
    
    All eleven designations from ctlseqs land: `% 6` Portuguese, `" >` Greek,
    `% 2` Turkish, `% =` Hebrew, `" 4` DEC Hebrew, `" ?` DEC Greek, `% 0` DEC
    Turkish, `% 5` DEC Supplemental Graphics, `& 4` DEC Cyrillic. `% 3` and
    `& 5` share `DecSet::Undocumented`, because ctlseqs says "no documentation
    has been found for the mappings" -- which costs nothing, since baud
    translates none of them and only records which was designated.
    
    A two-byte final the specification does not list is counted rather than
    recorded. Recording it would make it look designated, and the point of
    naming the eleven is that a name means the designation was understood.
    
    Done count 134 -> 136.

