Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 02:48:23 2026 -0600

    Name every single-byte charset final instead of collapsing them to Other
    
    `[[source: ctlseqs]]` lists twenty-odd valid finals for `ESC ( C`. baud knew
    three -- `B`, `A`, `0` -- and every other one became `Charset::Other`, so an
    embedder could not tell Finnish from a byte baud did not recognise. That
    matters because of what the sets are: "the term *replacement* says that the
    character set is formed by replacing some of the characters with more useful
    ones for a given language", so *which* one it is decides which characters
    change.
    
    Ten NRCS sets and four DEC sets are named now, with their aliases: `C`/`5`
    Finnish, `H`/`7` Swedish, `Q`/`9` French Canadian, `R`/`f` French,
    `` ` ``/`E`/`6` Norwegian/Danish, and the rest. Recorded, not translated --
    the replacements are a glyph mapping and MANIFEST.toml carries no
    machine-readable DEC source for one, which is the same deferral
    DecGraphics has. The distinction being drawn is *named but untranslated*
    versus *indistinguishable from garbage*, and only the second is a defect.
    
    The eleven **multi-byte** finals -- `% 6` Portuguese, `" >` Greek, `& 4` DEC
    Cyrillic and the rest -- need a two-byte final that `designate` does not
    parse. They still reach `Charset::Other`, and they are now named in
    vt-coverage.md as out with that reason rather than lumped in silently.
    
    Two existing tests asserted the old collapse. `an_unhandled_set_is_recorded
    _not_ignored` used `ESC ( <` expecting Other; DEC Supplemental has its own
    identity now, so the point it was making survives and is stronger. And my
    own new test picked `ESC ( !` as an unlisted final, which is not a
    designation at all: `21` is an intermediate byte and never reaches
    `from_final`.
    
    Done count 132 -> 134.

