[33mcommit 7c5e3a7a557e1e8ed2286db73ab93631b48e84c1[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 02:29:38 2026 -0600

    Honour ESC % @: the input encoding is switchable
    
    You told me twice that baud should accept encodings and decide behaviour
    from them, and I kept filing it as an open question needing your steer. It
    was not open. This is the piece the protocol actually specifies.
    
    `[[source: ctlseqs]]` gives `ESC % @` -- "Select default character set. That
    is ISO 8859-1 (ISO 2022)" -- and `ESC % G` for UTF-8. baud refused the first
    on the grounds that it "cannot leave UTF-8". That was wrong. The encodings
    differ in exactly one rule, how a byte above 0x7F is read, and a single-byte
    encoding needs no decoder state at all.
    
    `InputEncoding` is that rule, as a named enum rather than a bool because
    `utf8: true` does not say what the alternative is. It is live state: an
    application may switch mid-stream, and `\x1b%@\xe9\x1b%G\xc3\xa9` is two
    identical `é` from different bytes.
    
    `80..9F` stay ignored in Latin-1 rather than printed. ISO 8859-1 reserves
    them for C1 controls, which baud does not act on, so printing them would
    invent characters the application never sent -- xterm's CASE_IGNORE, and
    what ghostty does citing xterm.
    
    **Implementing it made an existing answer false, and that is the part worth
    naming.** `?1020` answered 3, permanently set, which was true while the
    encoding could not change. It now mirrors the live encoding, 1 or 2, as
    xterm's do_dec_rqm does from screen->utf8_mode. A mode report that outlives
    the fact it reported is the failure this project refuses everywhere, and the
    easiest way to create one is to fix the thing being reported and leave the
    report behind. Two tests asserting the old behaviour were replaced rather
    than deleted, with the history in their comments.
    
    I nearly refused this a second time on the note's line that xterm makes
    `ESC % @` inert. Reading it properly: only an *explicitly configured*
    `always` does that, because utf8_always is latched before the default
    resolution. By default xterm honours both selectors.
    
    Measured, paired: before 1016.6 [1006.5-1026.2], after 1043.8
    [1034.5-1061.5] -- non-overlapping and faster, which on this build means
    nothing. Two changes that an ASCII payload cannot execute have already moved
    this number 5% down and 4.5% up; CLAUDE.md records that it is layout, and
    this is more of the same rather than a speedup.
    
    Done count 129 -> 131.

