[33mcommit de36a81665b8d52142dade9779fb26ae11e694b2[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 02:17:38 2026 -0600

    Require every `out` row to say why, and fix the one whose reason was wrong
    
    `out` is the only status that means "not implemented", which makes it the
    hole in "nothing is partial": any inconvenient row can be relabelled out of
    scope and every other check still passes. The legend already demanded a
    reason -- "deliberately not in scope; **the reason is in the row**" -- and
    nothing enforced it.
    
    every_out_row_states_a_reason does. It cannot judge whether a reason is
    good; that is review. It can insist one exists where a reader scanning the
    table will find it, which is what stops the relabelling. Two rows said only
    "see below", a pointer rather than a reason, and now carry both.
    
    **And one of the eight reasons was wrong.** SUB not displaying the DEC error
    character was justified here as "a rendering decision made where no renderer
    exists". That does not hold: baud stores codepoints, not pixels, so writing
    U+2426 would be storing a *character*, which it does all day, and the
    missing renderer prevents nothing.
    
    The real reason is a source conflict. `[[source: williams]]` says "SUB will
    also display the error character, a reversed question mark", describing
    VT220 through VT500 hardware; `[[source: ctlseqs]]` documents no such glyph
    and xterm writes none. This file already has a precedent for resolving that
    -- DECAWM's default is "No autowrap" in the VT510 manual and on in xterm,
    and baud follows xterm because that is what applications are written
    against. Same call here, and the asymmetry settles it: a glyph written that
    the application did not send is indistinguishable afterwards from text it
    did, while not writing one loses nothing observable, because the
    cancellation itself is the behaviour and is implemented.
    
    The behaviour does not change. The argument for it does, which matters more
    than it sounds: a bad reason in a table like this survives by looking like a
    decision, and the next person to touch SUB would have inherited it.
    
    The other seven hold up: the C1 and GR rows are forced by UTF-8 decoding,
    `ESC % @` cannot be honoured while baud is UTF-8-only, legacy charsets are
    under active reconsideration and say so, `?1001` would let a page hang the
    terminal, and the SGR font group is a bit set and never read.

