[33mcommit 7f17251db0abcaaeba5638717200b2bf00a68d4d[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 02:09:03 2026 -0600

    One cluster, one cell: wire ClusterBreak into the write path
    
    My own reasoning for deferring this was wrong, and worth naming: I said
    full cluster semantics needed the cell to know its cluster and CellFlags had
    no free bits. That was the wrong constraint. A continuation does not need
    the *cell* to identify anything -- it needs somewhere to live, and the marks
    table added last commit already is that. No cell bits were required.
    
    `put_cp` now asks ClusterBreak whether a codepoint continues what came
    before it. A continuation attaches to the base cell instead of taking one of
    its own, so `U+1F469 U+200D U+1F4BB` is two columns rather than four, and a
    flag is two rather than four. Width alone could never have got this right:
    those are three codepoints of width 2, 0 and 2, and every column of drift
    desynchronises the cursor against an application counting the same text.
    
    put_run resets the cluster state, because printable ASCII is blitted and
    never reaches put_cp -- without it the emoji after `a` would be judged
    against the emoji before it. One store per *run*, not per byte.
    
    **Regional indicators are East Asian Width `N`.** One column, while carrying
    Emoji_Presentation=Yes -- so a flag measured from EAW alone is half as wide
    as every terminal draws it, and `a_flag_is_one_cluster` failed on exactly
    that. Both properties are needed and neither subsumes the other: EAW W/F
    covers CJK, which is not emoji, and Emoji_Presentation covers this, which
    EAW calls neutral. This is the question the deleted `EmojiWidth` config was
    gesturing at, and it is answerable now only because the cluster's base is
    known.
    
    DECRQM `?2027` moves from 0 to 3, permanently set. It answered 0 for as long
    as clustering was unimplemented and that was honest then -- 2 would have
    claimed the mode existed and was merely off. The row moved when the
    implementation did, which is the only order that keeps the answer true.
    
    Measured, two paired runs: before 1038.3 [1015.4-1061.7] and 1035.9
    [1021.5-1044.1], after 1018.6 [989.6-1035.1] and 1016.6 [1003.5-1019.2].
    The first pair overlaps and the second misses by 2.3, so **at most ~2% and
    not cleanly resolved** -- reported as that rather than as a finding either
    way, because this file's own history is orderings that flipped between runs.
    The plausible mechanism is the per-run store in put_run.
    
    tests/docs.rs caught a stale citation on the way through: vt-coverage.md
    still named decrqm_does_not_claim_grapheme_clustering after the rename. That
    is the rot class it exists for, one commit after it was written.
    
    Done count 126 -> 129.

