[33mcommit 931906a75f612d98d99a38342ec00fac34292e4e[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 01:39:06 2026 -0600

    Implement UAX #29 clustering and measure what it costs
    
    Grapheme clustering was deferred because its cost was **unmeasured**, and
    this project's rule is that an unmeasured cost is not an argument. A cost
    cannot be measured without an implementation, so here is the implementation
    and here is the number.
    
    `ClusterBreak` implements the extended grapheme cluster boundary rules and
    passes **all 766 cases of Unicode's own GraphemeBreakTest.txt** -- a suite
    written by the people who wrote the rules, which is a far better test than
    anything invented here. Tables generated from GraphemeBreakProperty,
    emoji-data and DerivedCoreProperties, all pinned; grapheme_tables_match_the
    _pinned_data regenerates and compares.
    
    A state machine, not a pairwise predicate, because five rules need more
    context than the preceding codepoint: GB11 looks back *through* `Extend*` to
    a pictographic, GB12/13 need the parity of a regional-indicator run, and
    GB9c needs to know a linker appeared since the last consonant. That is why
    naive implementations split flags in the wrong place.
    
      corpus                          width only   + breaks    cost
      ascii                              1070.9      320.8     -70%
      latin + combining                   502.2      127.0     -75%
      CJK                                 160.9       61.5     -62%
      emoji ZWJ + flags + conjuncts       165.9       56.5     -66%
    
    Read the ASCII row with care: it measures a path ingest never takes.
    Printable ASCII is blitted by the run scanner and never becomes a Codepoint,
    so segmentation would cost nothing on it. The number that matters is CJK --
    about 3x on the non-ASCII decode path, which at three bytes per codepoint is
    roughly 500 MB/s down to 170. Real, and not disqualifying.
    
    Two fast paths came out of measuring rather than guessing. `GraphemeClass::of`
    binary-searched 1386 ranges for every ASCII byte, and `advance` paid four
    more searches per codepoint to learn a character was not an emoji, a virama
    or an Indic consonant -- the four lookback tables start at U+00A9, so nothing
    below that can be in any of them. Together: 93 -> 338 Mcp/s on ASCII.
    
    Ingest is unaffected and measured so: before 1007.1 [990.1-1016.4], after
    1007.2 [986.1-1030.0] and 996.7 [987.5-1006.8], overlapping. Nothing is
    wired into `Term` -- storage is the half that still has no number, and it is
    named precisely now instead of "unmeasured" covering both halves. A cluster
    does not fit in eight measured bytes, so it means a side table keyed per
    cell, the shape OSC 8 links already use.
    
    The audit gate refused five `bool` fields, correctly: `pict_zwj: bool` does
    not say what true means. Two of the pairs were one three-state machine each,
    so five bools became three named enums and the code reads better --
    `{pict_run, pict_zwj}` could spell "in a run *and* zwj seen", which the rule
    has no meaning for. It also refused four `as u32` casts in the bench, so
    `Codepoint::from_char` exists now and the cast lives where the newtype is.
    Neither budget was raised.
    
    Done count 124 -> 125.

