[33mcommit a4cecba144b602952bb4da15b7232ddc200a2ff5[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 00:49:43 2026 -0600

    Generate the width tables from Unicode data; delete the width API that lied
    
    Note G's recommendation 3. There were three things pretending to be width
    machinery and only one of them worked.
    
    `Width::of` was real and used, but its ranges were hand-written -- five
    zero-width ranges and a dozen wide ones, each added when something looked
    wrong. That set was not the Unicode property it claimed to be. It covered
    U+0300..U+036F, the *Latin* combining marks, and nothing else in the
    category: every Arabic, Hebrew and Devanagari mark got a column of its own.
    `بّ` cost two columns, a Devanagari virama cost one, and each of those is a
    cursor silently desynchronised against an application counting with
    wcwidth. Generated now from EastAsianWidth.txt and DerivedGeneralCategory,
    both pinned: 375 zero ranges where there were 5.
    
    `decode::measure` had zero callers. `Bytes<Utf8>::measure` returned
    `from_cells(0)` behind a `// model only` comment while taking a WidthTable
    it discarded. Both deleted rather than fixed -- measuring a stored UTF-8 run
    is for reflow, selection and search, none of which exist, and an API that
    answers zero looks implemented where its absence does not. `from_cells` went
    with them, which is the compiler confirming the cascade.
    
    WidthTable's `ambiguous` and `emoji` fields were read by nothing at all --
    the failure this project already refuses for modes, sitting in the width
    path. `ambiguous` is honoured now, with a `set_ambiguous_width` accessor;
    `[[source: uax11]]` § 5 makes it the reader's context, not something an
    escape sequence carries, so it belongs to the embedder and defaults Narrow
    as xterm's cjkWidth does. `emoji` is deleted: U+FE0F is Cf and therefore
    zero-width, so the question is whether the *preceding* character is
    upgraded, which is a grapheme-cluster property baud does not have.
    
    The tables are generated and committed, and
    width_tables_match_the_pinned_data regenerates and compares -- baud-domain
    has no build script, so nothing else would notice a stale table.
    
    **This cost ~5% of the streaming cells path and I could not find why.**
    1047 [1035-1065] to 995 [976-1015], three before and five after runs,
    ghostty stable, ranges never overlapping. The payload is pure ASCII so
    `Width::of` is never called in that benchmark; ruled out const-vs-static
    tables, `#[inline(never)]`, and passing the policy by value instead of by
    reference. Remaining suspect is code/rodata layout from 5.4 KB of tables --
    the class this repo has hit twice before. Recorded in CLAUDE.md as an open
    regression with the trade stated, because correct rendering of non-Latin
    text is worth 5% of a synthetic ASCII benchmark and the next person should
    not "recover" it by going back to a hand-written list.

