[33mcommit 1085855fa339a3bf255b4d827ee1395eb109cb80[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 00:30:08 2026 -0600

    Report the encoding truthfully: ?1020, ESC % G, and the C1 row
    
    Note G's recommendations 4 and 5. Three sequences that asked baud what
    encoding it uses and got nothing back.
    
    `ESC % G` -- "Select UTF-8 character set, ISO 2022" -- asks for what baud
    already does, so it is *satisfied*, the same distinction S7C1T draws.
    `ESC % @` asks for ISO 8859-1, which baud cannot do, and stays counted in
    Term::unsupported so an application can discover the refusal.
    
    DECRQM `?1020` now answers, and answers `3`. **A named divergence from
    xterm, deliberately.** ctlseqs marks the resource "read-only", yet xterm
    answers 1/2. baud answers 3 (permanently set) because 1 invites an
    application to send `CSI ? 1020 l`, receive no error, and believe it had
    turned the decoder off -- the exact failure vt-coverage.md refuses for
    DECRQM in those words. DECRPM has value 3 for this case, and
    ModeState::PermanentlySet already existed and had no user. Recorded so it
    can be reversed if an application is found that mishandles 3.
    
    The C1 row is reworded. It said baud *omits* the 8-bit form, which reads as
    a gap; `[[source: ecma48]]` § 5.3 specifies the 7-bit ESC Fe forms as the
    representation of C1 in a 7-bit environment, so those *are* the C1
    interface rather than a substitute for one. The row also now names who baud
    agrees with: ghostty refuses 8-bit C1, alacritty and wezterm accept it.
    
    Done count 121 -> 123.

