    It only ever checked `.md`, and doc comments in the code cite sources in
    exactly the same form -- `vt510-decscpp` had been cited from a doc comment
    with no manifest entry at all, and nothing noticed. A citation an engineer
    reads while changing the code is the one most likely to be acted on, so it
    is the last one that should go unverified. 19 citations checked before, 23
    now; verified the gate fails on a planted bad key rather than just counting
    higher.
    
    Section 2.2 of the note stays in the past tense on purpose. Both C10
    violations are fixed, but how they survived is the reusable part: one was
    invisible to Codepoint::new, the type that looks like it should have caught
    it, and the other misfired only for a single byte class.
    
