[33mcommit 036a432155d2331024e1a3709cf523486d9bf79c[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Aug 27 00:20:49 2026 -0600

    Reject overlong UTF-8, and stop error recovery eating a valid successor
    
    Two conformance bugs in the decoder, both violations of Unicode clause C10
    ("shall treat ill-formed code unit sequences as an error condition and shall
    not interpret such sequences as characters"). Found by a literature survey
    that read the standard against the code; both reproduce, and the first is a
    security bug.
    
    **1. Every overlong three- and four-byte form decoded to a real character.**
    `E0 80 AF` rendered `/`. That is the canonical path-traversal overlong: a
    filter checking for a slash would not have seen it. Verified against the
    real crate before the fix, not inferred.
    
    `[[source: unicode-ch3]]` Table 3-7 enforces well-formedness with two
    mechanisms and baud had one. The first-byte column excludes C0-C1 and
    F5-FF, which `utf8_lead` did. The *second*-byte column is narrowed for
    exactly four lead bytes -- E0, ED, F0, F4 -- and the standard flags this
    itself: the exceptions "occur only in the second byte of a sequence".
    Nothing checked it. `Codepoint::new` could not: a non-shortest form still
    yields a legal scalar value, so nothing is wrong with the result, only with
    the encoding of it, and by then the bytes are gone.
    
    Fixed as `Utf8Min` -- the shortest-form floor, carried with the sequence,
    checked at `finish`. What all four second-byte exceptions encode is one
    rule, so it is written once as that rule rather than four times as byte
    ranges. It is carried and not derived because `Pending` cannot tell "second
    byte of three" from "third byte of four" -- both have two continuations
    left. Deriving it was the first attempt and it rejected F0 90 80 80, which
    is U+10000; the test caught it.
    
    **2. Error recovery consumed a valid printable-ASCII successor.**
    § 3.9.5 names this exact input: a converter "must not consume the successor
    bytes as part of the ill-formed subsequence whenever those successor bytes
    themselves constitute part of a well-formed UTF-8 code unit subsequence", so
    `<C2 41 42>` "must not return `<U+FFFD>` or `<U+FFFD, U+0042>`". baud
    returned `<U+FFFD, U+FFFD, U+0042>`.
    
    The mechanism: the failed-decode arm re-feeds the byte to `control`, whose
    Ground arm had no printable-ASCII path -- ASCII is taken by the run scanner
    before the state machine sees it -- so the `A` fell through to "stray
    continuation byte or 0x7f: not text". Recovery already worked for ESC, for
    C0 and for a multi-byte lead; it failed for precisely the case the standard
    illustrates. Ground now prints a printable ASCII byte, an arm reached only
    on re-feed.
    
    Note what is *not* changed: the U+FFFD count. § 3.9.5 says an ill-formed
    subsequence "could be treated as a single error or as multiple errors" and
    both are conformant. Only non-consumption and non-interpretation are
    normative.
    
    New tests/encoding/ suite, separate from tests/parser/ because this is the
    bytes that *are* text. Five tests, each verified to fail before the fix,
    using the standard's own vectors.
    
    Measured, two paired runs, memory transport, medians of 9: after 1034.9
    [1031.8-1046.7] and 1045.5 [1037.9-1053.7], before 1003.2 [985.5-1007.8]
    and 1012.0 [1004.6-1018.2]. Ranges do not overlap and ghostty was stable in
    the second pair (1111.1 against 1109.1), so it is ~3% *faster* -- on a
    change that only adds work, to a path the ASCII payload never enters. Most
    likely a layout or codegen effect of the class this repo keeps hitting
    (State gained a field, and `control` is reached from the inline(always)
    ingest loop). Unconfirmed from disassembly, so recorded rather than
    explained.

