[33mcommit 2ec1157e6ac023322df2170a4b690049a84eff75[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 23:36:19 2026 -0600

    Implement S8C1T: sending and accepting C1 are different axes
    
    I refused S8C1T one commit ago on the grounds that emitting 9B would make
    baud's own output invalid UTF-8. That reasoning does not hold, and the
    mistake was conflating two independent directions.
    
    baud cannot *parse* an 8-bit C1 introducer, and that half stands: it decodes
    UTF-8, where 9B is a continuation byte and nothing else. But a reply is not
    UTF-8 text. It is a control stream the application asked for by sending
    S8C1T, and an application that asks is prepared to read it back.
    `[[source: ctlseqs]]` describes S7C1T/S8C1T purely in terms of what the
    terminal *sends*: "this tells the terminal to send C1 control characters as
    8-bit sequences, e.g., its responses to queries". There was never a reason
    baud could not emit one.
    
    C1Transmit carries the choice — an enum, not a bool, because `c1: true` does
    not say which way round it is. CSI, DCS and ST introducers all follow it, and
    the DCS case needed both ends: threading the mode into CSI and forgetting the
    terminator would send an application a reply half in each form, which is
    `s8c1t_applies_to_dcs_replies_including_the_terminator`.
    
    This is entirely off the ingest path. Replies are DA, DSR, CPR and DECRQSS;
    the mode is read when one is built and never per byte.
    
    an_unimplemented_intermediate_escape_is_counted used S8C1T as its probe for
    "recognised and dropped". It now points at ESC SP L (ANSI conformance level
    1), which really is out of scope.
    
    Done count 120 -> 121.

