[33mcommit 0f3fca1176b88a2a554333f244438b47568e71d8[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 23:24:50 2026 -0600

    Split the DCS prefix states, and ignore C0 inside control strings
    
    Auditing every rule in `[[source: williams]]` against the code rather than
    spot-checking. dcs_entry, dcs_param and dcs_intermediate were one merged
    arm — the same merge csi_entry had, carrying the same three bugs:
    
      * `3C-3F` is a private marker in dcs_entry and an error in dcs_param.
        "Occurrences of 3C to 3F in positions other than the first character of
        the parameter string cause the entire control sequence to be ignored."
        baud accepted one anywhere, so `DCS 1 ? $ q` answered a DECRQSS.
      * `30-3F` after an intermediate is malformed — dcs_intermediate sends
        them to dcs_ignore. The merged arm walked back into dcs_param and
        accepted the digit, so `DCS $ 1 q` answered too.
      * C0 in the prefix is **ignored**: "C0 controls other than CAN, SUB and
        ESC are not executed while recognising the first part of a device
        control string." baud sent them to dcs_ignore, so a stray NUL cost an
        application its DECRQSS reply.
    
    Read that last one against the CSI rule it sits next to: williams says C0
    controls "are executed immediately during the recognition of a control
    sequence" and *not* executed during a device control string. Same byte,
    opposite action, decided by which state you are in — which is the whole
    reason these are separate states rather than one arm that looks equivalent.
    
    Unlike CSI there is no ctlseqs sub-parameter form to accommodate in a DCS
    prefix, so `3A` follows williams unmodified and ends the sequence.
    
    Separately: C0 inside a control string *payload* is ignored, not stored.
    osc_string takes the ignore action on 01-17,19,1C-1F and only 20-7F is
    osc_put; dcs_passthrough is the same. baud appended every byte that was not
    a terminator, so a raw control byte landed in the title — and OSC payloads
    are attacker-supplied, which puts this in the threat model and not only in
    the conformance table.
    
    fast_esc copies an OSC payload wholesale and scans straight to the
    terminator, so it can reproduce neither rule. It now declines any payload
    containing a C0 at all, which is one comparison instead of the two the
    CAN/SUB check needed and covers both rules at once.
    
    Five tests, each verified to fail on the previous commit and pass on this
    one. Measured, paired, memory transport: before 1025.6 [1009.7-1041.4],
    after 1002.7 [985.8-1028.1] and 1026.4 [995.4-1035.4] — ranges overlap, no
    result, which is expected for cold-path work.

