[33mcommit 8b6b3c36c30a124f548add9ff7a226fe14697356[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 23:10:33 2026 -0600

    Implement williams' "anywhere" block: ESC, CAN and SUB from any state
    
    `[[source: williams]]`, on the transitions that fire regardless of state:
    
      On the VT220, VT420 and VT500, the C0 controls CAN and SUB cancel any
      escape sequence, control sequence or control string in progress and
      return to ground state.
    
    baud had none of them. Four arms wrote `if b.is_c0() { self.execute(b) }`,
    and is_c0() is `b < 0x20` — which *includes* 1B ESC, 18 CAN and 1A SUB. So
    an ESC arriving mid-sequence was executed while the parser stayed in the
    parameter string: `CSI 1 ESC [ 2 m` lost its escape, dispatched a nonsense
    `CSI 1 [`, and printed `2m` as text.
    
    Handled once, ahead of the state match, so no arm can forget it again. The
    states that decline are the ones that must: inside a control string ESC is
    the first half of ST, so the payload has to survive until the next byte
    decides between terminator and cancel. Those arms are already right.
    
    Two more holes in the same area, both found by reading the state table
    rather than the code:
    
      * csi_entry executed a C0 and then advanced to csi_param, so the marker
        rule was applied one byte early and `CSI <BS> ? J` — legal DECSED —
        was discarded. williams has csi_entry execute and *stay*. Same for DEL.
      * csi_intermediate had no C0 branch at all, so `CSI ! <NUL> p` fell to
        csi_ignore and lost its DECSTR.
    
    **This broke chunk-invariance the moment it landed, and the property caught
    it within seconds.** fast_esc scans an OSC straight to its terminator, so it
    could not see a cancel in between while the state machine could — the same
    bytes set a title or did not, depending on whether they fit inside one
    read(2). fast_esc now declines a payload containing CAN or SUB. Pinned
    deterministically by a_cancel_inside_an_osc_is_honoured_in_both_feeds
    rather than left to a saved seed.
    
    Measured, and the first version was not free. Naive — three comparisons at
    the top of `control` — cost **6%** of the streaming cells path: 1063.2
    [1036.0-1070.5] down to 995.6 [973.3-1020.3], ranges not overlapping.
    `control` is the byte-at-a-time path and multibyte text runs through it a
    byte at a time. Gating on is_c0() alone did not recover it; splitting the
    rare half into an #[inline(never)] anywhere_c0 did, which is the same
    finding open_gap already records — what costs is growing the body inlined
    into the ingest loop, not the work itself. Paired re-runs: before 1041.0
    [846.5-1059.2] / 1044.4 [999.6-1057.3], after 1037.6 / 1037.1 / 1021.1, all
    overlapping. No result, which is the goal.
    
    One behaviour in the source deliberately not implemented and now recorded as
    `out` with the reason: williams says SUB additionally displays the DEC error
    character. baud cancels and writes nothing — that is a rendering decision
    made where no renderer exists, and a glyph written into the grid on a
    control byte is indistinguishable from text the application sent.
    
    coverage.rs now also fails on `parsed` and `todo`, not just `partial` —
    vt-coverage.md says in its own words that a `parsed` row is a bug, which is
    the stronger claim and was the one nothing enforced. Done count 112 -> 117.

