[33mcommit 6cf171d0d4e0a702e4c2e0641a54916b449d0508[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 22:48:47 2026 -0600

    Give the ECMA-48 erase geometry one definition
    
    erase_display and selective_erase_display each computed "whole rows away
    from the cursor, then the cursor's own row split at the cursor" from
    scratch, and erase_line and selective_erase_line each computed the column
    span from scratch. Four copies of two rules.
    
    That is how the § 8.3.39 bug got fixed twice: ED erased whole rows,
    destroying the text to the left of the cursor, and when that was found
    DECSED had the identical defect sitting in its own copy — caught only
    because someone remembered to look.
    
    EraseExtent now carries the geometry, in baud-domain where it is pure:
    
      line_span(cx, cols)    -> ColSpan       EL, DECSEL
      full_rows(cy, rows)    -> RowSpan       ED, DECSED
      cursor_span(cx, cols)  -> Option<ColSpan>
    
    ColSpan/RowSpan are pairs rather than loose arguments because fill(r, from,
    to) and fill(r, to, from) both compile and one of them silently does
    nothing. This also retires erase_span, which returned a bare (usize,
    usize) — a raw pair of untyped indices in the middle of a typed module.
    
    One inconsistency surfaced by putting the three side by side: erase_line
    and selective_erase_display clamped the ToStart end to cols, erase_display
    did not. With a pending wrap the cursor sits at cols, so cx.after() is
    cols + 1. It was never observable, because View::fill clamps `to` again
    underneath — a downstream guard covering an upstream inconsistency, which
    is exactly why it survived and why no external test could have found it.
    The clamp is in line_span now, once, and fill's clamp goes back to
    guarding only its own bounds.
    
    Also lifted discard_row: marking a row promoted and zeroing its run count
    always happen together, and doing the first without the second leaves a
    stale span list that a later promote would replay over the blanks.
    
    Tests: selective_erase_splits_the_row_where_plain_erase_does asserts ED and
    DECSED agree for all three extents with nothing protected — DECSCA decides
    which cells are eligible, not where the boundary falls. And
    erase_to_start_with_a_pending_wrap_covers_the_whole_row pins the sentinel
    case, with a comment saying plainly that it does not prove the clamp,
    because nothing could have.
    
    Measured, same session, memory transport, medians of 9: all six baud rows
    overlap their before ranges (cells/computed streaming 1055.0 [1012.6-1069.5]
    -> 1049.7 [1028.6-1061.1]; TUI 983.7 [951.5-992.2] -> 973.5 [960.7-989.0]).
    No result, which is what a pure move should produce.

