[33mcommit ef855dc0a3bc93581e5ae191d9c8a819ceb4e5d0[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 22:42:10 2026 -0600

    Split csi_entry from csi_param: a marker past first position must be ignored
    
    `[[source: williams]]` gives csi_entry and csi_param one differing
    transition, and it is the load-bearing one: `3C-3F` is collected as a
    private marker in csi_entry and sent to csi_ignore from csi_param. A marker
    is legal only immediately after CSI.
    
    baud folded the two states, so it accepted a marker anywhere in the
    parameter string and then *executed* the result — the dangerous direction:
    
      CSI 2 ? l  malformed, must be discarded. baud ran it as DECRST 2, reset
                 DECANM and switched the terminal into VT52 mode. A protocol
                 change out of a sequence the spec says to throw away.
      CSI 1 ? J  malformed, must be discarded. baud ran it as DECSED and erased
                 the screen.
    
    `nparam.is_first()` cannot express the rule — it cannot tell "no parameter
    yet" from "not the first byte", so `CSI ? ? m` slips through it. The state
    had to come back.
    
    Two smaller holes in the same arm, found by reading the state table rather
    than the code:
    
      * C0 inside a control sequence. williams gives csi_param an execute action
        on 00-17,19,1C-1F returning to the same state. baud had no C0 branch, so
        a stray NUL fell to csi_ignore and silently cancelled an unrelated SGR.
      * `<` and `=` were dropped silently. Only `?` and `>` were recognised, so
        the other two namespaces vanished before dispatch and never reached
        Term::unsupported — a `parsed` failure. Marker now carries all four, and
        Csi::parse rejects Lt/Equals before the standard table: falling through
        would read `CSI < c` as a primary DA and answer in the wrong shape.
    
    One test asserted the old behaviour as correct — a_malformed_csi_is_consumed
    _whole fed `\x00\x01` and expected the CSI discarded, while its own comment
    described a `<` probe it did not use. Rewritten to use `<`, with the C0 rule
    pinned separately, including that "execute" means executed and not skipped.
    
    Both feeds share Marker::from_private, so the rule has one definition and
    the fast path cannot drift from the state machine.
    
    Docs: csi_entry moves partial -> done. `CSI m` also moves partial -> done —
    that one was a vocabulary error, not a gap: its only unimplemented group is
    the SGR font block, which is `out` (no renderer can consume it) rather than
    missing. No row is marked partial now, and no_row_is_marked_partial fails if
    one ever is again — reaching for `partial` means the operation is not done,
    and the fix is the operation. Done count 110 -> 112, with the three stale
    quotes updated by the check that caught them.
    
    Measured, same session, memory transport, medians of 9, ghostty-normalised:
    cells/computed streaming 0.939 -> 0.966, TUI 0.968 -> 0.963; templated
    0.938 -> 0.975 and 0.963 -> 0.967. Raw ranges overlap on three of the four
    pairs, so the honest reading is no measurable cost, not a speedup — the box
    drifted upward between runs and ghostty moved with it.
    
    Repo/Cargo/README description no longer says "built from measurements rather
    than from what reads nicely". It defined the project by what it is against,
    and the implied contrast — that readable code was the rejected alternative —
    is both untrue here and a dig at every other implementation.

