[33mcommit 74c0ceb36a0c5f6a75755550960ce12e03e2d0c4[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 22:21:22 2026 -0600

    Add Csi::level(), profile docs, and a test that checks the doc's tiers
    
    Answers "should the Csi enum split per protocol?" — no. Dispatch keys on
    (marker, intermediate, final byte), which does not partition by level:
    `CSI J` and `CSI ? J` share a final byte across VT100 and VT220, and
    `CSI s` is SCOSC or DECSLRM depending on DECLRMM at runtime. Splitting the
    enum would make the parser choose a protocol before it knows which
    operation it has.
    
    So the level is data, not structure: `Csi::level()`. That makes it
    checkable, which is the part that was missing — docs/vt-coverage.md sorts
    rows under Tier 1/2/3 headings and nothing kept those headings honest.
    tests/conformance/levels.rs now cross-checks every parseable row's tier
    against the code's level, and fails on a mismatch rather than letting
    whoever notices decide which one was right.
    
    Also: docs/protocols/profiles/ (10 terminfo profiles, one per fixture) and
    an architecture.md section on why the three layers file differently —
    domain by sequence family, core by responsibility, tests and docs by
    protocol level.

