      xterm  osc, mouse, modes          tests/xterm/    docs/protocols/xterm.md
    
    Each says what the level is, what baud implements, what it declines and
    why, and which primary sources the claims rest on. They deliberately do
    not repeat `vt-coverage.md`'s per-operation table — they carry the parts
    that are easy to get subtly wrong, which a status table cannot express:
    that ED splits at the cursor, that LF is index and not newline, that
    `CSI s` is ambiguous and the parser is told rather than guessing, that
    the alternate screen must not take the ring-advance fast path.
    
    `docs/protocols/README.md` also states the thing that makes the whole
    question answerable: **a `$TERM` is a capability profile, not a
    protocol.** `xterm-256color` is not a different protocol from `xterm`,
    so there is no implementation per name and there should not be — there
    is one parser, and a profile is a question asked of it. The table of all
    ten profiles and their capability counts lives there, next to the note
    that input capabilities (88 of them for xterm) are counted separately
    because baud has no input path.

