[33mcommit 43f91d49bd2ce2fc41d1e2d6d5901bfb62a461c7[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 22:01:10 2026 -0600

    feat: VT52, nine private modes, and a real ED bug the literature exposed
    
    All ten `$TERM` profiles in the terminfo database now pass conformance:
    ansi, dtterm, nsterm, rxvt, vt52, vt100, vt102, xterm, xterm-16color,
    xterm-256color.
    
    **A `$TERM` is a capability profile, not a protocol**, so there is no
    implementation file per name — there is one implementation, and
    `tests/conformance/` asks per profile whether it covers what the profile
    promises. Method: replay every *output* capability from the ncurses
    entry and require `Term::unsupported` to stay zero. Input capabilities
    (`k*`) are counted separately, not skipped: xterm declares 88 of them and
    baud has no input path.
    
    `Term::unsupported` is new and is the point of the exercise. It counts
    sequences the parser recognised and did not act on — the `parsed` failure
    that `docs/vt-coverage.md` calls worse than not implementing something,
    made observable for the first time. Removing the `_ => {}` arm from the
    CSI dispatch means an unhandled new variant is now a compile error.
    
    **Three real bugs, all found by reading specifications:**
    
    * **ED erased whole rows.** `[[source: ecma48]]` 8.3.39: `Ps = 0` erases
      "the active presentation position and the character positions up to the
      end of the page". baud erased the cursor's entire row, so `CSI 0 J`
      mid-line destroyed the text to the *left* of the cursor. Every existing
      ED test placed the cursor at column 0, where the two are identical —
      which is why a full VT suite missed it. Found while implementing VT52's
      `ESC J`, the same operation. DECSED had it too.
    * **Multi-parameter DECSET/DECRST applied only the first.** `CSI ?7;25l`
      reset autowrap and left the cursor visible; rxvt's own reset string is
      `CSI ?1;3;4;6l`.
    * **`ESC =`/`ESC >`** were consumed by the fast path and acted on by
      nobody — fixed in the same pass as the modes.
    
    **VT52 is a mode, not a subset**, and the sources say so: xterm's "VT52
    Mode" table has no CSI in it at all. `ESC D` is IND in ANSI and
    cursor-left in VT52; `ESC H` is HTS and home. So it gets its own module,
    its own `Vt52` type, and its own ground state. The only exits are
    `ESC <` and a host reset — `reset(1)` sending `ESC c` does not help,
    because `ESC c` is not a VT52 sequence either. That is the hardware's
    behaviour, not an omission.
    
    `ESC Y` resolved a source conflict: the VT510 manual says "Move cursor to
    column Pn", one operand; ctlseqs and terminfo both say two, row then
    column, 0-based, +32. baud follows ctlseqs and records the disagreement.
    
    Nine private modes added: DECCKM, DECANM, DECCOLM, DECSCLM, DECSCNM,
    DECARM, cursor blink, reverse wrap, legacy `?47` alt screen, meta. Those
    that are input-side or renderer-side are exposed by accessor, the
    established pattern. `ModeBit` now uses 15 of its 16 bits, so there is a
    `const` assertion that a 16th does not silently shift off the end.
    
    Tests move into per-protocol directories — `tests/vt100/`, `tests/vt220/`,
    `tests/vt420/`, `tests/vt52/`, `tests/xterm/`, `tests/parser/`,
    `tests/sgr/`, `tests/conformance/` — so a test's protocol is visible from
    its path. `coverage.rs` had to learn to recurse, and failed loudly when
    it could not, which is the check working.
    
    **Measured, and not fully explained.** Normalising against ghostty in-run
    (the box was thermally drifting; ghostty fell 1097 -> 1040 across runs):
    
      baseline          0.970  [0.968, 0.972, 0.970]
      this change       0.930  [0.926-0.952 across five runs]
    
    That is a real ~3-4% cost on the cells path, and I did not isolate it.
    Ruled out by measurement, not by argument: it is **not** the VT52 check
    in the byte loop (removing it entirely did not recover the numbers, and
    neither did making VT52 a ground state so the check costs nothing), and
    **not** the multi-mode loop being inlined into the CSI dispatch (moving
    it behind `#[inline(never)]` did not recover it either). Remaining
    suspects are the `Term` layout change, the new `State` variants, and the
    larger `erase_display`. Bisecting it is the next perf task; it is
    recorded here rather than left for someone to rediscover.

