    refactor(bench): split the two harness binaries by responsibility
    
    `baud-bench/src/main.rs` was 370 lines and
    `bench-others/multibench/src/main.rs` was 433, each mixing transport,
    per-core adapters, statistics and the driver.
    
      baud-bench:  main / harness (Tr, run_once, feed_dispatch) / drive / payload
      multibench:  main / transport / cores (the per-core adapters) / ffi / report
    
    Largest file is now 168 lines.
    
    `audit_all.sh` gains a second aggregate. `baud-bench`'s 12 permitted
    raw-integer sites were pinned to `main.rs`; they now live across
    `main.rs`, `harness.rs` and `drive.rs`, so the budget is a crate-level 14
    with a per-file breakdown printed. Per-file limits would have to be
    re-guessed every time a function moved between modules — which is how a
    gate stops being a gate.
    
    Measured after the split, and this needs reading carefully: every figure
    is ~4% below the previous run, **including ghostty** (1064.2 vs 1112.5).
    That is machine drift, not the split — nothing here touches the parser,
    only how the harness is filed. The ratios are what to look at, and they
    do not move:
    
      baud spans / ghostty   1.48  (was 1.51)
      baud cells  / ghostty  0.98  (was 0.98)
    
    Recording it this way rather than quoting the lower absolute numbers as
    a regression, or the ratios as an improvement.

