[33mcommit dca45c9fb1a6365bea3130913c04dd9fc666e36a[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 19:26:57 2026 -0600

    refactor: five bools on the protocol API surface become named enums
    
    Answering "are the protocols properly typed, no primitives?" honestly
    meant checking rather than asserting, and the answer was *nearly*.
    
    `baud-domain` is exempt from `audit_all.sh` for a good reason -- a
    newtype's whole job is to wrap a primitive, so counting them there
    measures nothing. But that exemption had a hole: it also hid primitives
    on the crate's **API surface**. Five `bool` fields had accumulated inside
    protocol types, each of them precisely the failure `Marker`'s own
    doc-comment argues against ("a `bool` argument at a call site says
    nothing; this says which namespace"):
    
    * `Csi::Mode { set: bool }` and `Csi::PrivateMode { set: bool }`
      -> `ModeChange { Set, Reset }`
    * `Csi::SelectProtection { protect: bool }` -> `Protection { Erasable,
      Protected }`, which also stops the wire and the state being blurred:
      DECSCA has three parameter values for two states, since 0 and 2 both
      mean erasable.
    * `Csi::parse_with(.., side_margins: bool)` -> `MarginMode { Off, On }`.
      This one was the most dangerous: the same call already passes another
      bool (`inter.is_none()`), and nothing would have caught them swapped.
    * `Report::MouseSgr { cb: u16, release: bool }` -> `Report::MouseSgr
      (MouseEvent)`. `cb` was a pre-encoded wire value being carried through
      the type system as an integer; it is now computed inside `encode`,
      where it belongs -- the only place that should know how to build a wire
      encoding is the place that writes it.
    
    The gate is extended so this cannot come back: `audit_all.sh` now
    requires **zero** `bool` fields in baud-domain. That limit needs no
    judgement call, because a `bool` field is never a newtype internal -- a
    newtype over bool is `struct X(bool)`, which the pattern does not match.
    Verified by reintroducing one and watching it fail.
    
    What remains primitive in baud-domain after this is five private
    newtype internals -- `Params.sub`, `ReportBytes.len`, `Split.sep`,
    `AtlasSlot.v` -- plus zerocopy's explicit `StyleWord._pad`. All private,
    all the payload of the type that wraps them.

