[33mcommit 3cd8604abd3bbdd1d2b30919d1f0947b8ee21b4f[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 18:58:46 2026 -0600

    feat: DECKPAM/DECKPNM and XTVERSION, and the `>` CSI namespace
    
    `ESC =` and `ESC >` were a live `parsed` bug: both were in the fast
    path's list of known two-byte escapes, were consumed, and then did
    nothing. Recognised, accepted, dropped, with no way for the application
    to find out — the exact state `docs/vt-coverage.md` is built to catch,
    sitting in the code while the table claimed nothing about it.
    
    The keypad mode is now recorded as `KeypadMode { Numeric, Application }`
    and read with `Term::keypad()`. Input-side, so exposing it is how the
    core acts on it: the keypad sends different sequences in each mode and an
    embedder has no other way to know which.
    
    XTVERSION (`CSI > 0 q`) answers, which lets an application feature-detect
    instead of guessing from `$TERM` — and guessing from `$TERM` is how a
    terminal ends up being sent sequences it does not implement.
    
    It also needed the `>` marker, which is a third CSI namespace alongside
    `?` and unprefixed: `CSI > 0 q` and `CSI ? 0 q` are unrelated. Added to
    both feeds, since a marker handled in only one is the silent divergence
    this file keeps having.
    
    XTVERSION is the one report baud sends that is **not** a CSI —
    `[[source: ctlseqs]]` gives the response as `DCS > | text ST`. That is
    the case for the reply side being a typed `Report` rather than byte
    literals: the shape comes from the variant, so the one odd-shaped answer
    cannot accidentally be written in the shape of the others.
    
    `keypad_application: bool` became the `KeypadMode` enum rather than
    raising the type audit's budget, which it had pushed to 16.

