[33mcommit 750e189851a5411c203f1aeb7d2b5d6f826a5628[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 18:55:01 2026 -0600

    feat: mouse reporting — tracking modes and the SGR encoding
    
    `?1000`, `?1002` and `?1003` select which events an application hears
    about; `?1006` selects the encoding. `Term::mouse_report` takes an event
    and returns whether it was queued, so an embedder can tell "the
    application does not want this" from "it was sent".
    
    **SGR is the only encoding, and that is correctness rather than
    laziness.** The X10 scheme puts each ordinate in one byte at
    `value + 32`, capping it at 223; baud's grid goes to 2048 columns. A
    report from column 400 in that scheme is not a truncated answer — it
    names a different cell and the application acts on it. So without
    `?1006` baud reports nothing, and counts the drop in
    `mouse_unencodable`: an application that enables tracking and hears
    silence should be diagnosable from outside rather than left guessing.
    This is the wrong-shaped-answer rule applied to a report the terminal
    sends rather than one it is asked for.
    
    Encoding follows `[[source: ctlseqs]]` exactly and each rule is tested
    against the quoted text: button bits 0/1/2, release as a lowercase `m`
    ("to resolve the X10 ambiguity regarding which button was released"),
    +32 for motion, +64 for wheel buttons, and modifiers at 4/8/16. The two
    32s are different — the motion indicator stays in SGR while the X10
    printability offset does not — which is the easy one to conflate.
    
    The tracking modes are a level, not three flags: `?1003` is a superset of
    `?1002` is a superset of `?1000`, so DECRQM answers "set" only for the
    one in force. Claiming all three would tell an application it is getting
    motion events it is not.
    
    `?1001` highlight tracking is deliberately out of scope:
    `[[source: ctlseqs]]` warns "this mode requires a cooperating program,
    else xterm will hang", and a terminal that can be hung by a sequence is
    worse than one that ignores it.
    
    Events take `Col`/`Row`, not integers, so an event cannot be built from a
    pixel position by accident — that division belongs to the embedder and is
    where an off-by-one would otherwise hide.
    
    One existing test moved: `?1000` was the example of an unimplemented mode
    in the DECRQM suite and now answers "reset". It became `?1001`, which is
    the point of that answer being tracked rather than constant.
    
    Measured same-session; every range overlaps, so no regression:
    
      cells, computed  before 1097.4 [1091.5-1100.5]  after 1074.1 [1013.8-1096.0]
      cells TUI        before  965.9 [ 949.6- 984.2]  after  955.5 [ 913.6- 997.2]
      spans, streaming before 1692.8 [1251.7-1716.7]  after 1642.0 [1059.2-1670.6]

