[33mcommit 80fe4d82c507dd36aaaf26ea5b06a32c4a4e902b[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 18:47:51 2026 -0600

    feat: character set designation and the SO/SI locking shifts
    
    `ESC ( ) * + C` designate G0-G3, SO invokes G1 into GL and SI invokes
    G0, per `[[source: ctlseqs]]`. Designation and invocation are kept
    distinct, which is the part that is easy to get wrong: `ESC ) 0` alone
    must change nothing, because G1 is not in GL until SO puts it there.
    
    **The glyph table is deliberately not implemented, and that is a
    sourcing decision rather than a shortcut.** ctlseqs names the set --
    "C = 0 -> DEC Special Character and Line Drawing Set, VT100" -- but
    gives no byte-to-glyph mapping, and MANIFEST.toml already records
    `dec-special-graphics` as `gated`: vt100.net publishes the table as a
    figure, so no claim may rest on it. Writing it from memory is exactly
    what "Do not implement a control function from memory" forbids.
    
    So a byte written under the line-drawing set reaches the cell **as the
    byte the application sent, flagged** (`CellFlags::GRAPHICS`, in bits the
    cell already had). That loses nothing recoverable: a renderer holding
    the table has the byte and the fact that it is a graphics byte, which is
    everything it needs. Translating to a guessed codepoint would be
    unrecoverable, and would be the `parsed` failure in its worst form --
    the terminal would look like it worked. When a citable table turns up,
    the mapping is a renderer change and these tests do not move.
    
    Sets baud does not distinguish are recorded as `Charset::Other` rather
    than folded into ASCII, so designating DEC Supplemental is not mistaken
    for a no-op.
    
    The charset is folded into `flags` when it changes, not per cell, so the
    write path still reads one value -- the same reason the AutoWrap check
    is hoisted out of `put_run`.
    
    Measured same-session; ghostty stable at 1123.1 / 1119.3:
    
      cells, computed  before 1050.7 [1040.1-1065.6]  after 1087.1 [1076.2-1092.1]
      cells TUI        before  962.3 [ 940.3- 980.2]  after  967.6 [ 940.6- 988.6]
      spans, streaming before 1700.1 [1029.3-1720.6]  after 1676.7 [1181.7-1696.8]
    
    No regression. Streaming cells measured *faster* with non-overlapping
    ranges, which is not a claim about this change -- nothing here touches
    that loop, so it is code layout, and it is recorded rather than taken
    credit for.

