[33mcommit 3e923cc3b4d6ce629af98f6478c7f5ca790fa7a0[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 18:29:29 2026 -0600

    feat: alternate screen buffer (CSI ? 1049)
    
    Tier A #1 in the roadmap: every full-screen program -- vim, less, htop,
    tmux -- switches on start and back on exit, and without it quitting vim
    does not restore the shell's scrollback. To a user that reads as lost
    session history rather than as a missing feature.
    
    A second full grid in `Storage`, switched by `?1049`, with the cursor
    saved on the way in and restored on the way out per
    `[[source: ctlseqs]]`. Two things keep it cheap. It has no scrollback --
    no ring, no run table, no promotion -- so `OpMode::Runs` materialises
    directly there rather than building spans it could never scroll back
    into. And the O(1) ring-advance fast path in `scroll_region_up` is
    deliberately *not* taken on it: that path retires a line by moving the
    scrollback window, so using it on the alt screen would scroll the
    primary screen's history from under it.
    
    Two edges that are easy to get wrong and are tested: entering twice must
    not re-save the cursor, or an application that sets the mode twice is
    restored to a position from inside the alt screen; and the switch keeps
    its own saved cursor rather than sharing DECSC's, so a program that does
    both gets each back.
    
    `the_alt_screen_never_leaks_into_the_primary` runs an arbitrary
    generated program, snapshots the primary screen, runs a second arbitrary
    program entirely inside a switch, and requires the primary back
    byte-identical. Passes at PROPTEST_CASES=20000. Writing it took two
    tries -- the first two counterexamples were the generator escaping the
    alt screen on its own, not the code.
    
    `alt: bool` became `Screen { Normal, Alternate }` and the two grid sizes
    became a named `GRID`, which took the type audit from 15 to 14 rather
    than needing its budget raised.
    
    Measured same-session against the previous commit, medians of 9:
    
      cells, computed  before 1143.1 [1125.5-1154.4]  after 1132.9 [1099.4-1143.9]
      cells TUI        before 1047.5 [1024.4-1064.4]  after 1024.9 [ 970.0-1044.4]
      spans, streaming before 1735.7 [1092.2-1753.9]  after 1764.7 [1199.1-1786.5]
    
    Every range overlaps, so there is no result -- no regression.
    
    One correction to my own working: an earlier cross-session comparison
    looked like a 4% TUI regression and a 7% win from moving `alt` to the
    end of `Storage`. Neither survived a same-session A/B, and the "~7%"
    had already been written into a source comment. It is now removed: the
    field ordering is the reasoned default for keeping hot structures
    adjacent, flagged as unmeasured rather than quoted as a measurement.

