[33mcommit 9b2ad189a421d4bc0a66fd7d8a413eb2f727abd7[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 18:12:18 2026 -0600

    feat: SGR sub-parameters, DECSCUSR, DECRQM, bracketed paste, synchronised output
    
    Four Tier 2/3 rows and the one the roadmap singled out as "the one
    implementations get wrong".
    
    **The ISO 8613-6 colon form of SGR colour.** `[[source: ctlseqs]]` gives
    two spellings of direct colour and they are not the same shape:
    `38 : 2 : Pi : Pr : Pg : Pb` carries a colour-space identifier that
    `38 ; 2 ; Pr ; Pg ; Pb` does not. Flattening colons into semicolons
    reads `Pi` as red and shifts every channel by one. The parser now
    records which separator introduced each parameter -- a bitmask beside
    `params`, in both the fast path and the byte-at-a-time machine, since a
    separator handled in only one feed is the silent-divergence bug this
    codebase has had before -- and the colon group is consumed as a single
    attribute. The five-part `38:2:r:g:b` spelling some emitters produce is
    accepted too, decided by group length rather than by the separator
    alone.
    
    **DECRQM**, `CSI Ps $ p`, answered with DECRPM. The interesting answer
    is the negative one: `[[source: ctlseqs]]` gives `0` as "not
    recognized", and baud returns it for every mode it does not implement.
    That is correct rather than a fallback -- an application uses DECRQM to
    discover whether a mode exists, so replying `2` ("reset") would tell it
    the mode is available and off, and it would set the mode and believe it
    took effect. The reply carries the `?` marker because `CSI 4` is IRM and
    `CSI ? 4` is not.
    
    **DECSCUSR**, `CSI Ps SP q`. Shares a final byte with DECSCA and is told
    apart only by the intermediate, which is now tested in both directions.
    
    **Bracketed paste (?2004) and synchronised output (?2026)** are recorded
    and exposed as `Term::bracketed_paste()` / `Term::synchronized_output()`.
    The core has no input path and no renderer, so exposing them *is* acting
    on them -- an embedder has no other channel through which to honour
    either. Bracketed paste is a security mode: without it a pasted newline
    reaches the shell as typed input and runs.
    
    The generated-program property suite covers all four, and
    `replies_are_well_formed` grew to allow intermediate bytes, since DECRPM
    legitimately ends `$ y`. Both property suites pass at
    PROPTEST_CASES=20000.

