[33mcommit 8c7a6478c6059b51548545a4a8b7ed74d7910a0d[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 17:16:11 2026 -0600

    feat: selective erase (DECSCA/DECSED/DECSEL), and properties over real sequences
    
    Three sequences that only make sense together: DECSCA marks characters
    unerasable, and DECSED/DECSEL are the erases that honour that mark. The
    erases without the protection are silent aliases for ED and EL, which is
    the `parsed` failure `docs/vt-coverage.md` exists to prevent.
    
    Protection is a separate field of `StyleWord`, not an `Attrs` bit.
    `[[source: vt510-decsca]]`: "DECSCA does not effect visual character
    attributes set by the select graphic rendition (SGR) function" -- so it
    must not sit where a renderer reading attributes could mistake it for
    one. It is part of the interning key, so protected and unprotected text
    are distinct renditions and the flag rides along in the template table
    without widening the 8-byte `PackedCell`.
    
    The erase blank drops the protection (`Term::blank`). Otherwise the
    first DECSEL under `DECSCA 1` protects every cell it just cleared, and
    the line becomes permanently unerasable -- pinned by a unit test that
    compares the blanks' rendition and by
    `a_cleared_screen_is_fully_erasable`, both of which fail without it.
    
    Adds `vt_properties.rs`: property tests over *generated VT programs*
    rather than over `any::<u8>()`. Uniform random bytes essentially never
    spell a well-formed CSI, so everything added for Tier 0-2 was outside
    the reach of a property. The new generator emits valid sequences, which
    puts chunk invariance, the AVX2/portable equivalence, cursor bounds and
    reply well-formedness on the parser doing real work.
    
    Sources fetched and hashed before implementing, per the standing rule:
    vt510-decsca, vt510-decsed, vt510-decsel.

