[33mcommit 8ddc05ec456cb1676d78a769f79f1898572a9d3a[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 16:40:01 2026 -0600

    fix: the parser state machine, and a pre-existing string-terminator bug
    
    Tier 0 from docs/vt-coverage.md. Four states were missing and each absence was
    observable from the stream:
    
    - sos_pm_apc_string. DCS/SOS/PM/APC fell through to Ground, so a control
      string's payload was executed as commands. kitty's graphics protocol is
      APC G, so this was reachable from ordinary image output, not only from a
      hostile stream. Payloads are consumed now.
    - csi_ignore. A malformed CSI returned to Ground mid-sequence and printed the
      remainder as text.
    - escape_intermediate. `ESC # 8` dropped the `#` and printed the `8`.
    - csi_intermediate. The intermediate was discarded, so `CSI ! p` was
      indistinguishable from `CSI p`. It is recorded now, in BOTH feed paths --
      dropping it in fast_csi would have made the AVX2 loop dispatch DECSTR as
      `CSI p` while the portable loop dispatched DECSTR, which is the silent
      divergence this codebase has had before.
    
    DECSTR lands with it, since it is what makes the intermediate observable: a
    bit set and never read is the same failure as a sequence parsed and dropped.
    
    C0 controls now execute inside escape and CSI sequences rather than ending
    them, per williams' `execute` action.
    
    And a PRE-EXISTING bug, found by ingest_is_chunk_invariant while doing the
    above and confirmed to fail on the parent commit too. fast_esc scanned for a
    string terminator and, on finding ESC, consumed the following byte without
    checking it was the backslash -- its own comment said "ESC needs the following
    byte to confirm ESC-backslash" and the code did not. So `OSC ... ESC E`
    swallowed the E and the NEL never ran, while the byte-at-a-time path cancelled
    the string and executed it: whether a stream got its line break depended on
    where the read boundary fell. ctlseqs line 201 defines ST as `ESC \` and
    nothing else.
    
    Two things learned while minimising, both recorded in comments rather than
    left as traps:
    
    - Term::new on a REUSED Storage does not reset nrun/promoted, so a harness
      that reuses one manufactures false divergences. My first minimiser did.
    - In Runs mode viewport() reports representation, not rendered content: it
      reflects only promoted lines, and MAXRUN is 8, so 112 single-byte reads
      promote a line that one read does not. Chunk-invariance assertions over
      rendered content belong in Computed mode.
    
    26 tests added across vt_parser_states.rs and vt_c0.rs. The regression case is
    checked into .proptest-regressions AND pinned by a deterministic test, so it
    does not depend on proptest replaying a saved seed.

