[33mcommit 520884f76b07d9f2b627936fc2b392f01833fd02[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 16:08:48 2026 -0600

    feat: scrolling regions, and the vertical movement operations done properly
    
    DECSTBM, DL and SM/RM were parsed and dropped; SU and SD did not exist. They
    are one feature rather than five -- each either respects the scrolling region
    or corrupts the screen -- so they land together with the operations that share
    the region: IND, RI, NEL, LF and IL.
    
    Two semantic bugs found on the way, both of which an application can see:
    
    - IND (ESC D) reset the column, making it behave as NEL. ctlseqs defines IND
      as index only.
    - LF reset the column unconditionally, i.e. LNM (mode 20) permanently on, when
      ctlseqs lists it as reset by default. LNM is now real state and decides it.
    
    RI was keyed on row 0 rather than the region top, so ESC M inside a region
    scrolled the whole screen. IL shifted to the bottom of the screen rather than
    the region, moving rows DECSTBM had placed outside it.
    
    The O(1) ring-advance fast path is preserved and is now explicitly the
    full-screen case: a region smaller than the screen cannot use it, because rows
    outside the region must not move, so it pays for the copy. Streaming ingest --
    which is the full-screen case -- is unaffected.
    
    Modes are a bitset newtype, not a struct of bools. A bool per mode would be an
    untyped flag each, and `self.lnm = self.decom` would compile. Only modes baud
    acts on get a bit: a bit that is set and never read is the same "accepted, did
    nothing" failure the coverage matrix calls `parsed`.
    
    delete_lines_in computed `bot - n` on usize, which underflows when the whole
    region is deleted from row 0. Half-open range now, with a test.
    
    17 tests in baud-core/tests/vt_scrolling.rs, run against all three
    representations. They discriminate: reintroducing the IND column reset makes
    ind_preserves_the_column and lf_does_not_return_to_column_one_by_default fail,
    verified by mutation rather than assumed.
    
    MEASUREMENT DISCONTINUITY. baud-bench and perf_floor emitted bare LF, and
    their PTY is raw so cfmakeraw has disabled ONLCR -- nothing translated it.
    With LF correctly not implying CR those payloads would march rightwards and
    wrap, measuring something else, so both now emit CRLF. baud-bench numbers
    before and after this commit are not comparable. multibench already used CRLF,
    so every figure in MEASUREMENTS.md Host B and Host C is unaffected.

