[33mcommit f1bb00509372c646ebb082c33f8af95d0b56df22[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 15:59:53 2026 -0600

    docs: enumerate the VT surface and define what "the whole protocol" means
    
    "Implement the VT protocol" has no truth value until the surface is
    enumerated. ctlseqs.txt documents 117 CSI sequences alone, many of them xterm
    extensions with no second implementation, so docs/vt-coverage.md states tiers:
    what baud commits to, what it does not, and why.
    
    Three primary sources retrieved and hash-pinned: xterm's ctlseqs, Paul Flo
    Williams' DEC ANSI parser, and ECMA-48 5th edition.
    
    The inventory is worse than the README's "~22 operations" suggested, in a
    specific way. Three sequences are *parsed and then dropped* -- DL (CSI M),
    DECSTBM (CSI r), and DECSET/DECRST (CSI ? h/l) all reach `dispatch` and fall
    into `_ => {}`. That is worse than missing: the terminal accepts the sequence,
    does nothing, and gives the application no way to find out. The matrix has a
    `parsed` status specifically so this class cannot hide again.
    
    Two structural gaps block whole tiers and are not any single sequence:
    
    - No reply channel. DA, DSR, DECRQM and cursor reports must write back to the
      PTY, and Term cannot emit bytes at all. An application that waits for a reply
      hangs rather than degrades. This is larger than any operation count.
    - No tab stop table. HT, HTS, TBC, CHT and CBT are one feature, and the
      differential test already names tab stops among its failures.
    
    And Tier 0, the substrate: Williams specifies 13 parser states; baud has 5.
    Missing sos_pm_apc_string is a correctness gap rather than a feature gap -- an
    APC payload is currently interpreted as commands instead of being consumed.
    
    check-sources.sh now scans all of docs/ rather than only research/notes/, or
    this file's own citations would have gone unverified.

