[33mcommit 28b7727b133091b8d631285e0d78a9cebaf250de[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 15:52:30 2026 -0600

    docs: record Host C, x86-64 with AVX2 and a real n_tty
    
    The first trustworthy x86-64 measurement this project has. Neither existing
    host could produce one: the Xeon numbers predate most of the current code, and
    the Mac can only reach x86 through Rosetta, which translates AVX2 into NEON --
    its perf floors fail there while passing on this runner, which is the
    demonstration that those timings were measuring the translator.
    
    From memory, no range overlaps on either payload:
    
      baud vs        streaming   TUI
      ghostty          2.09x    2.69x
      alacritty       14.2x    11.9x
      vt100           15.9x    17.0x
      termwiz         18.8x    23.9x
      wezterm          152x     100x
    
    Wider than Host B's 1.55x/2.14x, which is what a SIMD scanner should buy:
    AVX2 here, scalar there. AVX2 is not inferred -- multibench calls feed_avx2
    unconditionally on x86-64 with no runtime detection, so a runner without it
    would fault rather than measure.
    
    And the honest half: through a real PTY on the same host, baud does NOT beat
    ghostty. Spans fall 2023 -> 202, -90%, and every fast core lands in a 200-240
    MB/s band that is the line discipline rather than the parser. baud's cell
    modes tie ghostty there; spans come last among the fast cores.
    
    That is the argument this file has always made, now measured on both sides:
    read(2) is capped at N_TTY_BUF_SIZE - 1, so a core fast enough to saturate the
    tty is measured as the tty. The corollary is uncomfortable and belongs in the
    record: on today's Linux tty, baud's ingest advantage is not reachable by a
    real terminal without changing the transport.

