    perf: stop erase_line filling a row it already truncated
    
    CSI K fires once per ~77 input bytes on the TUI payload, against a 200-column
    grid. erase_line set nrun to ZERO for an unpromoted line in Runs mode -- the
    O(1) truncation that *is* the erase, since the viewport is a projection of the
    spans -- and then promoted the line and filled [cx, cols) regardless. The cheap
    path was computed and thrown away.
    
    That fill was ~134 cell writes per 77 input bytes: write amplification of
    roughly 20:1 against rule #1's <= 1, to blank a row that had already been
    truncated.
    
    From memory, two runs, TUI: 1048.6 -> 2276.0 MB/s, 2.17x. Against ghostty that
    turns 1.03x -- overlapping, no result -- into 2.14x with no overlap. baud now
    leads every core on both payloads:
    
                  streaming   TUI
      ghostty        1.55x    2.14x
      termwiz        4.9x     6.6x
      alacritty      7.7x     8.3x
      vt100         10.3x    10.3x
      wezterm         98x      70x
    
    and does it on the scalar path, against a ghostty that vectorises.
    
    Guarded by known_bugs.rs::erase_to_end_preserves_text_left_of_cursor, which
    asserts in all three modes that CSI K keeps [0, cx). That test was written
    before trusting the change and it earned its place immediately: zeroing nrun
    drops every span on the line, including those before the cursor, and
    chunk-invariance could not have caught it because it compares baud against
    baud. Its first draft used CSI G, which baud does not implement -- so the
    cursor never moved and the test asserted nothing. CUP now.

