[33mcommit 9c491a7823aa3cef76de02eeac7b15fb78794451[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 15:30:54 2026 -0600

    feat: measure every core from memory, and find the PTY was the whole table
    
    MEASUREMENTS.md has recorded the tty tax for alacritty since the beginning and
    never for baud. Measuring it explains every through-a-PTY table in this file:
    on Darwin the transport caps the pipeline near 200 MB/s, so any core fast
    enough to reach that cap measures as "200 MB/s" regardless of how much faster
    it is. The field looked compressed because it was being measured through a
    channel none of the fast cores could saturate.
    
    From memory, over three runs, medians:
    
      baud      1648.8  1689.9  1699.2      ghostty  1103.9  1122.5  1121.1
      ratio       1.49x   1.51x   1.52x
    
    and no range overlaps against any core: 1.52x ghostty, 4.5x termwiz, 7.9x
    alacritty, 10.1x vt100, 95x wezterm. baud is running its scalar path here --
    there is no NEON scanner -- against a ghostty that vectorises.
    
    The transport cost is a gradient, and that is the finding: wezterm -10% (it is
    compute-bound and barely notices), alacritty -33% (close to the -30% already
    recorded for it on Linux), ghostty -81%, baud -89%.
    
    TUI is explicitly not a result: 1076.1 [929.4-1084.8] against ghostty's
    1047.7 [1033.2-1052.7], overlapping in all three runs with baud's lower bound
    beneath ghostty's median. Recorded as the open half rather than rounded up.
    
    The header now names its transport. A table that said "through a real PTY"
    while running from memory is the exact failure this file exists to prevent.

