[33mcommit d668a1f3ea60472fe110ccb49b8cdfc67ea5dd6f[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 15:23:50 2026 -0600

    docs: start the research corpus, after zebu's convention
    
    docs/research/{notes,sources} with a MANIFEST.toml recording url, sha256 and
    bytes for every source, and scripts/check-sources.sh as a devenv task so a
    note cannot cite a key the manifest does not carry, or one recorded as gated
    and therefore never read. A design claim citing a paper nobody fetched is
    worse than one with no citation, because it looks checked.
    
    Two sources retrieved and read in full, not summarised from abstracts:
    
    - Langdale & Lemire, "Parsing Gigabytes of JSON per Second", VLDB J 2019.
    - Mytkowicz, Musuvathi & Schulte, "Data-Parallel Finite-State Machines",
      ASPLOS '14.
    
    The second is the one that matters for the goal. Every competitor vectorises
    *scanning* -- finding the next interesting byte -- and ghostty is measurably
    good at it. Nobody vectorises the *state machine*, and a VT parser is a state
    machine. The paper breaks the cross-iteration dependence by enumerating
    transitions from all possible start states at once, at one or two shuffle ops
    per symbol, for up to 3x over an optimised sequential baseline on a single
    core. baud's State set is small enough to fit one lane per state in a single
    NEON or AVX2 register, and a 16-entry table lookup is what tbl/pshufb do
    natively.
    
    The note records what is not established as loudly as what is: the 3x is
    against their own sequential baseline, and baud already skips printable runs
    without touching the state machine, so the fraction of baud's time that is
    state machine has never been measured. That measurement decides whether this
    is worth doing, and it comes first.

