[33mcommit 4d4d2c59d9030cffcd2b8248119ecd926413d346[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 14:59:46 2026 -0600

    feat: portable SIMD scans, and the fast paths on every ISA
    
    fast_csi and fast_esc were reachable only from feed_avx2, and all three were
    #[cfg(target_arch = "x86_64")]. On aarch64 every escape therefore went through
    the byte-at-a-time state machine: the 85.2% -> 99.8% fast-path win was x86
    only, and baud on ARM was running the pre-optimisation parser.
    
    The three scans are the only ISA-dependent code in the parser, so they are now
    a trait with two backends. Portable uses core::simd, which lowers to NEON on
    aarch64 and SSE2 on baseline x86-64; Avx2 keeps the 32-byte path. Every fast
    path and the ingest loop itself are written once against that trait and
    monomorphised, so feed and feed_avx2 cannot diverge in logic -- which is the
    bug this codebase has already had, when ESC 7/8/M were implemented in the AVX2
    loop only and chunk-invariance could not see it because it drives one path.
    
    Two things fall out of the design rather than being added:
    
    - Portable's run scan tests `< 0x20 || > 0x7e`, two comparisons. The AVX2 path
      needs three because it tests `>= 0x80` separately, and once omitted the
      `== 0x7f` term -- the vector body then accepted DEL into a printable run
      while its own scalar tail rejected it.
    - ingest_is_chunk_invariant now covers the vector body against the scalar tail
      on every target, for free: LANES is 16, so a chunk of 1 can only take the
      tail while a chunk of 512 takes the body, and it ranges over both.
    
    Also pins DEL handling in a test. baud renders 0x7f as U+FFFD in a cell;
    ECMA-48 says ignore it and alacritty drops it, so this is a candidate for one
    of the seven cases the differential still fails. Recorded as an open question,
    not asserted as correct -- deciding it needs a differential run.
    
    The audit limit moves 12 -> 15: one `trailing_zeros() as usize` per portable
    scan, the same lane-index category already permitted for the AVX2 masks.
    
    Not measured on x86-64. feed_avx2 now reaches its scans through the trait
    rather than calling them directly, which may cost inlining across the
    target_feature boundary. Host A must re-run before this is trusted there.

