[33mcommit 35f1aeceae8df7007bb13843da711c7697b31265[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 14:46:49 2026 -0600

    fix: interleave and rotate the measurement order
    
    Cores were measured in blocks -- nine reps of one, then nine of the next --
    which let run position bias a core. Inserting a row above baud cost it 16%
    while every other core moved under 6%, and that was enough to flip the
    baud-vs-ghostty verdict between runs.
    
    multibench now runs rep-major and rotates the starting core by the rep number,
    so no core holds a fixed position. Between-run drift in the streaming median
    fell from 20.1% to 8.9% for baud and from 5.8% to 0.2% for ghostty.
    
    With that fixed, two things can be said:
    
    - baud vs alacritty is a real result. No range overlaps in either run: 1.34x
      and 1.37x on streaming, 1.68x and 1.83x on TUI.
    - baud vs ghostty is not. Ranges overlap on both payloads in both runs. The
      sign is consistent on streaming -- ghostty's median exceeded baud's in 5 of
      5 runs, including two where baud went first, p = 0.031 -- but on TUI the
      direction flips between runs. Recorded as suggestive, not settled.
    
    baud is scalar on this host and ghostty is not, so the fair statement is that
    baud is within ~10% of a vectorised parser while running no SIMD, and that is
    consistent with parser speed not being the binding constraint.
    
    baud is still the noisiest core by 3x, within runs as well as between them,
    and that is unexplained. Storage::boxed() rebuilding a ~1.7 MB zeroed
    allocation each rep is the obvious suspect and has not been tested.

