[33mcommit e5f50701fa0b4a46378f7e214b5fdcdb28b3c5f3[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 14:36:23 2026 -0600

    fix: retract the macOS baud-vs-ghostty result, and add kitty
    
    The previous commit published a baud-vs-ghostty verdict for Host B. It does
    not hold. Three runs of identical baud code gave 195.9, 194.3 and 163.1 MB/s
    on streaming -- a 17% spread, wider than almost every difference these tables
    are used to argue about -- and the verdict flipped with it, from "ranges
    overlap, no result" to "ghostty clearly ahead". The only change between those
    runs was a row added above baud, i.e. run order. Recorded rather than deleted:
    this project's notes already list orderings that flipped between runs and were
    reported as findings.
    
    The `ceiling (discard, no parse)` row I added to compare across harnesses is
    not a ceiling, and the same run proves it: baud TUI (249.0) and ghostty TUI
    (272.0) both exceed it. through_pty times a producer-consumer pair, and a sink
    that discards makes the reader block in read(2) continuously, paying more
    scheduler round trips per byte than a sink that does work. It is a different
    operating point, not a bound -- so harness cost cannot be subtracted as if it
    were additive, which was the only way the kitty numbers could have been made
    comparable to the Rust ones. The row stays, relabelled, because the fact that
    it is not a bound is itself worth showing.
    
    kitty is measured for the first time. Its parser is a CPython object -- Parser
    is PyObject_HEAD and alloc_vt_parser resolves its target through kitty's
    global window registry -- so it is driven through the same buffer-protocol
    cycle its own test suite uses, in Python, on payload bytes dumped by
    multibench so the two harnesses cannot drift.

