[33mcommit 5bc237ac074aa48d5060b1c08cb2ca9d8ae7e561[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 14:27:49 2026 -0600

    feat: measure ghostty, and fix how multibench reports
    
    ghostty is measured against baud for the first time. bench-others/README.md
    said ghostty-bench was blocked on ghostty's build-time-generated
    unicode_tables; that is obsolete. libghostty-vt is a shipped artifact now,
    built by ghostty's own build.zig which runs that codegen itself, so ghostty
    links into multibench from Rust over its C ABI and measures in the same
    harness, through the same PTY, against the same payloads as every other core.
    The separate Zig harness was the confound MEASUREMENTS.md warns about.
    
    Two harness defects fixed, both of which mattered more than the numbers:
    
    - best() returned the maximum of N runs and printed no range. That is an
      upward-biased estimator that also hides variance, and variance here is
      large: two runs of identical baud code differed by 9.3%. It is stat() now,
      median-of-9 with [min-max], matching what baud-bench has always reported.
    - multibench pinned alacritty by relative path to a sibling checkout, so it
      did not run on any machine that had not cloned alacritty to exactly the
      right place. It uses alacritty_terminal 0.26 from crates.io, which is also
      a version a reader can quote.
    
    On macOS/aarch64 the result is that baud and ghostty are indistinguishable:
    every range overlaps, on both payloads, in both ghostty builds, with the
    medians moving in opposite directions between builds. baud vs alacritty is a
    real result at 1.33-1.36x streaming and 1.69-1.95x TUI.
    
    MEASUREMENTS.md now stamps host, harness, statistic and payload generator on
    every table. The absence of those stamps is how two tables measuring the same
    core at 318 and 204 MB/s sat three lines apart: different harness, different
    statistic, and — never previously written down — different payloads, since
    payload.rs addresses an arbitrary column and emits ECH/IL/ED while
    multibench's TUI payload does neither.

