[33mcommit 89181181b65653c6fd89874bbbd19213e6eeb95b[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 12:36:15 2026 -0600

    feat: build and run baud-bench on macOS and aarch64
    
    Three things stopped the harness compiling off Linux/x86, and none of them
    were the PTY: is_x86_feature_detected! is an x86-only macro, Term::feed_avx2
    is #[cfg(target_arch = "x86_64")] so the branch naming it could not compile,
    and F_SETPIPE_SZ does not exist on Darwin. openpty already worked — build.rs
    links libutil on both platforms.
    
    Each is now a cfg with the consequence written down rather than hidden:
    
    - avx2_available() is a cfg, not a runtime probe. Off x86 the answer is not
      "this CPU lacks AVX2" but "there is no such entry point", and there is no
      NEON scanner yet, so aarch64 measures the portable path and only that.
    - feed_dispatch() keeps one signature across both, so the call site does not
      grow a cfg.
    - The 1 MiB pipe buffer is Linux-only. It is why the pipe transport reaches
      8155 MB/s in README.md, and Darwin's ~64 KiB default makes that row a
      different quantity rather than a slower version of the same one.
    
    So the harness now prints a warning off Linux. CLAUDE.md's first listed
    measurement failure is comparing across machines, and the tty layer is the
    thing being measured: Darwin has no n_tty, hence no N_TTY_BUF_SIZE cap on
    read(2). Nothing measured here is comparable to MEASUREMENTS.md.
    
    baud-bench is a plain default member again, and devenv.nix no longer branches
    the check scope on the host OS.
    
    Carries bench_t's #[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)] — its eight parameters
    are the eight things that vary between rows of the table it prints.

