[33mcommit 42bc22adf67e0cb96e97478614958e925abccec0[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 12:36:15 2026 -0600

    fix: clear every clippy lint in the library crates
    
    Clippy aborts at the first crate that fails, so baud-core and the test
    targets had never been linted at all until baud-domain compiled clean. What
    looked like 11 errors surfaced in three waves.
    
    Applied as suggested where free: div_ceil, two #[must_use] messages, a mixed
    inner/outer attribute, two then -> then_some, an elided lifetime, four
    is_multiple_of, one !is_empty.
    
    Renamed rather than silenced in one place. ByteLen::add called
    saturating_add in its body, so a method named `add` saturated silently —
    exactly the invisible behaviour this crate exists to prevent. It is
    ByteLen::saturating_add now, with its one call site updated.
    
    Allowed with a written reason in four places, because clippy's advice
    contradicts a decision the code already made:
    
    - RowCount::add and Slot::as_mut. An Add/AsMut impl makes the quantity
      invisible at the call site, and a visible quantity is the point.
    - Grid::put_ascii_run and Slot::write. needless_range_loop wants iterator
      chains on the two hottest loops in the crate. put_ascii_run's own doc
      records that going per-cell cost ~25% of ingest, and CLAUDE.md is explicit
      that this needs a benchmark rather than a lint fix.
    
    The allows are per-item rather than crate-level, so the next occurrence of
    the same lint somewhere that is not hot still fails.

