[33mcommit f5399fb65a8f0b10dc97c9d017e0742124bff17e[m
Author: Ido Samuelson <i-am-logger@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 12:22:55 2026 -0600

    feat: port the baud ingest core into the scaffold
    
    Replaces the rust-template placeholder crate with the real project: a
    no_std, allocation-free terminal ingest core (baud-domain, baud-core), its
    PTY benchmark harness (baud-bench), the differential test against alacritty
    (baud-difftest), and the cross-implementation harnesses in bench-others/.
    
    Workspace layout decisions, each with a reason rather than a default:
    
    - baud-difftest and bench-others/ are excluded. They pin alacritty and
      ghostty by path to sibling checkouts that do not exist on a fresh clone,
      so including them would make `cargo check` fail on the checkout rather
      than on the code.
    - baud-bench is a member but not a default member. It uses F_SETPIPE_SZ,
      which is Linux-only, so bare cargo invocations cover the portable crates
      everywhere and devenv adds --workspace on Linux, where CI runs.
    - edition stays 2021, not the scaffold's 2024. Migrating an edition across a
      measured hot path is a change that needs a benchmark, not a flag flip.
    
    devenv.nix read cargoToml.package.*, which does not exist in a virtual
    workspace manifest; it now reads workspace.package with the name pinned to
    the repo. Two gates that were shell scripts in the tarball are now devenv
    tasks, so `devenv test` and CI run them by construction: test:bare builds
    baud-core for x86_64-unknown-none (the actual no_std proof) and test:audit
    runs audit_all.sh.
    
    Cargo.lock is dropped rather than edited; it described the scaffold's
    dependency tree, which no longer exists.

